var __index = {"config":{"lang":["en"],"separator":"[\\s\\-]+","pipeline":["stopWordFilter"],"fields":{"title":{"boost":1000.0},"text":{"boost":1.0},"tags":{"boost":1000000.0}}},"docs":[{"location":"index.html","title":"User Guide","text":"<p>This guide is for people who use AI tools such as ChatGPT, Claude or Gemini on a computer where ScienceSoft AI Endpoint Firewall (called AI Firewall in this guide) is installed and managed by their company. If you are trying ScienceSoft AI Endpoint Firewall in a trial, ask your ScienceSoft representative for the trial guide instead.</p>"},{"location":"index.html#what-sciencesoft-ai-endpoint-firewall-does","title":"What ScienceSoft AI Endpoint Firewall does","text":"<p>When you send a message, a file or a picture to an AI tool, it can contain private data. This can be a name, a card number, a password or a company secret.</p> <p>ScienceSoft AI Endpoint Firewall checks your request on your computer, before it leaves. If it finds private data, it follows the rule your company has set. It can record it, replace it with a placeholder, or stop the request.</p>"},{"location":"index.html#what-stays-private-and-what-is-recorded","title":"What stays private, and what is recorded","text":"<p>Both are true, so we say both.</p> <ul> <li>Stays on your computer: the checking itself. Your messages, files and images are not sent anywhere to be scanned. The private values that were found are not collected.</li> <li>Recorded: if your computer is connected to a workspace, the workspace's administrators can see what was detected: how many values were found and which types. By default the message is also recorded there, with the private values replaced. So they can read what you wrote, but not the personal data found in it. How much is recorded is set by an administrator (the setting is called Data Capture Mode).</li> <li>Whose workspace: normally the workspace belongs to your company. If your computer is connected to a server run by ScienceSoft, as in a trial, ScienceSoft can see what that workspace records.</li> <li>Not connected to a workspace: nothing is sent.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"index.html#what-is-in-this-guide","title":"What is in this guide","text":"Page Read it to learn Install How ScienceSoft AI Endpoint Firewall gets onto your computer, and how to check that it works. Using the app What each screen in the app shows, and what you can change. Check status What each protection status means, and what to do. What is protected What is protected, what happens when private data is found, and what is not covered. Troubleshooting What to do when something does not work. FAQ Short answers to common questions, including privacy. Uninstall How to remove ScienceSoft AI Endpoint Firewall, and what happens to your data."},{"location":"index.html#good-to-know-first","title":"Good to know first","text":"<ul> <li>You do not need to do anything every day. ScienceSoft AI Endpoint Firewall works in the background.</li> <li>Your company sets the rules. Many settings are locked. If a rule gets in your way, ask your IT team.</li> <li>It protects only what it covers. An AI tool that is not named on the coverage page is not protected.</li> <li>It must be running. If the app is stopped, protection may stop too, depending on how it was installed. See the coverage page.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"index.html#need-help","title":"Need help?","text":"<p>Open Help in the app. It has troubleshooting steps and a Contact us form. You can also ask your IT team.</p> <p>Using the Admin Portal, or deploying ScienceSoft AI Endpoint Firewall to many computers? See the Admin Guide.</p>"},{"location":"check-status.html","title":"Check status","text":"<p>This page explains how to check if ScienceSoft AI Endpoint Firewall is protecting you, what each status means, and what to do about it.</p>"},{"location":"check-status.html#where-to-see-the-status","title":"Where to see the status","text":"<ul> <li>The status card at the bottom of the menu in the app. It shows the status, who sets the rules (\"Managed by IT\" or \"Local settings\") and the mode.</li> <li>The Dashboard. The first line shows the same status, in capital letters.</li> <li>The i button at the top right of every screen (\"Why this status?\"). It lists the checks behind the status and shows which ones passed.</li> </ul> <p>The i window shows three checks:</p> Check What it means Traffic is steered here Your operating system sends AI-tool traffic to the firewall, so it can be read. Detection engine is at full strength The on-device model that finds sensitive data is running normally. Interception confirmed A test request went through the firewall in the last few minutes. <p>When all three pass, the window says \"Running and protecting: every check passed.\" If a check fails, it is marked and the status above changes.</p> <p></p> <p>Example: the status card when your organisation has paused protection.</p> <p></p>"},{"location":"check-status.html#what-each-status-means","title":"What each status means","text":"<p>The first rows are the good ones. The later rows need attention.</p> Status What it means What to do Protecting this device (or Protection is on, when your IT team manages the computer) AI traffic on this computer is checked before it leaves. Nothing. Protected \u2014 browsers and proxy-aware apps Browsers and apps that follow the system proxy setting are protected. Other programs are not routed through the firewall at the moment. The screen gives a reason. Nothing you can press. If it stays, tell your IT team. Inspecting with limits Traffic is checked, but one part of detection is not available on this computer (for example the name model, or reading text in images). The screen says which part and what it means. Restart the computer or install a pending update. If it stays, contact your IT team. Mode not confirmed Traffic is checked, but the app could not confirm that the engine is using the mode that was chosen (Record only, Replace or Block). Treat the mode as not applied. See If you see \"Mode not confirmed\" below. Wait a minute, then restart the app. If it stays, tell your IT team. Checking protection The app is checking. This is normal for a few seconds after start-up. Wait. If it stays for more than a few minutes, the app may be out of date and unable to run the check. It should update itself. If not, contact your IT team. Turning on protection / Turning off protection A change is in progress. Wait. Not inspecting traffic The protection is running, but AI traffic is not reaching it. Nothing is being checked. Open Device &amp; coverage and look at Certificate trust and System proxy to see which part is missing. Tell your IT team. Protection could not start The app could not start protection. The message gives the cause and the fix. Follow the message. If it fails again, contact your IT team. Protection has stopped Protection stopped working. The app is trying to restart it. See When protection has stopped. Wait a moment. If it does not come back, contact your IT team. Protection is paused by your organisation Your IT team paused protection. Only they can turn it back on. See When protection is paused. Contact your IT team if you did not expect this. Some settings remain Protection is off, but some settings could not be removed. AI tools on this computer may not connect. Restart the app. If it stays, contact your IT team. Another ScienceSoft AI Endpoint Firewall app is protecting this computer A second ScienceSoft AI Endpoint Firewall app on this computer checks traffic from desktop apps. Browsers may still be protected by this app. Uninstall one of the two apps, or reinstall this one. Not set up yet This computer is not enrolled. Your IT team needs to finish the setup. Contact your IT team."},{"location":"check-status.html#if-you-see-mode-not-confirmed","title":"If you see \"Mode not confirmed\"","text":"<p>Your AI traffic is still being checked. What is not certain is which mode is in use. Until the status goes back to normal, treat the chosen mode (for example Replace) as not applied: private data might not be replaced or blocked.</p> <p>The app asks the engine to confirm the mode, and tries three times. The status appears when the engine does not answer in time. Common reasons:</p> <ul> <li>The engine restarted, and has not confirmed the mode again yet.</li> <li>The engine was not ready when the mode was set (for example just after start-up or a change of rules).</li> <li>A setting pushed by your IT team did not reach the engine.</li> </ul> <p>What to do:</p> <ol> <li>Wait a minute. When the app sees that the engine is answering again, it sends the mode again by itself.</li> <li>Restart the app.</li> <li>If the status stays, tell your IT team. Copy the details from Help (see How to report a problem).</li> </ol>"},{"location":"check-status.html#when-protection-has-stopped","title":"When protection has stopped","text":"<p>What happens to your AI requests depends on how ScienceSoft AI Endpoint Firewall was installed.</p> <ul> <li>Windows with the system guard, and macOS installed from the .pkg: AI services are blocked on this computer until protection restarts. Nothing is sent without being checked.</li> <li>Any other installation: AI requests still go out, and personal data in them is not checked or replaced. Do not send private data to AI tools until the status is back to \"Protecting this device\".</li> </ul> <p>In both cases the app is trying to restart the protection by itself.</p>"},{"location":"check-status.html#when-protection-is-paused","title":"When protection is paused","text":"<p>A pause always comes from your organisation. There is no button for you to turn protection back on.</p> <p>The title is the same in both cases: Protection is paused by your organisation. The sentence under it tells them apart:</p> <ul> <li>\"Traffic is still routed through the app, but nothing is being inspected.\" Nothing is checked, but the connection is not broken.</li> <li>\"AI traffic from this device is going out without being inspected.\" Nothing is routed through the app. AI traffic goes out without being checked.</li> </ul> <p>In both cases, do not send private data to AI tools until your IT team turns protection back on.</p>"},{"location":"check-status.html#how-to-report-a-problem","title":"How to report a problem","text":"<ol> <li>Open Help and select Copy details.</li> <li>Select Contact us, describe the problem and paste the details. Personal data in the message is replaced before it is sent, and you see what will be sent.</li> <li>Or send the details to your IT team.</li> </ol>"},{"location":"check-status.html#see-also","title":"See also","text":"<ul> <li>Using the app, for the other messages you may see (reduced detection, audit log upload, update needs attention).</li> <li>What is protected, for what is and is not protected.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"coverage.html","title":"What is protected","text":"<p>This page explains what ScienceSoft AI Endpoint Firewall protects, what it does when it finds private data, and what it does not cover.</p>"},{"location":"coverage.html#what-sciencesoft-ai-endpoint-firewall-looks-for","title":"What ScienceSoft AI Endpoint Firewall looks for","text":"<p>ScienceSoft AI Endpoint Firewall reads your request on your computer, before it goes to the AI tool. It can find 32 kinds of private data. 20 of them are on by default. Examples:</p> <ul> <li>names and contact details</li> <li>ID numbers and bank card numbers</li> <li>passwords and secret keys</li> <li>other personal or confidential details</li> </ul> <p>The Protection screen shows the full list, in four groups: People, Credentials, Financial and Government identity.</p> <p>It also checks:</p> <ul> <li>Files: Word, Excel, PowerPoint, PDF and OpenDocument.</li> <li>Images and screenshots: it reads the text inside the picture.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"coverage.html#what-it-does-when-it-finds-something","title":"What it does when it finds something","text":"<p>There are three choices. Each one is a mode.</p> Mode (top bar) Name on the Protection screen What happens When it fits Monitor Record only The request is sent as you typed it, and a log entry shows what left your computer. The exceptions are machine secrets such as API keys and your organisation's keywords: those are replaced. The first days, to see what is really being sent. This is the default for new teams. Mask Replace (recommended) Private details are swapped for placeholders before sending. They are put back in the answer you read. The AI can still help you. A detection is replaced only when the firewall is sure enough (see below). Everyday use. Protect Block A request is not sent when the firewall is sure enough (see below) that it holds private data. You see what was found. You can edit your prompt and try again. Two cases are replaced and sent instead: a search question that an app sends in a web address, and a request whose only findings are your organisation's keywords. When private data must never leave your computer. <p>How sure is \"sure enough\"? Each finding has a confidence score, and two checks use it differently:</p> <ul> <li>Names, organisations and addresses (found by the language model). A finding below the model's cutoff is dropped: it is not replaced and it is not recorded as a detection. The cutoff is set for each kind of finding. The model ScienceSoft ships is tuned to lean towards catching names, so a name it is only fairly sure about is still replaced. Your IT team can change the cutoff for each kind.</li> <li>Numbers and codes found by patterns (cards, account numbers, ID numbers, e-mail addresses). In Replace and Block a weak match, for example an ordinary word that looks like a bank code, is not replaced and does not block a request. It stays in the record of what was found, and the log on this computer shows it. In Record only every match is recorded.</li> </ul> <p>You cannot change this on your computer. Ask your IT team what is set.</p> <p>Detection of passwords and other credentials is always on. It cannot be switched off. What happens to a credential still depends on the mode: it is blocked in Block mode, replaced in Replace mode, and recorded in Record only mode (except machine secrets, which are replaced).</p> <p>Because Monitor is the default, a new team is not yet replacing most private data. Ask your IT team which mode is set for you.</p>"},{"location":"coverage.html#who-chooses-the-mode","title":"Who chooses the mode","text":"<p>Your company decides. Your IT team sets the rules for all computers. You cannot change the mode or the list of data types in the app. The Protection screen shows them, so you know what protects you, and shows how long ago your rules were last updated. If the computer is not connected to a company, the app uses the default: Record only.</p> <p>What you can change on your own computer:</p> <ul> <li>Company patterns: your own patterns for things only your company would recognise. They stay on your computer.</li> <li>The detection model and the languages for reading text in images (Detection engine screen).</li> <li>Notifications, and whether ScienceSoft AI Endpoint Firewall starts with your computer.</li> <li>Command-line tools: whether Claude Code, Codex or Kimi Code go through the firewall (see below).</li> </ul>"},{"location":"coverage.html#where-it-works","title":"Where it works","text":"Where you use AI Covered? Web browsers: Chrome, Edge, Safari Yes, automatically Firefox Yes, if it is set to \"Use system proxy settings\". On a company-managed Windows computer, your IT team's policy can set this for you Desktop apps built on web technology, such as Claude Desktop Yes, automatically Claude Code Yes, after it is turned on Codex Yes, after it is turned on Kimi Code Yes, after it is turned on <p>You do not need to set anything up for Claude Desktop. It is covered automatically.</p> <p>Command-line tools are off until someone turns them on. These tools do not follow the system proxy setting, so ScienceSoft AI Endpoint Firewall has to change each tool's own settings. It does not do that unless you ask, or your IT team asks. Until then, the tool's requests are not checked.</p> <ul> <li>To turn a tool on yourself, open Device &amp; coverage and select Connect a command-line tool.</li> <li>Your IT team can also turn tools on for the whole company.</li> <li>Turning protection off, or uninstalling, puts the tool's settings back.</li> <li>Start a new session of the tool afterwards. A session that was already running keeps its old settings.</li> <li>Claude Code and Kimi Code cannot both be covered at once.</li> <li>On Device &amp; coverage, the row Command-line tools shows if they are covered.</li> </ul> <p>At the time of writing, ScienceSoft AI Endpoint Firewall covers 38 AI services in the browser, including Claude, ChatGPT, Gemini, Microsoft Copilot, GitHub Copilot, Perplexity and Grok. The list grows with new versions.</p>"},{"location":"coverage.html#what-is-checked-inside-a-request","title":"What is checked inside a request","text":"<ul> <li>What you type, and what your AI tools read back (for example, files that Claude Code reads for you).</li> <li>Images and screenshots (text is read on your computer).</li> <li>Documents: Word, Excel, PowerPoint, PDF and OpenDocument files, and <code>.zip</code> files one level deep. In Replace mode, a cleaned copy is sent instead of the original.</li> <li>Document types: your organisation can also set rules by kind of document (for example NDAs or contracts): allow, record, replace personal data, ask you, or block. If a file is blocked, a notification names the document type.</li> <li>A file that cannot be read (encrypted, damaged, unknown format, or a zip inside a zip) is recorded as not checked, with the reason. By default it is still sent. Your IT team can choose to refuse such files instead.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"coverage.html#what-is-not-covered","title":"What is NOT covered","text":"<p>We state this clearly, so you never find out by accident.</p> <ul> <li>Command-line tools that are not turned on, and all other command-line tools, including <code>curl</code> and your own scripts. They send requests straight to AI services.</li> <li>Aider, Visual Studio Code, Cursor and Continue. ScienceSoft AI Endpoint Firewall does not set these up. Cursor's built-in browser side is covered like any web-based app.</li> <li>JetBrains AI Assistant. Not covered today.</li> <li>Documents attached in Microsoft 365 Copilot. They are stored in your company's OneDrive and read there, so they never pass through the firewall. They are not checked. Each upload is recorded as \"An attachment's contents were not inspected\". Images attached in Copilot are checked. Your IT team can choose to block these uploads.</li> <li>Apps that pin their own certificate. ScienceSoft AI Endpoint Firewall cannot read their traffic, and the connection fails instead of being skipped.</li> <li>Some VPN clients. A VPN can take over the network setting. When that happens, the agent records it and your IT team can see it.</li> <li>Anything encrypted by the app itself before it reaches the network.</li> <li>Any AI service not named in the list. There is no catch-all.</li> </ul> <p>An AI tool that is not named in this guide is not protected.</p>"},{"location":"coverage.html#known-side-effect-microsoft-teams-and-outlook","title":"Known side effect: Microsoft Teams and Outlook","text":"<p>To cover Microsoft 365 Copilot, ScienceSoft AI Endpoint Firewall also inspects <code>graph.microsoft.com</code>. The Teams, Outlook and Office desktop apps pin their certificate for this address. On a computer that has them, they may fail to sign in or send messages while protection is on. If that happens, tell your IT team. Turning protection off restores them.</p>"},{"location":"coverage.html#when-sciencesoft-ai-endpoint-firewall-is-not-running","title":"When ScienceSoft AI Endpoint Firewall is not running","text":"<p>The app can be closed, for example with Quit in the tray menu. When it is closed, protection stops.</p> <ul> <li>Windows with the system guard, and macOS installed from the .pkg: AI services it covers become unavailable instead of being reached without protection. Your IT team can grant an exception. On macOS this holds for the network connections the Mac had at install time. A connection added later, such as a USB adapter, can reach AI services directly.</li> <li>macOS installed from the .dmg: there is no system guard. If the app is closed, AI services are reached directly and your requests are not protected.</li> </ul> <p>The app says so on its own screen, and the device reports it to your IT team.</p>"},{"location":"coverage.html#languages-and-limits","title":"Languages and limits","text":"<ul> <li>Languages: the detection model, the ScienceSoft PII Encoder, supports 7 languages: English, Russian, Arabic, Spanish, German, Italian and French. Images in these languages are read on your computer.</li> <li>Images: the app reads one language group at a time (Latin, Cyrillic or Arabic letters). A picture in a different language may be read badly, without a warning. Reading text from blurry or low-quality images is less accurate.</li> <li>Arabic person names are not found yet.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"coverage.html#what-is-recorded","title":"What is recorded","text":"<p>Protection happens on your computer. The private values that were found are not collected.</p> <p>If your computer is connected to a workspace, the workspace's administrators can see what was detected: how many values were found and which types. By default the message is also recorded there, with the private values replaced. So they can read what you wrote, but not the personal data found in it.</p> <p>How much is recorded is set by an administrator. The setting is called Data Capture Mode (<code>TraceContent</code>). It has three levels:</p> Level What the workspace keeps Full (the default) The conversation, with the private values replaced. Masked A short excerpt of the text with the private values replaced, with the detection record. Metadata Only what was detected. No message text. <p>Whose workspace it is matters. Normally it belongs to your company. If your computer is connected to a server run by ScienceSoft, as in a trial, ScienceSoft can see what that workspace records. ScienceSoft can disconnect a computer on request.</p> <p>A computer that is not connected to a workspace sends nothing.</p> <p>What the AI service receives depends on the mode (see the table above).</p>"},{"location":"faq.html","title":"FAQ","text":"<p>Short answers to the questions people ask most. For steps to fix a problem, see Troubleshooting.</p>"},{"location":"faq.html#privacy","title":"Privacy","text":""},{"location":"faq.html#can-my-manager-read-my-chats","title":"Can my manager read my chats?","text":"<p>This tool has two jobs: it keeps your company's confidential data out of the prompts that your apps send to AI services, and it collects as little as possible about you while doing that.</p> <p>Plainly:</p> <ul> <li>The workspace's administrators can see what was detected: how many values were found, and which types. They cannot see the values themselves.</li> <li>If your computer is connected to a workspace, the message is also recorded there, by default, with those values replaced. So they can read what you wrote, but not the personal data that was found in it. An administrator can choose to keep less (a short excerpt, or no message text at all). The setting is called Data Capture Mode.</li> <li>What the AI service receives depends on your company's setting. In Replace mode, values that the firewall is sure enough about are replaced before sending. In Record only mode, your message is sent as typed, apart from your organisation's keywords and machine secrets such as API keys.</li> <li>A computer that is not connected to a workspace sends nothing.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"faq.html#is-my-data-sent-anywhere-to-be-scanned","title":"Is my data sent anywhere to be scanned?","text":"<p>No. Your messages, files and images are checked on your computer. The detection model runs on your computer too.</p>"},{"location":"faq.html#does-sciencesoft-ai-endpoint-firewall-read-all-my-internet-traffic","title":"Does ScienceSoft AI Endpoint Firewall read all my internet traffic?","text":"<p>No. Only traffic to AI services is sent through it. Every other site goes straight out, untouched and unread.</p>"},{"location":"faq.html#does-sciencesoft-see-my-data","title":"Does ScienceSoft see my data?","text":"<p>Only if your computer is connected to a server run by ScienceSoft, as in a trial. Then ScienceSoft can see what that workspace records. ScienceSoft can disconnect a computer from its server on request.</p> <p>In a normal installation, the workspace belongs to your company, and ScienceSoft does not see it.</p>"},{"location":"faq.html#why-does-it-install-a-certificate-is-that-safe","title":"Why does it install a certificate? Is that safe?","text":"<p>AI traffic is encrypted. To check a request before it leaves, your computer has to trust ScienceSoft AI Endpoint Firewall. The certificate does that.</p> <ul> <li>It is made on your computer.</li> <li>It works on this computer only.</li> <li>Its private key never leaves your computer.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"faq.html#day-to-day","title":"Day to day","text":""},{"location":"faq.html#do-i-need-to-do-anything-every-day","title":"Do I need to do anything every day?","text":"<p>No. ScienceSoft AI Endpoint Firewall works in the background. Open the app when you want to see what it did, or when something looks wrong.</p>"},{"location":"faq.html#can-i-turn-protection-off","title":"Can I turn protection off?","text":"<p>No. There is no on/off switch in the app. Your company decides when protection is on.</p> <p>You can close the app from the tray menu, but then nothing is checked. Depending on how ScienceSoft AI Endpoint Firewall was installed, AI services are then either blocked or reached without being checked (see What is protected). Your IT team can see that the device has stopped reporting.</p>"},{"location":"faq.html#why-did-a-window-ask-me-to-confirm","title":"Why did a window ask me to confirm?","text":"<p>Your organisation can ask you to confirm before you use an AI service or upload a file. Choose Continue to go ahead for up to 8 hours without being asked again for that service, or Cancel. Your choice, and a reason if you add one, is recorded for your organisation. If you do not answer in time, the request is cancelled and you can send it again.</p>"},{"location":"faq.html#does-it-slow-down-my-ai-tools","title":"Does it slow down my AI tools?","text":"<p>A little. The Dashboard shows the median scan time measured on your own computer. Large screenshots take longer, because the text inside them is read first.</p>"},{"location":"faq.html#why-was-a-name-replaced-when-it-was-my-own","title":"Why was a name replaced when it was my own?","text":"<p>The detection model does not know which names are safe in your work. Tell your IT team, and they can add an exception for your team. You cannot add one yourself.</p>"},{"location":"faq.html#what-happens-if-sciencesoft-ai-endpoint-firewall-stops-working","title":"What happens if ScienceSoft AI Endpoint Firewall stops working?","text":"<p>It says so, and does not pretend otherwise. The status changes (see Check status), and Device &amp; coverage stops showing the affected traffic as covered.</p> <p>What happens to your AI requests depends on how ScienceSoft AI Endpoint Firewall was installed:</p> <ul> <li>On Windows with the system guard, and on macOS installed from the .pkg, AI services are blocked until protection is back.</li> <li>In other installations, requests go out without being checked. Do not send private data until the status is back to \"Protecting this device\".</li> </ul>"},{"location":"faq.html#what-is-protected","title":"What is protected","text":""},{"location":"faq.html#does-it-work-with-claude-chatgpt-and-gemini","title":"Does it work with Claude, ChatGPT and Gemini?","text":"<p>Yes, in your browser and in desktop apps built on web technology (for example Claude Desktop). Claude Code, Codex and Kimi Code are covered too, after they are turned on (see What is protected). The full list, and what is not covered, is on the same page.</p>"},{"location":"faq.html#what-does-it-do-with-files-and-images","title":"What does it do with files and images?","text":"<p>Word, Excel, PowerPoint, PDF and OpenDocument files are opened on your computer and their text is checked. Your organisation can also set rules by kind of document, such as NDAs or contracts. For images and screenshots, the text inside the picture is read on your computer. In Replace mode, a cleaned copy of a file is sent instead of the original.</p> <p>A file that cannot be read (for example an encrypted file) is recorded as not checked. By default it is still sent. Your IT team can choose to refuse such files instead.</p>"},{"location":"faq.html#which-languages-does-it-support","title":"Which languages does it support?","text":"<p>The detection model, the ScienceSoft PII Encoder, supports 7 languages: English, Russian, Arabic, Spanish, German, Italian and French. Arabic person names are not detected yet (see What is protected).</p>"},{"location":"faq.html#what-is-not-protected","title":"What is not protected?","text":"<p>An AI tool that is not named on What is protected is not protected. Examples: other command-line tools, scripts, and the JetBrains AI Assistant.</p>"},{"location":"faq.html#updates","title":"Updates","text":""},{"location":"faq.html#does-the-app-update-itself","title":"Does the app update itself?","text":"<p>Yes, or your IT team delivers updates, if they manage them. Every update must be signed by ScienceSoft and newer than the version you have, and the app never asks you for a password. When an update is ready, select Restart now. The restart takes a few seconds, and AI traffic pauses briefly.</p>"},{"location":"install.html","title":"Install","text":"<p>This page explains how ScienceSoft AI Endpoint Firewall gets onto your computer and how to check that it works.</p>"},{"location":"install.html#what-you-need","title":"What you need","text":"<ul> <li>A Windows or macOS computer. There is no Linux version.</li> <li>Permission to approve a security certificate on this computer. On Windows this needs an administrator account. On macOS you need your login password.</li> <li>An internet connection.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"install.html#two-ways-to-install","title":"Two ways to install","text":"<p>Your company chooses one of these.</p> Way 1: You install it Way 2: Your IT team installs it Who does the work You Your IT team, on many computers at once What you receive An installer file from your IT team (<code>.exe</code> on Windows, <code>.dmg</code> on macOS) Nothing. The app just appears. What you do Follow the steps below Approve the certificate if asked, then check that it works (Steps 3 and 4)"},{"location":"install.html#way-1-install-it-yourself","title":"Way 1: Install it yourself","text":""},{"location":"install.html#step-1-run-the-installer","title":"Step 1. Run the installer","text":"<ol> <li>Open the installer file you received.</li> <li>Follow the on-screen steps and finish the installation.</li> <li>Open AI Firewall.</li> </ol>"},{"location":"install.html#step-2-connect-to-your-company","title":"Step 2. Connect to your company","text":"<p>The enrollment code is the key that joins this computer to your company's workspace. You do not type it in the app. It is given to the installer as an argument when the installer is run. Your IT team does this: they either run the installer for you, or give you the command to run. The switches are described in Roll out the agent.</p> <p>Treat the enrollment code like a password. Do not paste it into chats, tickets or AI tools.</p> <p>Trial only</p> <p>In a trial, the app asks for a server address and an enrollment code from your welcome email. Your ScienceSoft representative can send you the trial guide.</p>"},{"location":"install.html#step-3-approve-the-certificate","title":"Step 3. Approve the certificate","text":"<p>AI traffic is encrypted. To read a request before it leaves your computer, ScienceSoft AI Endpoint Firewall needs your computer to trust it. It does this with a certificate.</p> <ol> <li>A prompt appears. On Windows it is a security prompt. On macOS it asks for your login password.</li> <li>Read it. The certificate is issued to AI Firewall Local CA.</li> <li>Approve it.</li> </ol> <p>About this certificate:</p> <ul> <li>It is made on your computer and works on this computer only.</li> <li>Its private key never leaves your computer.</li> <li>If you decline, ScienceSoft AI Endpoint Firewall cannot read encrypted traffic. It will tell you this. It will not pretend that you are protected.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"install.html#step-4-check-that-it-works","title":"Step 4. Check that it works","text":"<ol> <li>Open Device &amp; coverage in the app.</li> <li>Find Certificate trust. It must say Yes \u2014 installed.</li> </ol> <p>If it says anything else, see If something goes wrong below.</p>"},{"location":"install.html#way-2-your-it-team-installs-it","title":"Way 2: Your IT team installs it","text":"<p>Your IT team installs ScienceSoft AI Endpoint Firewall for the whole company. You do not need to download or set up anything.</p> <p>You may still see the certificate prompt described in Step 3. Approve it. Then check that it works with Step 4.</p> <p>Your IT team can find the deployment details in Roll out the agent.</p>"},{"location":"install.html#what-your-company-can-see","title":"What your company can see","text":"<p>ScienceSoft AI Endpoint Firewall works on your computer. The text you type, your files and your images are checked on your computer. They are not sent anywhere to be scanned.</p> <p>If your computer is connected to a workspace, the workspace's administrators can see what was detected (how many values and which types) and what was done about it. By default the message is also recorded there, with the private values already replaced. So they can read what you wrote, but not the personal data found in it. An administrator can choose to keep less (the setting is called Data Capture Mode).</p> <p>The private values themselves are not collected. A computer that is not connected to a workspace sends nothing.</p>"},{"location":"install.html#if-something-goes-wrong","title":"If something goes wrong","text":"What you see What to do Certificate trust is not Yes \u2014 installed You may have declined the prompt. A banner at the top of the app offers Install certificate. Select it, follow the short steps and approve the system prompt this time. The app says the code was not accepted Check that you copied the whole code. If it still fails, ask your IT team for a new one. The app says it cannot reach the server Check your internet connection. A company network or VPN may block the request. Ask your IT team. <p>If ScienceSoft AI Endpoint Firewall is closed or stopped, AI services are either unavailable or reached without protection, depending on how it was installed. See When ScienceSoft AI Endpoint Firewall is not running. Start the app again.</p> <p>For more problems, see Troubleshooting.</p>"},{"location":"management.html","title":"Using the app","text":"<p>This page explains the ScienceSoft AI Endpoint Firewall app: what each screen shows and what you can do on it.</p> <p>ScienceSoft AI Endpoint Firewall protects you in the background. You do not need to open the app for it to work. Open it when you want to see what it did, or when something looks wrong.</p>"},{"location":"management.html#the-app-at-a-glance","title":"The app at a glance","text":"<p>The app has a menu on the left. It has three groups.</p> Group Screen What it is for Monitor Dashboard Today's summary: is protection running, and what did it do? Monitor Activity Every request the firewall saw, and what it did with each one. Monitor Notifications Every notice the firewall raised, including ones you missed: what was found, why, and the request behind it. The latest 500 are kept. Configure Protection What the firewall looks for, and what happens when it finds something. Configure AI tools protection The skills and MCP servers installed on this computer. Configure Detection engine The on-device model that finds sensitive data. This device Device &amp; coverage Connection to your company, and which AI traffic is covered. This device Updates The installed version and how updates arrive. This device Help Troubleshooting, common questions and who to ask. <p>At the bottom of the menu, a status card shows if protection is on, for example \"Protection is on\". It also shows who sets the rules (\"Managed by IT\" or \"Local settings\") and the mode. Below it you see the app version and a button to switch between light and dark theme. The button at the top of the menu folds it to icons.</p> <p>There is no on/off switch in the app. Your company decides when protection is on. You can close the app from the tray menu (Quit), but then protection stops. See When ScienceSoft AI Endpoint Firewall is not running.</p> <p>The i button at the top of every screen (\"Why this status?\") shows the checks behind the current status. For the meaning of each status, see Check status.</p> <p></p> <p>In this example the organisation has paused protection, so the status card does not say \"Protection is on\". See Check status.</p>"},{"location":"management.html#who-is-in-charge-you-or-your-it-team","title":"Who is in charge: you or your IT team","text":"<p>Your IT team sets the rules. You cannot change the mode or the list of data types in the app. You can see them, so you know what protects you. Where a rule is set by your IT team, the screen says \"Set by your IT team. To change it, contact them.\"</p> <p>Things you can change on your own computer:</p> <ul> <li>Company patterns (Protection screen).</li> <li>Detection model and the languages for reading text in images (Detection engine screen).</li> <li>Notifications, Start with this laptop, and saving, restoring or resetting your settings (Device &amp; coverage screen).</li> <li>Command-line tools: which tools go through the firewall (Device &amp; coverage screen).</li> </ul> <p>Restoring or resetting your settings never changes the rules set by your IT team.</p>"},{"location":"management.html#dashboard","title":"Dashboard","text":"<p>Shows today's picture:</p> <ul> <li>A headline, for example \"No AI traffic was inspected on this laptop today.\"</li> <li>A bar showing how requests were handled, and tiles such as most common find, busiest AI tool and number of AI tools inspected.</li> <li>Traffic by AI tool: which AI tools were used today.</li> <li>What was protected: what kinds of data were found. \"Adjust\" opens the Protection screen.</li> </ul> <p>Two buttons take you on: Review today's activity and See what is protected.</p> <p>If the lists are empty, nothing has been scanned yet. Requests to AI services will appear as you use them.</p>"},{"location":"management.html#activity","title":"Activity","text":"<p>Everything stays on your computer. There are three tabs.</p> <ul> <li>Apps: which apps talked to which AI services. Open a row to see details.</li> <li>Requests: every request, newest first, with time, size and scan time. Open a row to see what was found and what was done.</li> <li>Conversations: the same traffic shown as chats. You can search messages, tools and chats, and filter by who sent the message or by tool.</li> </ul> <p>If the list is empty, the screen tells you why. For example, protection may be paused, or AI traffic is not reaching the firewall yet. In that case open Device &amp; coverage to see which part is missing.</p>"},{"location":"management.html#protection","title":"Protection","text":"<p>This screen shows what is protecting you. The mode and the data types are read-only. You can look, but not change them.</p> <p>When sensitive data is found in a request. Three modes:</p> Name What happens Record only The request is sent as typed, apart from machine secrets and your organisation's keywords, which are replaced. You get a log entry so you know what left your computer. Replace Details are swapped for placeholders, then put back in the answer you read. Block The request is not sent. You see what was found and can edit the prompt and try again. Two cases are replaced and sent instead: a search question that an app sends in a web address, and a request whose only findings are your organisation's keywords. <p>Details of what each mode does, including the exceptions for machine secrets, are on the page What is protected.</p> <p>What the firewall looks for. 32 types of data in four groups: People, Credentials, Financial and Government identity. The heading shows how many are on, for example \"10 of 32 types active\". Each type shows how many were found today. A type marked \"Not detected yet\" cannot be found by the firewall at the moment. Credential detection is always on.</p> <p>A type that is switched off is not replaced, but the firewall still records that it saw it, so your IT team can spot a gap.</p> <p>AI services this applies to. The AI services that are inspected. A service that is not on the list is not inspected.</p> <p>Things only your company would recognise. Patterns specific to your company, for example an internal project code. You can add your own with Add a company pattern, and remove them again. They stay on your computer. Patterns pushed by your IT team are separate and read-only.</p> <p>Never replace these. A list of terms that are never replaced. It is set by your IT team and you cannot change it. If something is replaced that should not be, tell your IT team.</p>"},{"location":"management.html#notifications","title":"Notifications","text":"<p>The Notifications screen keeps every notice the firewall raised, including the ones you missed. Each entry has a label (Record only, Replaced, Credential or Held), what was found, why, and the request behind it. It shows the kinds of data, never the data. The list survives a restart and keeps the latest 500. Selecting a system notification opens this screen.</p>"},{"location":"management.html#when-your-organisation-asks-you-to-confirm","title":"When your organisation asks you to confirm","text":"<p>Your organisation can ask you to confirm before you use an AI service or upload a file. A window asks \"Continue to &lt;service&gt;?\" or \"Upload this document to &lt;service&gt;?\". Choose Continue to go ahead; you are not asked again for that service for up to 8 hours. Choose Cancel to stop. You can add a reason (optional). If you do not answer in time, the request is cancelled and you can send it again. Closing the window or pressing Enter counts as Cancel. Your choice and reason are recorded for your organisation, and personal data in the reason is replaced.</p>"},{"location":"management.html#ai-tools-protection","title":"AI tools protection","text":"<p>Lists the skills and MCP servers found in the configuration files on your computer. Nothing is sent while it looks.</p> <ul> <li>A skill is a set of instructions the AI model follows. An MCP server is a program the AI tool can call.</li> <li>Scan now looks again.</li> <li>For each item you see its name, type (MCP server or skill), the tool it belongs to, where it is configured, and a risk level (high, medium, low) with the reason.</li> <li>\"Could not be read\" means a configuration file could not be parsed.</li> </ul> <p>This is a list, not an approval. Nobody has reviewed these items, and an MCP server that opens its own connection is not inspected by the firewall. If your organisation does not allow an MCP server or skill, the app removes it from Claude Code or Codex and shows a notice at the top of the window. Uninstalling the app puts it back.</p>"},{"location":"management.html#detection-engine","title":"Detection engine","text":"<p>Shows how the firewall finds data. Two things run on every request: pattern matching (always on) and a language model that finds names, addresses and similar free text.</p> <ul> <li>Model in use: the models you can choose from, with size, languages and download state. You can choose a model on your own computer.</li> <li>Text inside images: the languages used to read text in pictures. You can add or remove a language.</li> </ul> <p>These choices are local to your computer. Your IT team does not lock them.</p>"},{"location":"management.html#device-coverage","title":"Device &amp; coverage","text":"<p>Shows the state of this computer.</p> <p>This device: status, your company's server, enrolment state, when the app last checked in, device ID, policy version, app version, and whether the certificate is trusted (\"Yes \u2014 installed\"). If the connection to your company's server fails, the screen explains why and offers Try connecting again. Protection keeps running either way. The connection only affects settings and reports.</p> <p>Coverage: which AI traffic passes through the firewall.</p> <ul> <li>Browsers and desktop apps</li> <li>Command-line tools (Claude Code, Codex and Kimi Code)</li> <li>Tools that those tools start</li> </ul> <p>Each one is shown as Covered, Partial or Not covered, with a reason.</p> <p>AI services covered: the services and addresses that are inspected.</p> <p>Install certificate. If the certificate is missing, a banner appears at the top: ScienceSoft AI Endpoint Firewall needs to install a security certificate to inspect AI traffic. Select Install certificate, follow the short steps and approve the system prompt.</p> <p>Connect a command-line tool. Claude Code, Codex and Kimi Code do not follow the system proxy, so they are off until you turn them on. Select this button, choose the tool, and follow the three steps. ScienceSoft AI Endpoint Firewall then changes that tool's own settings so its requests go through the firewall. Turning protection off, or uninstalling, puts the settings back. Start a new session of the tool afterwards. Your IT team can also turn tools on for the whole company.</p> <p>What you are told about. Choose which notifications you get. Some are always on and cannot be turned off, for example \"A request was not sent\", \"A request was blocked by your organisation's policy\" and \"Protection stopped\". \"Details were found and recorded\" (Record only) shows at most once a minute, and turning it off does not stop the recording. Others are \"Your IT team changed the policy\", \"An AI coding tool is not allowed\" and a weekly summary, which is off by default.</p> <p>This laptop. Start with this laptop, save a copy of your settings, restore from a copy, or reset to defaults.</p>"},{"location":"management.html#updates","title":"Updates","text":"<p>Shows the installed version, the newest version and when the app last checked.</p> <ul> <li>Check now looks for an update.</li> <li>Restart now appears when an update is ready. The restart takes a few seconds, and AI traffic pauses briefly.</li> <li>Every update is checked before it is installed: it must be signed by ScienceSoft and newer than the current version. The app never asks you for a password.</li> <li>If your IT team manages updates, the screen says so. You stay protected on the installed version until they deliver the update.</li> <li>If the app cannot update itself on your computer, an orange banner says so and asks you to contact your IT administrator.</li> </ul> <p></p>"},{"location":"management.html#help-and-contact-us","title":"Help and Contact us","text":"<p>Help has troubleshooting steps and common questions, including:</p> <ul> <li>Protection is on but nothing appears in Activity</li> <li>Names are not replaced, but emails and card numbers are</li> <li>Something was replaced that should not have been</li> <li>My command-line tool is not being inspected</li> <li>A setting will not change</li> <li>What happens if the firewall stops working?</li> </ul> <p>It also has Copy details, to send technical details to support, and Contact us.</p> <p>Contact us: choose Ask a question, Report a problem or Request a call, and write a message. The message is checked on your computer first. Personal data is replaced before it is sent, and you see exactly what will be sent before you press Send this. It needs a connection to your company's workspace.</p> <p>Remove this app is on the Help screen on macOS. On Windows, remove it in Settings &gt; Apps. See Uninstall.</p>"},{"location":"management.html#messages-you-may-see","title":"Messages you may see","text":"<p>For the protection status, see Check status. These are other messages.</p> Message What it means Reduced detection on this laptop Protection is on, but part of detection is not running (for example name detection). Restart the computer or install a pending update. If it stays, contact your IT team. Audit log upload Reports to your company's server are failing. Protection keeps working, and records wait on your computer until the problem is fixed. Connections to AI services The firewall could not connect to an AI service. The message says what failed and who can fix it. Downloading protection\u2026 The detection model is downloading. Update needs attention The last update did not finish, or the app cannot update itself on this computer. Select Retry now or Check now, or contact your IT administrator."},{"location":"management.html#trial-only","title":"Trial only","text":"<p>In a trial build, the app asks for a server address and an enrollment code from your welcome email. It shows a notice when the trial is about to end, and says that when it ends the device stops reporting to the workspace, while protection on the computer continues. Your ScienceSoft representative can send you the trial guide.</p>"},{"location":"troubleshooting.html","title":"Troubleshooting","text":"<p>This page helps you when ScienceSoft AI Endpoint Firewall does not behave as you expect. Find your problem in the list, then follow the steps. If a status in the app is not green, read Check status first.</p>"},{"location":"troubleshooting.html#quick-list","title":"Quick list","text":"Problem Go to The status is not \"Protecting this device\" Check status Protection is on, but Activity is empty Problem 1 Names are not replaced, or nothing is replaced at all Problem 2 Something of mine was replaced that should not have been Problem 3 My command-line tool is not checked Problem 4 A setting will not change Problem 5 Certificate trust is not \"Yes \u2014 installed\" Problem 6 The app cannot reach your company's server Problem 7 Teams or Outlook cannot sign in Problem 8 AI tools stopped working when I closed the app Problem 9 AI tools feel slower Problem 10 A window asks me to confirm Problem 11"},{"location":"troubleshooting.html#problem-1-protection-is-on-but-activity-is-empty","title":"Problem 1. Protection is on, but Activity is empty","text":"<p>Activity only fills after you send something to an AI service from this computer.</p> <ol> <li>Use an AI tool (for example, send a short message in a browser).</li> <li>Open Activity again.</li> <li>If it is still empty, open Device &amp; coverage. Look at Browsers and desktop apps.</li> <li>If it is not Covered, the certificate or the system proxy is not in place. Your AI traffic is going out directly, without being checked. Look at Certificate trust and System proxy on the same screen to see which part is missing. Then see Problem 6, or contact your IT team.</li> </ol> <p>The empty Activity screen also gives a reason, for example \"Protection is paused\" or \"Nothing is reaching the firewall yet\".</p> <p>If you only use Claude Code, Codex or Kimi Code, see Problem 4: they are off until they are turned on.</p>"},{"location":"troubleshooting.html#problem-2-names-are-not-replaced-or-nothing-is-replaced","title":"Problem 2. Names are not replaced, or nothing is replaced","text":"<p>There are two different causes. Check the first one first.</p> <ol> <li>The mode. Open Protection. If the mode is Record only, the request is sent as you typed it. Only machine secrets, such as API keys, and your organisation's keywords are replaced. This is the default for new teams. Ask your IT team if this is what they want.</li> <li>The detection model. Emails and card numbers are found by patterns, which always run. Names are found by a language model. If it is still downloading, or could not start, names are not replaced. Open Detection engine to see which model is in use and whether it started. A notice \"Downloading protection\u2026\" means the download is still going.</li> </ol> <p>Arabic names are not detected yet.</p>"},{"location":"troubleshooting.html#problem-3-something-was-replaced-that-should-not-have-been","title":"Problem 3. Something was replaced that should not have been","text":"<p>The detection model does not know which names or words are safe in your work.</p> <p>Tell your IT team the exact word and what it was replaced with. They can add an exception for your team. You cannot add exceptions yourself: the list of words that are never replaced is set by your IT team.</p>"},{"location":"troubleshooting.html#problem-4-my-command-line-tool-is-not-checked","title":"Problem 4. My command-line tool is not checked","text":"<p>Command-line tools do not follow the system proxy setting. They are off until someone turns them on. Until then, their requests are not checked.</p> <ol> <li>Open Device &amp; coverage. Look at Command-line tools. It must say Covered.</li> <li>If it does not, select Connect a command-line tool, choose Claude Code, Codex or Kimi Code, and follow the steps. Your IT team can also turn tools on for the whole company.</li> <li>Start a new session of the tool. A session that was already running keeps its old settings.</li> <li>Claude Code and Kimi Code cannot both be covered at once.</li> <li>Any other command-line tool is not covered. See What is protected.</li> </ol>"},{"location":"troubleshooting.html#problem-5-a-setting-will-not-change","title":"Problem 5. A setting will not change","text":"<p>If your IT team manages your computer, they set the rules. The mode and the list of data types are always read-only in the app. A banner says \"Set by your IT team. To change it, contact them.\" on the screens where your IT team sets a rule. To change a rule, contact them.</p> <p>You can change company patterns, the detection model, image languages, notifications and the start-up setting yourself.</p>"},{"location":"troubleshooting.html#problem-6-certificate-trust-is-not-yes-installed","title":"Problem 6. Certificate trust is not \"Yes \u2014 installed\"","text":"<p>Without the certificate, ScienceSoft AI Endpoint Firewall cannot read encrypted AI traffic.</p> <ol> <li>A banner at the top of the app says ScienceSoft AI Endpoint Firewall needs to install a security certificate. Select Install certificate.</li> <li>Approve the prompt from your operating system. On Windows it needs an administrator account. On macOS it asks for your login password.</li> <li>Open Device &amp; coverage. Certificate trust must now say Yes \u2014 installed.</li> </ol> <p>If you declined the prompt, repeat the steps and approve it this time. More details are on Install.</p>"},{"location":"troubleshooting.html#problem-7-the-app-cannot-reach-your-companys-server","title":"Problem 7. The app cannot reach your company's server","text":"<p>Protection on your computer keeps running. The connection only affects settings and reports.</p> <p>Open Device &amp; coverage. The line Why explains what happened.</p> What the screen says What it means and what to do The server rejected this device's credentials, or does not recognise this device any more The enrolment was probably reset or removed. Contact your IT team: the device needs to be enrolled again. This device can't reach the server Check your network, VPN or firewall. This usually clears on its own. The server is returning errors Usually temporary. Wait, then try again. <p>Select Try connecting again to ask the server to accept this device again.</p>"},{"location":"troubleshooting.html#problem-8-teams-or-outlook-cannot-sign-in","title":"Problem 8. Teams or Outlook cannot sign in","text":"<p>To cover Microsoft 365 Copilot, ScienceSoft AI Endpoint Firewall also checks <code>graph.microsoft.com</code>. The Teams, Outlook and Office desktop apps pin their certificate for that address, so they may fail to sign in or send messages while protection is on. Tell your IT team. Turning protection off restores them, but only your IT team can do that.</p>"},{"location":"troubleshooting.html#problem-9-ai-tools-stopped-working-when-i-closed-the-app","title":"Problem 9. AI tools stopped working when I closed the app","text":"<p>On Windows with the system guard, and on macOS installed from the .pkg, the AI services that ScienceSoft AI Endpoint Firewall covers are blocked while the app is not running. This protects you: requests are not sent unchecked. Start the app again. Your IT team can grant an exception.</p> <p>On macOS installed from the .dmg, nothing is blocked. If the app is closed, AI requests go out without being checked.</p>"},{"location":"troubleshooting.html#problem-10-ai-tools-feel-slower","title":"Problem 10. AI tools feel slower","text":"<p>A little delay is normal. Dashboard shows the median scan time measured on your own computer. Large screenshots take longer, because the text inside them is read first. If the delay is large, contact your IT team.</p>"},{"location":"troubleshooting.html#problem-11-a-window-asks-me-to-confirm","title":"Problem 11. A window asks me to confirm","text":"<p>Your organisation can ask you to confirm before you use a service or upload a file. Choose Continue to go ahead, or Cancel. If you do not answer in time, the message \"The request was cancelled\" appears. Send the request again to be asked again.</p>"},{"location":"troubleshooting.html#still-stuck","title":"Still stuck?","text":"<ol> <li>Open Help and select Copy details.</li> <li>Select Contact us, choose Report a problem, and write what happened. Paste the details.</li> <li>Your message is checked on your computer first. Personal data is replaced before it is sent, and you see what will be sent.</li> <li>Or send the details to your IT team.</li> </ol>"},{"location":"uninstall.html","title":"Uninstall","text":"<p>This page explains how to remove ScienceSoft AI Endpoint Firewall from your computer, and what happens when you do.</p>"},{"location":"uninstall.html#read-this-first","title":"Read this first","text":"<ul> <li>You need administrator access. ScienceSoft AI Endpoint Firewall runs as a protected part of the system. A standard user cannot stop or remove it. This is on purpose: it keeps protection from being switched off by mistake. If you do not have administrator rights, ask your IT team.</li> <li>Your company may not allow it. If your IT team manages your computer, they decide whether ScienceSoft AI Endpoint Firewall stays. Please ask them before you remove it.</li> <li>After removal you are not protected. Requests to AI tools are no longer checked.</li> <li>An update is not an uninstall. Updates keep your data and your connection to your company. Only an uninstall removes them.</li> <li>Quit the app first. Quit ScienceSoft AI Endpoint Firewall from the tray menu before you uninstall, so it puts the settings of your command-line tools back.</li> </ul> <p>ScienceSoft AI Endpoint Firewall is available for Windows and macOS. There is no Linux version.</p>"},{"location":"uninstall.html#windows","title":"Windows","text":"<ol> <li>Open Settings &gt; Apps &gt; Installed apps.</li> <li>Find AI Firewall followed by the version number, for example AI Firewall 2.2.7.</li> <li>Select the three-dot menu, then Uninstall. (Modify is greyed out. You cannot change the installation.)</li> <li>Approve the administrator prompt (UAC).</li> </ol> <p>ScienceSoft AI Endpoint Firewall is installed for every user of the computer, so removing it needs an administrator. If you are signed in as a standard user, Windows asks for the name and password of an administrator. Without them nothing is removed. If you do not have them, ask your IT team.</p> <p></p>"},{"location":"uninstall.html#what-the-uninstaller-removes","title":"What the uninstaller removes","text":"<ul> <li>The app and its folder in <code>C:\\Program Files\\AI Firewall</code>.</li> <li>The protection services and scheduled tasks that restart the app.</li> <li>The system proxy setting that sends AI traffic through the firewall. Your browsers go back to normal. If a browser is already open, restart it.</li> <li>The ScienceSoft AI Endpoint Firewall certificate (\"AI Firewall Local CA\"), from the computer and from every user.</li> <li>The browser settings that ScienceSoft AI Endpoint Firewall added.</li> <li>The settings ScienceSoft AI Endpoint Firewall added for Claude Code and Codex. Your own settings are kept. If Claude Code pointed at the firewall, its original address is put back. If your organisation did not allow some MCP servers or skills and the app took them out of your Claude Code and Codex settings, they are put back too (for the account that runs the uninstaller).</li> <li>Your local data, for every user on the computer: the activity history, saved settings, logs and the connection to your company.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"uninstall.html#silent-uninstall-for-it-teams","title":"Silent uninstall, for IT teams","text":"<p>IT teams can remove ScienceSoft AI Endpoint Firewall without prompts. See Windows and macOS in the Admin Guide.</p>"},{"location":"uninstall.html#macos","title":"macOS","text":"<p>Do not drag the app to the Trash. That removes only the app. The certificate, the proxy setting and the background services stay on the computer.</p> <p>Use the uninstall script that comes with the app. It works for every kind of installation (.dmg and .pkg).</p> <ol> <li>Quit ScienceSoft AI Endpoint Firewall from the menu bar.</li> <li>Open Finder &gt; Applications.</li> <li>Right-click AI Firewall and select Show Package Contents.</li> <li>Open <code>Contents/Resources</code>.</li> <li>Double-click uninstall.command.</li> <li>When asked, type <code>remove</code> and press Enter.</li> <li>Enter your administrator password when asked.</li> </ol> <p>Or, from Terminal:</p> <pre><code>\"/Applications/AI Firewall.app/Contents/Resources/uninstall.command\"\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"uninstall.html#what-the-script-removes","title":"What the script removes","text":"<ul> <li>The app, and its settings, preferences and logs.</li> <li>The background services and the login item.</li> <li>The ScienceSoft AI Endpoint Firewall certificate, from the login keychain and the System keychain.</li> <li>The system proxy setting, on every network connection, if it points at ScienceSoft AI Endpoint Firewall.</li> <li>The settings that ScienceSoft AI Endpoint Firewall added for Claude Code and Codex at the system level.</li> <li>Your local data and the connection to your company.</li> </ul> <p>The script removes data only for the user who runs it. Another user on the same Mac keeps their local data.</p>"},{"location":"uninstall.html#if-something-is-left-behind-on-macos","title":"If something is left behind on macOS","text":"<p>The script says on screen what it did and what it left. Read its last lines.</p> <ul> <li>The proxy setting. The script switches off the automatic proxy setting on every network connection that points at ScienceSoft AI Endpoint Firewall. A connection whose setting it cannot confirm as its own, for example your company's own proxy, it leaves alone, and says so in a line that starts with \"Left the auto-proxy\". If AI sites do not load afterwards, open System Settings &gt; Network, select your connection, open Details &gt; Proxies, and switch off Automatic proxy configuration.</li> <li>Your own Claude Code settings. The script removes the settings that ScienceSoft AI Endpoint Firewall added for Claude Code at the system level. It does not edit your own Claude Code settings file. If Claude Code still points at the firewall, remove the address that points at <code>127.0.0.1</code> from your Claude Code settings.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"uninstall.html#what-happens-to-your-data","title":"What happens to your data","text":"<ul> <li>On your computer: the activity history is deleted, and so is the list of replaced values. Once it is gone, replaced values cannot be turned back into the original text. The connection to your company is deleted too. If you install ScienceSoft AI Endpoint Firewall again, it is a fresh start.</li> <li>At your company: depending on the version, the app may send a short notice to your company's server when you uninstall. The device then shows as removed in the admin console. If no notice is sent, the device simply stops reporting.</li> <li>The device record and the history of what was recorded stay on the server. Your administrator removes the device's access and frees the licence seat. Uninstalling does not delete anything at your company.</li> <li>No notice is sent when your IT team removes the app for many computers at once.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"uninstall.html#after-uninstalling-check","title":"After uninstalling: check","text":"<ul> <li>Your browser opens AI sites normally. If a site does not load, restart the browser.</li> <li>The ScienceSoft AI Endpoint Firewall icon is gone from the menu bar or the tray.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"uninstall.html#things-that-may-stay-behind","title":"Things that may stay behind","text":"<ul> <li>An empty <code>C:\\Program Files\\AI Firewall</code> folder on Windows.</li> <li>Settings for other tools that ScienceSoft AI Endpoint Firewall did not add. ScienceSoft AI Endpoint Firewall does not set up Aider, Continue, Cursor or Visual Studio Code, so there is nothing to remove. ScienceSoft AI Endpoint Firewall does not write to the Claude Code instructions file (<code>CLAUDE.md</code>), so there is no note of ours to remove there.</li> <li>On macOS, other users' local data (see above).</li> </ul>"},{"location":"uninstall.html#common-questions","title":"Common questions","text":"<p>Can I remove it to reinstall a newer version? You do not need to. Install the new version over the old one. Your data and your connection to your company stay.</p> <p>I want to reconnect to a different company workspace. Uninstall, then install again. The connection to the old workspace is removed on purpose, so a new install does not join it again by itself.</p> <p>Does uninstalling delete what my company has recorded about me? No. Those records stay on your company's server.</p>"},{"location":"admin/index.html","title":"Admin Guide","text":"<p>This guide is for the IT or platform team that sets up ScienceSoft AI Endpoint Firewall (called AI Firewall in this guide) in an organisation.</p> <p>ScienceSoft AI Endpoint Firewall has two parts:</p> Part What it is Who uses it The server (control plane) A self-hosted service where your security team sets policy, watches how AI tools are used and reviews audit records. It runs on one Linux server that you own. IT and security administrators The agent A small program on each employee's computer. It checks requests to AI tools and applies the policy. Installed on every managed computer <p>All data stays in your environment. The server is installed and run by you.</p>"},{"location":"admin/index.html#the-parts-of-this-guide","title":"The parts of this guide","text":"Part Read it to learn Set up your self-hosted server What you need, how to install the server from an empty Ubuntu machine, how people sign in, and how to operate, back up and upgrade it. Roll out the agent How to install the agent on many Windows and macOS computers, and how to check that they joined your workspace. Configure your fleet How to use the admin portal: workspaces, enrolment keys, devices and groups, settings, policies, what is recorded, and the audit trail."},{"location":"admin/index.html#where-to-start","title":"Where to start","text":"<ol> <li>Read Server requirements, then follow Install the server.</li> <li>Sign in to the admin portal and give your colleagues access (Sign-in and roles).</li> <li>Roll out the agent to a pilot group of computers (Roll out the agent).</li> <li>Manage the computers in the portal (Configure your fleet).</li> </ol> <p>If you have questions that this guide does not answer, contact your ScienceSoft representative.</p>"},{"location":"admin/agents/index.html","title":"Roll out the agent to your computers","text":"<p>The agent is the small program that runs on each employee's computer. It checks requests to AI tools and applies the policy you set on the server. This part of the guide shows how to install it on many computers at once, and how to check that it joined your workspace.</p> <p>Install the server first. You need its address and the device enrolment key.</p>"},{"location":"admin/agents/index.html#what-you-need","title":"What you need","text":"You need Where it comes from The installer <code>AI-Firewall-&lt;version&gt;-x64.exe</code> for Windows and <code>AI-Firewall-&lt;version&gt;-arm64.pkg</code> for macOS. ScienceSoft provides them. The server address The public address you gave to setup, for example <code>https://ai-firewall.example.com</code>. It must be <code>https://</code>. The device enrolment key Shown by server setup in <code>credentials.txt</code>. You can also make a new one in the admin portal: open Tenants, select your workspace, open the Tokens tab and choose Mint token. The portal shows a new key only once, so copy it at once. Later it shows only a short hash. A way to deploy software Microsoft Intune, Jamf, a Group Policy startup script, or any tool that can run an installer as administrator."},{"location":"admin/agents/index.html#supported-computers","title":"Supported computers","text":"System Supported Windows 10 and Windows 11, 64-bit Intel or AMD Yes macOS on Apple silicon (M1 or newer) Yes macOS on Intel Macs No Linux No <p>These are the lowest versions of the platform that the app is built on: Windows 10, and macOS 13 (Ventura) or later. ScienceSoft has tested the agent on Windows 11 and on current macOS releases. Windows on ARM computers run the 64-bit Intel build under emulation. That has been tested only in a virtual machine.</p>"},{"location":"admin/agents/index.html#the-enrolment-key","title":"The enrolment key","text":"<p>The key joins a computer to your workspace. It is one key for the whole organisation. When a computer joins, it gets its own credential and stores it in the system keychain. The shared key is not used again by that computer.</p> <ul> <li>Anyone who has the key can join a computer to your workspace. Keep it in a password manager or a secret store. Do not put it in a ticket, a chat, or a script on a shared drive that everyone can read.</li> <li>Pass it to the installer when you deploy. After the pilot, consider replacing the key (see below).</li> <li>If the key is wrong, the computer does not appear in the portal. It keeps working on its own, with the local default settings. Nothing is blocked, but the policy does not reach it.</li> <li>To replace the key, make a new one in Tenants, your workspace, Tokens, update your deployment, and then revoke the old key. More than one key can be active at once, so there is no gap.</li> <li>A revoked or expired key stops only new joins. Computers that already joined keep working and keep their own credential. A computer that is reinstalled or enrolled again needs a key that is still active. A computer that tries a revoked key appears under Join failures in the portal.</li> <li>Installers or scripts that you already built with the old key no longer join computers. Update your deployment. The address and the key are read only when the agent is installed, so reinstall a computer to change them.</li> <li>To stop one computer, revoke that device in the portal. Revoking a key does not remove any computer.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"admin/agents/index.html#what-the-installer-changes-on-a-computer","title":"What the installer changes on a computer","text":"<p>Tell your security team about this before the pilot. ScienceSoft AI Endpoint Firewall is installed on your own managed computers, to check traffic to AI services.</p> <p>On Windows:</p> <ul> <li>Installs the app in <code>C:\\Program Files\\AI Firewall</code>. The folder is locked, so a standard user cannot change it.</li> <li>Creates a certificate for this computer (called \"AI Firewall Local CA\") and adds it to the computer's trusted root certificates. The private key stays on the computer.</li> <li>Sets a computer-wide proxy setting that sends AI traffic to the local firewall, and sets Chrome, Edge and Firefox to use it.</li> <li>Installs protection services and scheduled tasks that restart the app. A standard user cannot stop them.</li> <li>Needs no restart.</li> </ul> <p>On macOS:</p> <ul> <li>Installs the app in <code>/Applications</code>.</li> <li>Creates a certificate for this Mac and trusts it in the System keychain.</li> <li>Installs background services, and sets the automatic proxy setting of every network connection to the local firewall. This replaces an automatic proxy setting that your company already has.</li> </ul> <p>Network: the agent talks to your server address. It also downloads its detection models and updates. See Network access below.</p> <p>Uninstalling removes the certificate and the settings that ScienceSoft AI Endpoint Firewall added. See Windows and macOS.</p>"},{"location":"admin/agents/index.html#network-access","title":"Network access","text":"<p>The agent needs outbound HTTPS (port 443) to:</p> <ul> <li>Your server address.</li> <li>The update address. The address built into the installer, unless you set another one with <code>/UPDATE_URL</code> on Windows or with <code>updateUrl</code> in <code>enrollment.json</code>. To take updates from your own server, publish the update feed there and set that address. It must start with <code>https://</code>.</li> <li><code>huggingface.co</code>, where it downloads its detection models. Hugging Face also serves large files from its own download addresses, under <code>huggingface.co</code> and <code>hf.co</code>. Allow those names too. Test this from a pilot computer on a network that blocks everything by default. The agent checks every model file against a fixed hash after the download.</li> </ul> <p>The agent opens no inbound connections from the network. It listens only on the computer itself (<code>127.0.0.1</code>).</p>"},{"location":"admin/agents/index.html#what-the-employee-sees","title":"What the employee sees","text":"<p>With a silent installation, the employee sees nothing during the installation. The app starts in the tray (the Windows notification area, or the macOS menu bar), and the tray icon shows the protection state. The employee cannot stop protection or remove it. See the User Guide for what the app shows.</p>"},{"location":"admin/agents/index.html#next","title":"Next","text":"<ul> <li>Windows</li> <li>macOS</li> <li>Check that computers joined</li> </ul>"},{"location":"admin/agents/macos.html","title":"macOS","text":"<p>ScienceSoft AI Endpoint Firewall for macOS runs on Apple silicon Macs (M1 or newer). There is no Intel version.</p>"},{"location":"admin/agents/macos.html#the-installer","title":"The installer","text":"File Use <code>AI-Firewall-&lt;version&gt;-arm64.pkg</code> Deploying to many Macs. Signed and notarized. <code>.dmg</code> and <code>.zip</code> Manual installation by one person, and automatic updates. <p>Use the <code>.pkg</code> for managed deployment. It installs for the whole Mac, never for one user only.</p>"},{"location":"admin/agents/macos.html#the-enrolment-key-goes-in-a-file-not-in-the-command","title":"The enrolment key goes in a file, not in the command","text":"<p>On macOS the installer takes no arguments. The enrolment key and the server address are read from a file that you create before you run the installer:</p> <pre><code>/Library/Application Support/ai-firewall/enrollment.json\n</code></pre> <pre><code>{\"fleetUrl\": \"https://ai-firewall.example.com\", \"token\": \"&lt;enrolment-key&gt;\"}\n</code></pre> <ul> <li>The address must be <code>https://</code>. The key must be plain text without spaces.</li> <li>The file must belong to <code>root</code>, group <code>wheel</code>, and be readable by <code>root</code> only (mode <code>0600</code>). It holds the key.</li> <li>The installer copies the address and the key into a protected settings file. The file <code>enrollment.json</code> stays on the Mac with the key in it. Keep it readable by <code>root</code> only, or delete it after the install if your policy asks for that.</li> <li>The installer ignores an address or key that it considers invalid, and the Mac is then not enrolled. Use printable ASCII only. The key can be up to 4096 characters and the address up to 2048. The address must not contain <code>?</code>, <code>#</code>, <code>@</code> or <code>..</code>.</li> <li>The file is read when the package is installed. To change the address or the key later, write the new file and install the package again.</li> </ul> <p>Your management tool must create this file on every Mac before it installs the package.</p>"},{"location":"admin/agents/macos.html#install","title":"Install","text":"<p>From the command line, as an administrator:</p> <pre><code>sudo installer -pkg AI-Firewall-&lt;version&gt;-arm64.pkg -target /\n</code></pre> <p>The target must be <code>/</code>, the system volume.</p>"},{"location":"admin/agents/macos.html#jamf-pro","title":"Jamf Pro","text":"<ol> <li>Upload the <code>.pkg</code> to Jamf.</li> <li>Add a script, or a file-deployment step, that creates <code>enrollment.json</code> as described above, with your server address and key.</li> <li>Create a policy that has both. Set the script to run Before the package.</li> <li>Scope the policy to a pilot smart group first.</li> </ol>"},{"location":"admin/agents/macos.html#other-tools","title":"Other tools","text":"<p>Any tool that can run a script as root and then install a <code>.pkg</code> works in the same way: write the file, then install.</p>"},{"location":"admin/agents/macos.html#what-the-installer-does","title":"What the installer does","text":"<p>It runs as root and:</p> <ul> <li>creates a certificate for this Mac and trusts it in the System keychain. If trusting fails, your MDM must deliver a trust profile, or an administrator must approve it;</li> <li>installs the background services;</li> <li>sets the automatic proxy configuration of every network connection to the local firewall. This replaces an automatic proxy setting that your company already has.</li> </ul> <p>The agent needs no Full Disk Access, Accessibility, Screen Recording or other privacy permission, and it installs no system extension or network extension. It installs launch daemons from the package. The one approval it can need is trust for its certificate, as described above: from an install script, macOS does not let the certificate be trusted without an MDM trust profile or an administrator's approval. On macOS 13 and later the system may also show a notice that background items were added. That notice is not a request for approval.</p> <p>ScienceSoft AI Endpoint Firewall does not provide a configuration profile (<code>.mobileconfig</code>). The profiles that you may find for other ScienceSoft products do not work with it.</p>"},{"location":"admin/agents/macos.html#remove-the-agent","title":"Remove the agent","text":"<p>Run the uninstall script that comes with the app, as root:</p> <pre><code>sudo \"/Applications/AI Firewall.app/Contents/Resources/uninstall.command\"\n</code></pre> <p>It removes the app, the services, the certificate and the proxy setting if it points at ScienceSoft AI Endpoint Firewall. It also removes the managed Claude Code and Codex settings of the firewall and, for the user who runs it, puts back the MCP servers and skills that the agent had removed from those tools.</p> <p>The script asks you to type <code>remove</code>, and there is no switch to skip that. If it runs with no terminal, as it does in many management tools, it stops without changing anything, and it still returns exit code 0, so your tool shows success. Give it the answer on standard input:</p> <pre><code>echo remove | sudo \"/Applications/AI Firewall.app/Contents/Resources/uninstall.command\"\n</code></pre> <p>Try this on a pilot Mac first.</p> <ul> <li>The script always returns 0. Check the result in its output: for each item the last lines say that it is gone, or <code>STILL PRESENT</code> followed by the path.</li> <li>Run by a tool as root, it cannot clean the home folders of the people who use the Mac. It removes the app, the services, the certificate in the System keychain and the proxy setting. The per-user data of each user (history, preferences, logs and the user's keychain entry) stays, and the user can remove it.</li> <li>Notice to your server. In version 2.2.7, an uninstall run by a management tool sends no notice to your server. The Mac stops reporting. Remove it from the portal. Newer versions report the removal when the uninstall runs as root on a Mac that was installed from the package.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"admin/agents/verify.html","title":"Check that computers joined","text":""},{"location":"admin/agents/verify.html#in-the-admin-portal","title":"In the admin portal","text":"<p>Open the admin portal at <code>https://&lt;your-server&gt;/admin</code> and go to Dashboards &gt; Devices. A computer that has joined appears within a few seconds. Check:</p> <ul> <li>that it is online;</li> <li>that it has received the policy (the policy version is shown).</li> </ul> <p>If you deployed to a pilot group, compare the number of computers in the list with the number you targeted.</p> <p></p> <p>The picture shows a test workspace. Host names and IDs are examples.</p>"},{"location":"admin/agents/verify.html#on-the-computer","title":"On the computer","text":"<p>Windows</p> Check Where The enrolment file exists <code>%PROGRAMDATA%\\ai-firewall\\enrollment.json</code> The outcome of the installation <code>%PROGRAMDATA%\\ai-firewall\\install-enrolment.log</code> says whether enrolment worked, or why it was skipped. It never contains the key. The app is connected Open the app, then Device &amp; coverage. The fleet line shows your server. <p>macOS</p> Check Where The file that you wrote <code>/Library/Application Support/ai-firewall/enrollment.json</code> The app is connected Open the app, then Device &amp; coverage. The fleet line shows your server. <p>Also check Certificate trust. It must say Yes \u2014 installed. See the User Guide.</p>"},{"location":"admin/agents/verify.html#if-a-computer-does-not-appear","title":"If a computer does not appear","text":"What you see Likely cause What to do The computer works, but is not in the portal The key or the address is missing or wrong. On Windows, one of the two switches was missing: both are needed Check <code>install-enrolment.log</code>. Reinstall with both values. The log says enrolment was skipped The address was cut off, usually because it was not in quotes Give the full <code>https://</code> address in quotes. An address that does not start with <code>https://</code> is written to the file, but the app does not use it. The portal does not show it, and the app says it cannot reach the server A firewall or proxy blocks the server address Allow the address of your server from the computers. It joined once, and is now shown as offline The computer is off, or cannot reach the server Wait. Check the network. It was removed from the portal An administrator revoked it Reinstall. Computers installed before you replaced the key do not join They were installed with the old key Reinstall them with the new key. <p>An installation that is wrong in one computer does not stop protection on that computer. See the User Guide for the status messages the user sees.</p>"},{"location":"admin/agents/verify.html#for-security-teams","title":"For security teams","text":"<p>The agent asks for no inbound connections. It needs outbound access to your server address, plus the update address and <code>huggingface.co</code> for its detection models. The full list is under Network access. It listens only on the computer itself (<code>127.0.0.1</code>).</p>"},{"location":"admin/agents/windows.html","title":"Windows","text":""},{"location":"admin/agents/windows.html#install-on-one-computer","title":"Install on one computer","text":"<p>Run the installer as an administrator. You will see a short wizard and one administrator prompt. The enrolment key and the server address are given on the command line, so the computer joins your workspace by itself.</p>"},{"location":"admin/agents/windows.html#silent-installation","title":"Silent installation","text":"<p>Run the installer from an elevated command prompt, or as the system account (as Intune and most deployment tools do):</p> <pre><code>AI-Firewall-&lt;version&gt;-x64.exe /S /FLEET_URL=\"https://&lt;your-server&gt;\" /ENROLL_TOKEN=\"&lt;enrolment-key&gt;\"\n</code></pre> Switch Meaning <code>/S</code> Silent: no windows. <code>/FLEET_URL=</code> The address of your server. Must start with <code>https://</code>. <code>/ENROLL_TOKEN=</code> The device enrolment key. <code>/UPDATE_URL=</code> Optional. Where the agent looks for updates, usually <code>https://&lt;your-server&gt;/updates/generic</code>. <code>/D=&lt;folder&gt;</code> Optional. The install folder. It must be the last switch. Use the default folder. A folder that is a link to another place, or is inside a user's profile folder, is refused on a new install. A drive root or a system folder is not locked down. <ul> <li>Give both <code>/FLEET_URL</code> and <code>/ENROLL_TOKEN</code>. If only one is given, the installer ignores both and the computer works on its own, without your workspace.</li> <li>Put the values in quotes. If you start the installer from PowerShell, use <code>Start-Process -Wait</code> and read the exit code.</li> <li>The installer does not restart the computer.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"admin/agents/windows.html#exit-codes","title":"Exit codes","text":"Code Meaning What to do 0 Installed Success. Check that the computer joined in the portal. 1 Cancelled Someone stopped the installer. 2 Not installed The previous version could not be removed, or the install folder is a link to another place or is inside a user's profile folder, or the folder <code>%ProgramData%\\ai-firewall</code> could not be secured. Choose a folder under Program Files. If a file was held open, restart the computer and run the installer again. 3 Installed, but not locked down The app is installed, but the install folder was not locked, or the protection service may not have been registered. This happens on an update, with the folder problems described for code 2. Treat it as success for the rollout, then reinstall into the default folder. <p>A missing or incomplete server address still returns 0, and the computer then works on its own. The reason is written to <code>%ProgramData%\\ai-firewall\\install-enrolment.log</code>. Check the result with Check that computers joined.</p> <p>The reason for code 3 is shown only in the installer window, which is hidden in a silent install. For code 3 nothing is written to the Windows event log. If the server address is missing, the installer writes a warning to the Application event log (source AI Firewall, event 124) and adds it to <code>install-enrolment.log</code>, and the exit code stays 0.</p>"},{"location":"admin/agents/windows.html#microsoft-intune","title":"Microsoft Intune","text":"<p>The installer is an <code>.exe</code>, not an <code>.msi</code>. Deploy it as a Windows app (Win32).</p> <ol> <li>Put the <code>.exe</code> alone in an empty folder.</li> <li>Wrap it with the Microsoft Win32 Content Prep Tool (<code>IntuneWinAppUtil.exe</code>). It produces an <code>.intunewin</code> file.</li> <li>In Intune, add a Windows app (Win32) and upload the file.</li> <li> <p>Set the commands:</p> Setting Value Install command <code>AI-Firewall-&lt;version&gt;-x64.exe /S /FLEET_URL=\"https://&lt;your-server&gt;\" /ENROLL_TOKEN=\"&lt;enrolment-key&gt;\"</code> Uninstall command <code>\"%ProgramFiles%\\AI Firewall\\Uninstall AI Firewall.exe\" /allusers /S</code> Install behavior System Restart behavior No specific action </li> <li> <p>Detection rule: a file exists, <code>%ProgramFiles%\\AI Firewall\\AI Firewall.exe</code>. This works when the default folder is used. You can also use the installer's uninstall entry in the registry (<code>HKLM\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Uninstall</code>, 64-bit view). Its <code>DisplayName</code> is \"ScienceSoft AI Endpoint Firewall\" followed by the version, so do not match it exactly, and its <code>DisplayVersion</code> is the app version.</p> </li> <li>Requirements: 64-bit, Windows 10 or Windows 11.</li> <li>Return codes: add code <code>3</code> as Success, in addition to the defaults.</li> <li>Assign the app to a pilot group first.</li> </ol> <p>The key is visible in Intune</p> <p>The install command is stored in Intune with the key in it. Limit who can read the app's settings, and replace the key after the pilot if you are concerned.</p> <p>Try the install, the uninstall and the detection rule on a pilot computer before you assign the app widely.</p>"},{"location":"admin/agents/windows.html#group-policy-startup-script","title":"Group Policy (startup script)","text":"<p>ScienceSoft AI Endpoint Firewall has no Group Policy template (<code>.admx</code>). To use Group Policy, run the installer from a computer startup script:</p> <ol> <li>Put the installer on a share that computer accounts can read.</li> <li> <p>Add a startup script (Computer Configuration, Policies, Windows Settings, Scripts, Startup) that runs the installer with the switches from Silent installation, if the app is not installed yet.</p> </li> <li> <p>Link the policy to a pilot organisational unit first.</p> </li> </ol> <p>The enrolment key is on the command line of the script. Protect the share, so that only computer accounts can read it.</p>"},{"location":"admin/agents/windows.html#remove-the-agent","title":"Remove the agent","text":"<p>The uninstaller needs administrator rights.</p> <pre><code>\"%ProgramFiles%\\AI Firewall\\Uninstall AI Firewall.exe\" /allusers /S\n</code></pre> <p>This is the quiet uninstall command that the installer registers. Run it as an administrator or as the system account. Without administrator rights it asks for elevation.</p> <p>It removes the app, the services, the certificate and the settings that ScienceSoft AI Endpoint Firewall added. It also puts back the MCP servers and skills that the agent had removed from the user's Claude Code and Codex configuration, but only for the account that runs the uninstaller. It does not restore a computer-wide proxy setting that existed before ScienceSoft AI Endpoint Firewall was installed. Local data of every user on the computer is deleted. See the User Guide for what is removed.</p> <p>In version 2.2.7, an uninstall run by a deployment tool sends no notice to your server. The computer stops reporting. Remove it from the portal. Newer versions report the removal to the server when the uninstaller runs as the system account on a computer that has the protection service. An uninstall that an administrator starts by hand sends nothing.</p>"},{"location":"admin/fleet/index.html","title":"Configure your fleet","text":"<p>The admin portal is where your security team manages the computers that run the agent. It makes the keys that join computers to your server, shows which computers are protected, sets what the agent does, and shows what the agents reported. This part of the guide explains each screen by the job you do on it.</p> <p>This guide describes version 2.2.8. Open the portal at <code>https://&lt;your-address&gt;/admin</code>. Sign-in and roles are on Sign-in and roles.</p> <p>The pictures in this part come from a test workspace. Names, addresses and IDs in them are examples. Some pictures were taken on a pre-release build, so a screen on your server can differ in small details.</p>"},{"location":"admin/fleet/index.html#words-you-will-meet","title":"Words you will meet","text":"<p>The portal uses several words for the same thing. This guide uses the first word of each row.</p> In this guide In the portal Meaning Workspace Tenant One organisation's set of computers, settings and records on the server. A server can hold more than one Enrolment key Token, enrolment token The secret that lets a computer join a workspace. The agent asks for it as the \"enrollment code\" Group Group A named set of computers that can get their own settings and policies Settings Config The values the server sends to the agents. They are on the Config tab Policy Policy Per-service rules for the whole organisation or for a group. They are on the Policies page"},{"location":"admin/fleet/index.html#how-every-page-looks","title":"How every page looks","text":"<p>Every page has one header: the title on the left, then the Tenant list (the workspace you are looking at), a Window list on dashboards, the page's own buttons, and Sign out on the right. There are no Back buttons: use the menu or the browser's Back button.</p> <p></p> <p>The line under your name at the bottom of the menu says what the server grants your account: Super admin, Tenant admin \u00b7  or Read-only. <p>If you leave the Config or Policy tab of a workspace with changes that are not saved, the portal asks Save your changes? with Cancel, Discard and Save. The Policies page does not ask.</p>"},{"location":"admin/fleet/index.html#the-menu","title":"The menu","text":"<p>The left menu has these entries. The words are the portal's own.</p> Menu entry Use it to Page Overview See the most useful numbers of one workspace on one screen Audit and evidence Policies Set per-service rules for AI tools, for the whole organisation and for groups Policies Settings &gt; Tenants Create a workspace, make enrolment keys, manage groups, change settings, see who can sign in. A workspace has nine tabs Workspaces, Enrolment keys, Devices and groups, Settings Settings &gt; License See the licence and how many devices it covers Workspaces Audit Read what the agents reported and the recorded conversations. Tabs: Events, Activity, Conversations, False positives Audit and evidence Dashboards &gt; Alerts See computers and people that need attention now Audit and evidence Dashboards &gt; Devices Revoke a computer, delete a revoked record, change group membership Devices and groups Dashboards &gt; AI Activity, Dev Tools, Risk, Users See how AI tools are used. The screens are titled Capture, Developer Tool Inventory, Risk and Users Audit and evidence Dashboards &gt; Overall analytics, Detection quality See AI usage over a date range, and how accurately data is detected Audit and evidence Reports Print or export a summary Audit and evidence Join failures See computers that tried to join and were refused Enrolment keys Exception requests See requests for an exception to a blocked or masked message Audit and evidence <p>The list of computers of one workspace is on the Devices tab of the workspace, not on Dashboards &gt; Devices. See Devices and groups.</p> <p>Some entries are not covered here.</p> <ul> <li>Settings &gt; Setup guide prints commands for another product at step 3: do not use them. Use Roll out the agent. Gateway is not covered.</li> <li>Settings &gt; Releases has two tabs, SS AI Setup (it opens first) and AI Firewall. The AI Firewall tab lists the installers on your server's update feed. On a server that does not host a feed, the page says so.</li> <li>Two screens have no menu entry and open only by their address: Applications and Destinations. This guide does not cover them.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"admin/fleet/index.html#who-can-do-what","title":"Who can do what","text":"<p>Every person has one base role. The server checks the role on every request.</p> What you want to do <code>admin</code> of the workspace <code>analyst</code> or <code>auditor</code> <code>super-admin</code> Read the screens of the workspace Yes Yes Yes, in all workspaces Change settings, policies, groups, enrolment keys and devices Yes No Yes Approve or reject an exception request Yes No Yes Suspend, unsuspend or convert a workspace Yes No Yes Create a workspace, delete a revoked device record No No Yes Create, disable or reset the password of a workspace login No No Yes See Join failures, Releases and the platform-wide admin log No No Yes <p>An <code>admin</code>, <code>analyst</code> or <code>auditor</code> sees only the workspace in their sign-in. A <code>super-admin</code> sees all workspaces.</p> <p>The buttons look active for everyone, but the server refuses</p> <p>The portal does not hide or grey out the buttons that change things. An <code>analyst</code> or an <code>auditor</code> who presses Publish, Mint token or Revoke gets a refusal from the server (HTTP 403) and nothing changes. The menu line under the name says Read-only for them, but the chip on some pages still reads \"read-write\". Give people the role that fits the job, and tell analysts and auditors that their access is read-only.</p> <p>The emergency token on the sign-in page has the highest rights on the server, but the portal shows Read-only for it and disables most change buttons. Use a personal account for changes.</p>"},{"location":"admin/fleet/index.html#what-refreshes-itself","title":"What refreshes itself","text":"<p>The device list refreshes itself every 30 seconds and the Alerts count in the menu every minute. Other screens load once. Use their Refresh button. The workspace and the tab you are on stay in the address, so a reload keeps them. Some dashboards open on the first workspace in the list, so pick yours again.</p>"},{"location":"admin/fleet/index.html#where-to-start","title":"Where to start","text":"<ol> <li>Create a workspace, or use the one that setup made for you.</li> <li>Make an enrolment key and roll out the agent.</li> <li>Check the computers and put them in groups.</li> <li>Set the settings and the policies for your organisation.</li> <li>Read What the workspace records before you tell employees what the product does.</li> </ol>"},{"location":"admin/fleet/audit.html","title":"Audit and evidence","text":"<p>Everything on this page is read-only, except answering an exception request. The roles <code>admin</code>, <code>analyst</code> and <code>auditor</code> can read it, in their own workspace. What is recorded and for how long is on What the workspace records.</p>"},{"location":"admin/fleet/audit.html#events","title":"Events","text":"<p>Audit, tab Events, titled Recent audit events, lists what the agents scanned and did in the last 90 days. Older events are not available. The tabs of Audit are Events, Activity, Conversations and False positives.</p> <p></p> <p>The picture shows a test workspace. Names, addresses and IDs are examples.</p> <ul> <li>Pick the workspace in Tenant, set the filters and select Apply. With several workspaces, the page waits until you do. Until then it says \"Apply a filter to see recent audit events. Tenant is required.\" It says the same when the window has no events, so widen hours back before you decide that nothing was recorded.</li> <li>Filters. action (<code>pass</code>, <code>log</code>, <code>mask</code>, <code>reroute</code>, <code>block</code>), data type, hours back (24 by default), from, to, user (exactly the stored user value), deviceId, destination (exactly the host), application, and limit (up to 1000 rows). Reset clears them. Refresh is at the top of the page and Load more is under the table. The filters you apply are kept in the address.</li> <li>Columns. Time, Action, Policy (the policy, its layer and the control that decided; filled only for computers with a published policy; for a warned request it also says how the warning ended, for example \"warned; no prompt shown; blocked\"), Domain with the path under it, User, PII, Device (its ID, not the host name) and Bytes.</li> <li>PII lists what was found. Where a detector knows the identifier, it names it, for example \"Polish PESEL\"; rows from older agents show only the category. Under it, a row can say not fully inspected or not checked, with the reason: a password-protected file, a file nested too deeply, text recognition not available on the computer, an image that could not be read, a file type that is not supported, or a file too large. Such a request went out without a full check. Take it seriously: you do not know what it contained.</li> <li>Open a row that has context or images to see the captured context (the masked text around each detection, with offsets) and, for an image, only the redaction map (\"Image not retained\").</li> </ul> <p>The list is not only detections: the agent writes a row for every request it scans, including the ones where it found nothing (<code>pass</code>). The tooltip that says \"every detected PII event\" is not accurate.</p> <p>There is no export on the Events tab. Reports can export a CSV of the newest 500 events.</p>"},{"location":"admin/fleet/audit.html#the-integrity-checks","title":"The integrity checks","text":"<p>Chain integrity counts the events that passed the chain check since the server last started, in all workspaces. The figures are kept in memory and start again at zero after a restart. A re-anchored segment is normal after a network gap or a restore. It is not tampering.</p> <p>The section Merkle checkpoints \u2014 per-batch attestation is closed at first and loads when you open it. It lists the latest 20 batches the server attested for the workspace: range, number of events, Merkle root, source and when it was anchored.</p> <p>A standard server has no trusted timestamp</p> <p>On a server installed with <code>aifw</code>, the checkpoints are marked local marker only, which is not a trusted timestamp. A trusted timestamp needs a time-stamping service address that an administrator sets on the server. Without it, do not present the checkpoints to an auditor as one. The server makes checkpoints on its own, about every five minutes. The portal has no button to make one, so on a new server the section can say \"No tamper-evidence checkpoints yet for this tenant\" for a short time.</p> <p>How the chain works: each computer links its events in order, and signs each batch with its own key. The server checks every link and flags a repeat, a missing event, a change or an order break as suspect. This proves that events were not changed on their way to the server. It does not protect against someone who has administrator access to the server's database. The checkpoint section also tells an auditor to verify with a tool called <code>audit-verify</code>. This tool is not part of what you receive, so a check you run yourself is not available.</p> <p>An agent keeps events for up to 30 days when it cannot reach the server, and sends them when it can.</p>"},{"location":"admin/fleet/audit.html#activity-and-false-positives","title":"Activity and false positives","text":"<p>Audit &gt; Activity (titled Audit activity) is the same feed for the last hours of one workspace, with the columns Timestamp, User, Risk, Domain, PII types and Action, and a link Investigate \u2192 when a user exists. The Risk column is always empty.</p> <p>Audit &gt; False positives (titled False-positive reports) lists detections that people marked as wrong, with the noisiest patterns first. Choose a window of 7, 30 or 90 days. The page says \"Operators can flag a match from the Traffic Log in the desktop app \u2014 reports appear here once a device ships one.\" The tables are Top noisy patterns and Recent reports. A report sent by an older app can carry the matched text. The column Matched text then shows it, and otherwise says \"value not sent by the device\".</p>"},{"location":"admin/fleet/audit.html#conversations","title":"Conversations","text":"<p>Audit, tab Conversations, shows the conversations the computers had with AI services, as far as the workspace keeps them. See How much of a conversation is kept. Content stays for 30 days.</p> <p></p> <ul> <li>When the Trace content setting is <code>masked</code> or <code>metadata</code>, the page says \"This workspace keeps no conversations\" and explains what is kept instead, as in the picture. Changing the mode does not remove what is already stored: older conversations are hidden and removed after 30 days.</li> <li>Find. Search chats, models and devices. The search shows up to 25 of 200 matches. Filter by Masked, Blocked or Passed, by service, device and person. Group by time, device or person. The filters appear when there are conversations.</li> <li>Read. Each message has a label that says how it was recorded. The AI service's reply is not shown, because it is never sent. Attachments show by type and size only. A match on an organisation keyword is labelled Organisation keyword.</li> <li>Download. Download this exchange, or a JSON export of a conversation (the latest 60 exchanges).</li> <li>The Person filter and grouping are empty unless audit records name the person.</li> </ul> <p>\"No conversations yet\" and \"This workspace keeps no conversations\" are different: the second means the setting keeps none.</p>"},{"location":"admin/fleet/audit.html#overview-and-dashboards","title":"Overview and dashboards","text":"<ul> <li>Overview shows, for one workspace, four sections: Alerts, Devices, AI Activity of the last 7 days, and the Client tile. The Client tile checks update versions against the update feed of another product. On a server that hosts only this product it can be empty or say that no feed is hosted. Ignore it. Reload the page to see new figures: it does not refresh itself.</li> <li>AI Activity (the screen is titled Capture) shows counters: requests, scan rate, latency, the mix of actions (Pass, Mask, Block, Log, Reroute), the top destinations, and how the two detectors agree. The same numbers are on the Capture tab of a workspace. If it says \"No capture data yet\", counters are off or no computer has reported. The workspace setting <code>capture.stats</code> turns them on.</li> <li>Dev Tools (titled Developer Tool Inventory) lists the MCP servers and skills the agents found on the computers. It says \"No developer tools reported yet\" until they report. Paths show the operating-system account name. The card Not allowed in AI coding agents lists the items that the AI coding agents policy does not allow, with the device, the reason and the enforcement. The column Known vulnerabilities shows, for each MCP server, how many known vulnerabilities its package has and the worst one. A button Vulnerable only filters the list, and the device page lists the advisories with links. The data comes from OSV advisories, and the page shows its date. \"None known\" does not mean safe: a server without a pinned version, a removed one and one in a container cannot be checked, and skills are not checked.</li> <li>Overall analytics shows one workspace over a date range. It opens with Coverage: how much AI traffic was not fully inspected and why, and a table of the computers behind those numbers. Below it are the volume per channel, the top users and the breakdown of the kinds of data found. In version 2.2.8-beta.1 the two lists of top users still say \"Per-user aggregation pending\". The page is built once a day, so it can be up to a day old.</li> <li>Detection quality shows how accurately sensitive data is detected: precision and recall per type from lab tests, and how often the fleet's detectors agree. The link False-positive reports at the top opens the same list as Audit &gt; False positives.</li> </ul> <p>The pictures show a test workspace. Host names are examples.</p> <p></p> <p>The picture shows the portal's demo with sample data.</p> <p></p> <p>The picture shows a test workspace. Names, addresses and IDs are examples.</p>"},{"location":"admin/fleet/audit.html#alerts","title":"Alerts","text":"<p>Dashboards &gt; Alerts lists the computers and people that need attention now, in two sections: Devices and People and AI services. The number on the menu entry adds up the open alerts of up to 25 workspaces, and the hover says how many. An alert clears itself when its cause goes away. You cannot acknowledge or mute it. The portal sends no e-mail for these alerts. An uninstalled or revoked computer raises none.</p> <p></p> <p>The picture shows a test workspace. Names, addresses and IDs are examples.</p> <p>The page shows one workspace at a time (list Tenant, with the number of open alerts after each name), while the menu number counts all. Why these rules? lists the thresholds. They are the same for every workspace.</p> Alert Raised when Never reported, Lost connection A computer has not reported for 1 hour Protection stopped, Not inspecting AI traffic Protection or inspection has been down for 10 minutes Out of date An old version for 7 days. Judged against the feed of another product. On a server with no feed it reads \"not checked\" Policy not applied The computer runs another settings version for 15 minutes Repeated blocked requests 10 blocks in 15 minutes Secret blocked A secret was blocked Personal data in uploads 3 uploads with personal data in an hour New AI service A host not seen in the last 30 days Browser going around the firewall, Proxy setting replaced A browser skipped protection, or the proxy setting was changed No AI traffic while on VPN 2 hours with a VPN up and no AI traffic <p>A Not checked block says what could not be judged: no update feed, old agents that do not report, less than 30 days of history, or more data than the page reads (5000 security events, 2000 hosts). A half-loaded page never reads as \"all clear\". Alerts about people are worked out from audit rows and so depend on who the audit record names.</p> <p>The health of the server itself (database, disk, audit intake, backups, services) is a different thing. Servers set up with a current <code>aifw</code> can e-mail those alerts to the administrator address given at setup, if the server has mail settings. See Operate and upgrade.</p>"},{"location":"admin/fleet/audit.html#reports-risk-and-users","title":"Reports, Risk and Users","text":"<p>Reports has five reports: Executive summary, Data protection (DLP), AI tool usage (shadow AI), User risk and Compliance &amp; audit trail. Each has Export CSV and Print / PDF. Risk and Users have their own pages.</p> <p>A report page has three lists at the top: the report, the workspace (Tenant) and the window (for example Last 30 days). The Executive summary shows the number of computers, the active users, the requests blocked and masked, a chart of requests per day, and how the requests were handled. A workspace with no traffic in the window says \"No activity in this window yet\".</p> <p></p> <p>The picture shows the portal's demo with sample data.</p> <p>All three use only the newest 500 events of the workspace, with no warning on the page. A 90-day report on a busy workspace covers a small part of it. The audit-trail report is not the chain: it is the same 500 rows. Risk is not a score out of 100: 5 for each block, 2 for each mask and 0.5 for each log. Use Audit with filters when you need the whole picture.</p> <p>Risk shows four counters (Active users, Elevated activity, Requests blocked, Requests masked), a chart of passed, masked and blocked requests per day, the most frequent kinds of data, and two lists: the riskiest users by enforcement activity and the riskiest devices by behaviour score. A Window list sets how far back it looks. Devices appear in the second list only when their agents report a behaviour score. Users shows the activity of each person in the window, with the top service, the top kinds of data, the mix of actions and the number of computers, and the buttons CSV and JSON. When audit records do not name people, the user is shown as \"(unknown)\".</p> <p></p> <p></p> <p>The pictures show a test workspace.</p>"},{"location":"admin/fleet/audit.html#exception-requests","title":"Exception requests","text":"<p>Exception requests in the menu lists requests for an exception to a blocked or masked message, with the kinds of data and the AI service. Pick the workspace in Tenant.</p> <p></p> <ul> <li>The columns are Requested, Device, What was found (the kinds of data by name), AI service, What happened (Blocked, Held or Masked) and Decision. A waiting request has the buttons Approve and Reject. A decided one reads \"Approved by  \u00b7 \" or \"Rejected by  \u00b7 \". <li>The page never shows the text of the prompt, only the kinds of data.</li> <li>The page says: \"Approving or rejecting records your answer and who gave it. It does not change anything on the employee's device: nothing is unblocked or sent again.\" The pause described below is the one exception.</li> <li>An <code>admin</code> of the workspace or a <code>super-admin</code> can decide. For anyone else the page says \"This sign-in can see these requests but cannot decide them.\" If someone else decided first, the page says \"Already decided by someone else. The list has been refreshed to show their answer.\" The decision goes into the admin log as <code>exception.request.decided</code>.</li> <p>The page is expected to stay empty for now</p> <p>The page and the server routes exist, and the system service can relay a request to the workspace, but no app sends exception requests yet. No screen on the computer asks the employee to request an exception. Do not tell employees that they can ask for one.</p> <p>The console cannot give an approval a length: the page has no field for it. Through the API, the server accepts a pause of 1 to 43200 minutes when a request is approved, and puts an end time for that one AI service into the computer's configuration. A computer that reads it sends requests to that service as typed until the pause ends, but still inspects and records them. A block on access and a block on uploads stay in force. Without a pause, an approval changes nothing on the computer.</p>"},{"location":"admin/fleet/audit.html#what-administrators-changed","title":"What administrators changed","text":"<p>Settings &gt; Tenants &gt; workspace &gt; Audit is the log of changes that administrators of the workspace made: When, Actor, Action, Target and Details.</p> <p></p> <p>The picture shows a test workspace. Names, addresses and IDs are examples.</p> <ul> <li>Actions include <code>config.push</code>, <code>config.rollback</code>, <code>tenant.provision</code>, <code>tenant.suspend</code>, <code>tenant.unsuspend</code>, <code>token.mint</code>, <code>token.revoke</code>, <code>device.revoke</code>, <code>device.delete</code>, <code>user.create</code> and <code>user.disable</code>. The server also records converting a workspace, setting an override, changing groups, publishing policies (<code>fleet.policy_v2.publish</code>), exception decisions and changes to user groups (<code>fleet.user_group.*</code>). These appear when you do not filter by action.</li> <li>Filters: actor, time (hour, 24 hours, 7 days, 30 days, all), action.</li> <li>Export CSV and Export JSON give all matching rows, up to 2000. The page warns you when it is capped.</li> <li>A <code>super-admin</code> also has a platform-wide log.</li> </ul> <p>The portal states no retention for this log, and the code does not delete from it.</p>"},{"location":"admin/fleet/audit.html#using-the-audit-trail-as-evidence","title":"Using the audit trail as evidence","text":"<ul> <li>The chain shows that events were not changed after they reached this server. It is not a timestamp from an independent party unless you set one up.</li> <li>The audit shows the types of data and the text with values replaced, never the values. Names and numbers that no detector found stay readable in the preview.</li> <li>Write down the date range and the filters when you export or take a screenshot for an auditor, and remember that Reports, Risk and Users are samples.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"admin/fleet/devices-and-groups.html","title":"Devices and groups","text":"<p>Two screens show computers, and they do different jobs.</p> Screen Where Use it to The device list Settings &gt; Tenants &gt; workspace &gt; tab Devices See every computer of one workspace, its status and protection, and put it in groups The device panel Dashboards &gt; Devices Revoke a computer, delete a revoked record, and change group membership <p>Both read one workspace at a time. Pick it in Tenant.</p>"},{"location":"admin/fleet/devices-and-groups.html#the-device-list","title":"The device list","text":"<p>The tab Devices of a workspace lists its computers. It refreshes itself every 30 seconds.</p> <p></p> <p>The picture shows a test workspace. Names, addresses and IDs are examples.</p> <p>At the top, four tiles count the computers: Online, Stale, Offline and Revoked. A banner says how many computers are not protected right now. A banner above the tiles can show a licence problem or the trial state.</p> Column Meaning Status <code>online</code>, <code>stale</code>, <code>offline</code>, \"never checked in\", <code>revoked</code> or \"removed\". See below Last seen When the server last heard from the computer App ver The version of the agent Mode Not the protection mode. On this product it always reads <code>config-pinned</code>. Ignore it Firewall Whether protection is running, and the protection mode in words such as <code>mask</code>. Chips such as \"Lost connection\" or \"Policy not applied\" show the alerts of the computer Protection Protected, Degraded, Off, Not protected, Unknown, or \"Not protected: AI traffic blocked until the proxy restarts\" Applied cfg The version of the settings the computer last applied <p>A computer with a protection problem is pinned to the top and tinted. A computer whose agent is too old to report protection reads Unknown, not a healthy value. Under the host name, Groups\u2026 opens the groups of the computer.</p>"},{"location":"admin/fleet/devices-and-groups.html#status","title":"Status","text":"<p>The server works the status out from the last time it heard from the computer.</p> Status Rule online Heard from it in the last 90 seconds stale Heard from it in the last hour, but not in the last 90 seconds offline Silent for more than an hour never checked in Joined, but never reported, and joined more than an hour ago removed The agent reported that it was uninstalled, and nothing newer has arrived revoked An administrator revoked it. This beats every other status <p>Times for online and offline</p> <p>Use the times in the table above: they are the ones the server uses. The About panel on Dashboards &gt; Devices gives other numbers.</p>"},{"location":"admin/fleet/devices-and-groups.html#a-long-list","title":"A long list","text":"<p>The portal asks the server for the first 200 computers and shows no notice when there are more. The tiles, the banner and \"showing N devices\" count only those. The server supports no larger page. If your workspace has more than 200 computers, use the filters and the export. Ask your ScienceSoft representative how large one workspace can be.</p>"},{"location":"admin/fleet/devices-and-groups.html#filters-and-export","title":"Filters and export","text":"<p>The top bar filters by Firewall mode (Monitor, Mask, Protect) and by Protection. CSV and JSON write the list, with host name, device ID, status, region, app version, system, mode, applied config, enrolled and last seen.</p>"},{"location":"admin/fleet/devices-and-groups.html#one-computer","title":"One computer","text":"<p>Select a row to open the device page. It has the tabs Diagnostics, Updates, Logs, Config, Capture, Audit, Integrity and Override.</p> <ul> <li>Diagnostics shows a headline for protection and the facts the agent reported: versions, system, host name, OS user, uptime, the applied config version, and posture lines such as Certificate checking and Recording. A line the agent did not report reads \"Not in the last report\", never a healthy value.</li> <li>Config shows the settings this computer actually applies, after groups and policies are layered on. Changed values are highlighted.</li> <li>Logs needs <code>runtimeLogs</code> switched on in the settings.</li> <li>Audit lists the last 100 events of the computer for 1 hour, 24 hours or 7 days.</li> <li>Override holds a JSON block that wins over everything else for this one computer. Use it for a test. It is not locked to an <code>admin</code>.</li> <li>Updates has a button \"Update devices to a version\". It pins every computer of the workspace, not just this one.</li> <li>Integrity talks about \"SS-managed config\". It is for another product. Ignore it.</li> </ul> <p>The fields OCR backend and Device behaviour score are empty for this product. Recent errors never appears.</p> <p>Operating-system user name</p> <p>Every enrolled computer sends its operating-system user name and its host name with each status report, and the device page shows them as OS user and Hostname. The server also keeps a list of the names that computers report. This happens whatever you set for who audit records name. See What the workspace records.</p>"},{"location":"admin/fleet/devices-and-groups.html#revoke-or-remove-a-computer","title":"Revoke or remove a computer","text":"<p>Revoke a computer to stop it. It loses access at its next request, and its seat in the licence is freed.</p> <ul> <li>On Dashboards &gt; Devices, Revoke is on every computer that is not revoked. The column Groups has a pencil button, \"Edit memberships\". Filter by status narrows the list. Select Revoke and confirm: \"Revoke device \u2026? It will lose access on next request.\" <li>A computer that was revoked can be deleted there, by a <code>super-admin</code> only. Its audit history is kept, and it must join again to come back.</li> <li>On the Devices tab of the workspace, Revoke appears only on a row with the status \"removed\".</li> <p>A revoked computer can come back</p> <p>If the computer joins again with an enrolment key that still works, it is let back in and its revocation is cleared. After you revoke a computer, also revoke the keys that could bring it back.</p> <p>Only an <code>admin</code> or <code>super-admin</code> can revoke. For anyone else the button is active, but the server refuses.</p>"},{"location":"admin/fleet/devices-and-groups.html#groups","title":"Groups","text":"<p>A group is a named set of computers that can get its own settings and policies. Open Tenants, pick the workspace and open the Groups tab.</p> <p></p> <p>A new workspace has no groups.</p> <p>The list shows Name, Parent, Config keys (the settings the group sets), Devices and Created. The device count is the computers placed directly in the group, not through a child group.</p>"},{"location":"admin/fleet/devices-and-groups.html#create-and-edit-a-group","title":"Create and edit a group","text":"<p>Select + New group.</p> <ul> <li>Name is required.</li> <li>Parent is <code>(root)</code> or another group.</li> <li>configBlob (JSON) is the group's own settings, as a JSON object. The default is <code>{}</code>. The server checks only that it is an object. It does not check the values against the settings. Prefer the Config tab: choose the scope Group there, and it checks them when you validate.</li> </ul> <p>A group cannot sit under itself or its own children. The portal says \"Cycle detected\".</p>"},{"location":"admin/fleet/devices-and-groups.html#put-computers-in-a-group","title":"Put computers in a group","text":"<ul> <li>On the device list, Groups\u2026 under the host name opens the group list of that computer. Save replaces the whole set of groups that this computer is in directly. The same is done with the pencil button on the device panel.</li> <li>On the Groups tab, Add devices\u2026 adds many computers to one group. It adds them one by one and stops at the first failure.</li> <li>The portal offers only the first 200 computers of the roster.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"admin/fleet/devices-and-groups.html#delete-a-group","title":"Delete a group","text":"<p>Select Delete and type the group name. If the group still has computers, tick Reassign members to parent group (force delete). You cannot undo it.</p>"},{"location":"admin/fleet/devices-and-groups.html#how-the-settings-of-groups-are-used","title":"How the settings of groups are used","text":"<p>Read this before you rely on groups.</p> <ol> <li>A computer gets the settings of its workspace.</li> <li>Then, for each group the computer is in directly, the group's settings are layered on. Groups are taken in order of their name, and the last name wins.</li> <li>Then the computer's own Override wins.</li> <li>If the workspace has published policies, they are layered last. They decide the protection mode, the types of data and the advanced rules where they have a value.</li> </ol> <p>Two things surprise people:</p> <ul> <li>A group replaces a whole block, not one value. If a group sets <code>firewall</code>, it replaces the workspace's whole <code>firewall</code> block, including the protection mode. Put every value of a block in the group, or leave the block out.</li> <li>Groups do not pass settings on to child groups. Only the groups a computer is directly in count. The parent is used for display only.</li> </ul> <p>One privacy setting, <code>traceContent</code>, is set for the whole workspace. A group or computer can only narrow it.</p> <p>For policies per group, see Policies. There are also user groups, groups of sign-in names, which the console cannot create. See Policies for people.</p>"},{"location":"admin/fleet/enrolment-keys.html","title":"Enrolment keys","text":"<p>An enrolment key is the secret that lets a computer join a workspace. You give it to the installer, and the computer swaps it for its own credential. Setup makes the first key. You make more on the Tokens tab of the workspace.</p>"},{"location":"admin/fleet/enrolment-keys.html#make-a-key","title":"Make a key","text":"<ol> <li>Open Settings &gt; Tenants and pick the workspace.</li> <li>Open the Tokens tab.</li> <li>Choose how long the key lives in Expiry: 7 days (the default), 30 days, 90 days or No expiry.</li> <li>Select Mint token.</li> </ol> <p>The portal shows the key once, in a box called \"New token (visible once)\". Copy it now. You cannot read it again. Later the list shows only a short hash of it. Only an <code>admin</code> or a <code>super-admin</code> can make or revoke keys.</p> <p>The key that setup made has no expiry and is on the credentials sheet. Setup also stores it on the server as <code>AIFW_ENROL_TOKEN</code> in the settings file. See Install the server.</p>"},{"location":"admin/fleet/enrolment-keys.html#the-list-of-keys","title":"The list of keys","text":"Column Meaning Hash A short fingerprint of the key. Hover for the full hash. The key itself is not stored Status active, revoked or expired. Revoked wins over expired Created When it was made Expires Never, or the time left, for example \"in 3d\" <p>The picture shows a test workspace. Names, addresses and IDs are examples.</p> <p>Overview shows the counters \"Active tokens\" and \"All tokens\".</p>"},{"location":"admin/fleet/enrolment-keys.html#one-key-joins-many-computers","title":"One key joins many computers","text":"<p>A key can be used by any number of computers until it expires or you revoke it. It is not single-use, and the portal does not count how often it was used. The wizard text that says a key is used \"once\" is not correct.</p> <ul> <li>Treat a key as a shared secret for a group of computers. Keep it in a password manager or a secret store. Do not put it in a ticket or a chat.</li> <li>Make a separate key for each rollout wave, with an expiry that fits. When the wave is finished, revoke its key.</li> <li>To replace a key, make a new one, update your deployment, then revoke the old one. Several keys can be active at once, so there is no gap.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"admin/fleet/enrolment-keys.html#revoke-a-key","title":"Revoke a key","text":"<p>Select Revoke on the row and confirm. The key stops working for new joins at once. Computers that already joined keep working and keep their own credential. Revoking a key does not remove any computer. See Devices and groups to stop one.</p>"},{"location":"admin/fleet/enrolment-keys.html#what-happens-when-a-computer-joins","title":"What happens when a computer joins","text":"<ol> <li>The agent sends the key, its host name and a few facts about the computer to the server.</li> <li>The server checks the key. A key that is unknown, revoked or expired is refused, and so is a key of a suspended workspace.</li> <li>The server checks the licence and, for a trial workspace, the device limit.</li> <li>The server makes a device ID and a device key for this computer. The device key is shown to the agent once. The server keeps only its hash.</li> <li>The agent stores the device key in the system keychain. From then on it uses only that key.</li> </ol> <p>Joining writes no row in the administrators' log. The computer appears in Devices.</p> <p>The same computer joining again gets the same device ID and a new device key, and its old key stops working. A revoked computer that joins again with a key that still works is let back in, and its revocation is cleared. So revoking a computer does not keep it out while an active key exists. Revoke the key as well.</p>"},{"location":"admin/fleet/enrolment-keys.html#where-the-agent-finds-the-key","title":"Where the agent finds the key","text":"<p>On a computer, the agent looks in this order and uses the first source that works:</p> <ol> <li>The file <code>enrollment.json</code> in the machine-wide folder. This is what your deployment writes: see Windows and macOS.</li> <li>A file in the user's own app folder, or a join made in the app's own join screen.</li> <li>Values built into the installer.</li> <li>The environment variables <code>AI_FIREWALL_FLEET_URL</code> and <code>AI_FIREWALL_ENROLLMENT_TOKEN</code>. These are the lowest priority and do not override the others.</li> </ol> <p>If the key from one source is refused, the agent tries the next. It needs both the server address and the key. With only one of them, it works on its own, as Local only.</p>"},{"location":"admin/fleet/enrolment-keys.html#computers-that-could-not-join","title":"Computers that could not join","text":"<p>Join failures lists computers the server turned away, with the reason. Only a <code>super-admin</code> can open it, because the rows belong to no workspace. The page header shows a fixed label All tenants for a <code>super-admin</code>, not a list to choose from. For anyone else it shows an error with \"HTTP 403\".</p> <p></p> <p>The page is empty when no computer has been refused. A refused computer appears as a row with the reason.</p> Column Meaning Device The host name Platform The operating system Why it couldn\u2019t join The reason and what to do Attempts How many times it tried Last tried When it last tried. The first attempt is not shown Reason What to do Unrecognized join token The key is wrong or was never made. Check it for typing errors Join token has expired Make a new key Join token was revoked Make a new key Organisation is suspended Unsuspend the workspace Incomplete join request The installer sent no host name or system data. Reinstall App is newer than the server Upgrade the server Trial has ended, Trial device limit reached Convert the workspace, or ask your ScienceSoft representative License has expired, All licensed devices are in use, License could not be verified See Licence and seats <p>The page keeps at most 200 rows, one for each host name and reason. The rows live in the server's memory only, so restarting the server empties the page. The key is never stored on it. For each of the licence and trial reasons the computer keeps protecting its user on its own.</p> <p>The hint on this page that says \"Send them a fresh token from Tenants \u2192 enrolment\" means the Tokens tab.</p>"},{"location":"admin/fleet/policies.html","title":"Policies","text":"<p>A policy tells the agents what to do for each AI service: whether it is allowed, what happens to personal data in text and in files, which uploads are allowed, what happens to each kind of document, which words are always replaced, and which MCP servers and skills the coding agents may use. You set one policy for the whole organisation and, if you need to, more for groups of computers.</p> <p>Open Policies in the left menu. This part of the guide describes version 2.2.8.</p> <p>Some words on the screens may still change before release</p> <p>The wording of the Policies screens is not final. Check each name on your own server.</p>"},{"location":"admin/fleet/policies.html#first-use","title":"First use","text":"<p>A workspace that has never published a policy shows the screen Not using policies yet.</p> <p></p> <p>The picture shows a test workspace. Names, addresses and IDs are examples.</p> <p>The page says: \"Your current settings become the organisation default.\" Publishing takes the workspace's protection mode, its types of data and its advanced rules and makes them the Organisation default policy, version <code>v1</code>. A group or a computer that sets any of these on its own gets a policy of its own. The table What publishing creates lists each policy it will make, and Devices whose result changes lists the computers whose result would change, from what to what. Nothing is sent until you select Publish to start using policies.</p> <p>Use Review the organisation default to look at the policy before you publish.</p>"},{"location":"admin/fleet/policies.html#the-list-of-policies","title":"The list of policies","text":"<p>After the first publish, the page lists your policies. The organisation default is always first. It applies to every computer, is always on, and cannot be deleted. Under the name of each policy a line says what it is for: Device-group policy, Single-device policy, User policy or User-group policy.</p> Column What it shows Policy The name Applies to The groups of computers, or the people, the policy is for Except Groups or people left out Enabled Whether the policy is on Running How many computers run the current version, as \"N of M\" with a bar. It reads \"Off \u00b7 no effect\" for a policy that is switched off <p>A computer counts as running a version only when its status report says so. A computer on an older app that does not report a policy version is counted separately: \"report no policy version (an older app, or no heartbeat yet)\". See devices opens the Devices tab of the workspace.</p>"},{"location":"admin/fleet/policies.html#edit-a-policy","title":"Edit a policy","text":"<p>Select a policy to edit it.</p> <p></p> <p>A policy has these parts. Not every part is on every policy.</p> Part What it does Name, Description, Enabled Basic facts. The organisation default cannot be switched off Applies to, Except The device groups the policy is for. See Policies for people Start from a policy preset Fills in the policy in one step. See Presets Data types The kinds of personal and confidential data computers look for. See Data types Services A table with one row for each AI service. See The services table Document types What a computer does with each kind of document, such as an NDA or source code. See Document types Keywords Words that are always replaced. See Keywords AI coding agents Which MCP servers and skills Claude Code and Codex may use. See AI coding agents Edit advanced rules Opens the rules builder. On the organisation default only. See Policy rules <p>Select Test this policy on a group policy, or Tester on the list, to try a policy without publishing it.</p>"},{"location":"admin/fleet/policies.html#data-types","title":"Data types","text":"<p>The card Data types offers four presets: Personal (12 types), Financial (8), Health (3) and Credentials (2). The lists Added and Removed, with Add a type and Remove a type, adjust the result.</p> <ul> <li>The Credentials preset adds passwords and credentials as data types. Scanning for secrets such as API keys and tokens follows the Secrets setting, not this card.</li> <li>A group policy shows the Data types card only once it sets data types, for example through a preset. It then offers Stop setting data types here.</li> <li>A group policy that sets data types replaces the organisation default's list. It does not add to it.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"admin/fleet/policies.html#the-services-table","title":"The services table","text":"<p>The table Services has one row for each AI service, and a first row, Default for all services, for any service whose own row sets nothing.</p> <p></p> Column Values What it controls Access Allow, Warn, Block Whether computers may use the service at all. A blocked service shows a block page If no one can be asked Block, Allow What a computer does where a Warn would need to ask the person. See below Mode Record only, Replace, Block What happens to detected data. See Protection modes Text mode Inherit, Record only, Replace, Block The mode for what people type and for tool results File mode Inherit, Record only, Replace, Block The mode for attached documents and images, a text file sent as an upload, and a file's text sent inside a message Uploads: Images, Documents, Other files Allow, Warn, Block Whether a kind of file may be uploaded <p>A row that sets neither Text mode nor File mode uses its Mode for both. Inherit keeps the value from the row or policy below it, and the cell says where it comes from, for example \"inherits Allow from the product default\". Filter services searches all services, and Show N more services shows the rest.</p> <p>Warn is offered for Access and for each upload control, not for Mode. No app shows the question to the person yet. A computer therefore does what If no one can be asked says: it blocks, unless you choose Allow. The cell under a Warn says so, for example \"No app asks yet: blocked, as If no one can be asked says\". Audit marks such a row \"warned; no prompt shown\".</p> <p>Some services cannot be blocked</p> <p>A service that shares an address with another service cannot be blocked at that address. The row says \"Block not available\".</p> <p>Upload blocking is proven for four services only</p> <p>A Block on Images, Documents or Other files is proven to stop the upload for ChatGPT, Gemini (web), Perplexity and Qwen. For other services the screen says \"not enforced for this service yet\", and a Block there may stop nothing. Test each service you depend on.</p>"},{"location":"admin/fleet/policies.html#document-types","title":"Document types","text":"<p>The card Document types sets what a computer does when something a person sends to an AI service looks like one of these kinds of document: a non-disclosure agreement, a master services agreement, a statement of work, a commercial contract, a request for proposal or tender, a proposal or estimate, a price list or rate card, source code, a technical design or specification, a security report, a financial report, and more. It is on the organisation default, on group policies and on policies for people, not on a policy for one computer.</p> <p></p> <p>For each kind of document you choose one Action:</p> Action What a computer does Inherit Takes the value from the organisation default (on a group policy) Allow Sends it Allow and record the type Sends it, and records the kind of document Replace personal data Replaces the personal data in it, as the modes do Warn Would ask the person. No app asks yet, so a computer treats a Warn as a Block Block Does not send it <p>Below the table:</p> <p></p> <ul> <li>Enforce document rules. Off by default. While it is off, computers only record what each rule would do and change nothing for the person. Personal data is still handled by the policy's modes.</li> <li>If the text was read but its type could not be checked. Allow or Block. A file whose text cannot be read at all, such as a zip, an encrypted file or a scanned image, follows the organisation's document inspection setting instead.</li> <li>Your organisation's own names. The legal entities and brands of your organisation, on the organisation default only. A document that names a party that is not on this list counts as involving an outside party, such as a client, a vendor or a partner. With no names, a computer cannot tell an outside party from your own organisation.</li> <li>Use the Outsourcing / professional services template. Sets contracts, NDAs, tenders, price lists, source code, technical designs, security reports, financial reports, personnel records and strategy papers to Block, proposals to Replace personal data, and content that could not be checked to Block. It starts with Enforce off, so nothing is blocked until you turn enforcement on. Remove document rules (on a group policy, Stop setting document rules here) takes the rules away.</li> <li>A line says how many computers apply the rules, for example \"Not published yet: no device has these document rules\", or \"N of M devices\". Only computers on an app that supports document rules apply them.</li> </ul> <p>Limits of document rules</p> <ul> <li>Microsoft Copilot (web) and Copilot in the desktop Office apps are not covered. A document rule cannot block a Copilot chat message or a document uploaded to Copilot.</li> <li>Which kinds of document a computer recognises depends on its app version. The card lists the kinds that are \"Not recognised yet\". Test the rules with your own documents before you turn enforcement on.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"admin/fleet/policies.html#keywords","title":"Keywords","text":"<p>The card Keywords lists words and phrases that are replaced in every mode, including Record only.</p> <p></p> <ul> <li>On the organisation default there is one list, Words. On a group policy there are two: Adds and Takes out.</li> <li>Type a word or phrase and press Enter, or select Add. A keyword has 2 to 100 characters and at least one letter or digit. Upper and lower case count as the same. A list holds at most 200 keywords.</li> <li>A keyword matches as a whole word, in what people type, in tool results and in the text of attached files. It is replaced by a placeholder. The records say that a keyword matched, never which word.</li> <li>The words of a group policy start from the organisation default's list. Every group's Adds join the list and every group's Takes out leave it. A removal by any group beats an add by another.</li> <li>A computer that does not report a policy version does not apply the list.</li> <li>Requests to the Anthropic API do not read a search result's source and title yet. The record says that the attachment was not inspected.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"admin/fleet/policies.html#ai-coding-agents","title":"AI coding agents","text":"<p>The card AI coding agents sets which MCP servers and skills Claude Code and Codex may use on your computers. It is not offered on a policy for one computer or for people.</p> <p></p> <p>For each tool you set Enforcement:</p> Enforcement What happens Report only (the default) Nothing is blocked. The person is told about each MCP server or skill the policy does not allow Remove these MCP servers The app takes the blocked MCP servers out of the person's own settings and puts them back once they are no longer blocked. On Claude Code the system service also lists them as blocked in the managed settings, which the person cannot change. On Codex the person can turn them back on Only allowed MCP servers can run The system service writes the allowed list into the managed settings of the tool <p>The lists are Allowed MCP servers, Blocked MCP servers, Allowed skills and Blocked skills. An MCP server is added By URL (<code>https://mcp.example.com/*</code>, where one <code>*</code> stands for any run of characters) or By command (one part per line, an exact match), with a Name. A skill is added by name. A list holds at most 256 entries.</p> <p>Limits of this card</p> <ul> <li>It governs Claude Code and Codex only. Cursor, VS Code, Windsurf and Claude Desktop are listed in Dev Tools but not governed here.</li> <li>The managed settings are written only on computers where the product's system service is installed. Without it the app can only report, and remove servers from the person's own settings, which the person can undo.</li> <li>Codex has no managed block list. Allowed skills are \"Not checked on Codex devices yet\". Claude Code has no managed list of allowed skills.</li> <li>A blocked list is the union of all groups' lists. A group never lifts a block.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"admin/fleet/policies.html#presets","title":"Presets","text":"<p>The card Start from a policy preset fills in a policy in one step. Everything it sets stays editable. Use General or an Industry preset for the organisation default, and a Role preset for a group policy. It is not offered on a policy for one computer or for people.</p> Group Presets General Monitor prompts, mask files Industry Banking, Healthcare, Legal, Public sector Role Business analysts, Sales, Accountants, Developers, Customer support, HR, Executives, Contractors <p>A preset sets Text mode, File mode and data types, and it can also set blocked services, upload controls and keywords. For example Contractors sets both modes to Block, and Executives sets both to Replace.</p> <p>Choose a preset to see What applying it changes in this policy, line by line. Select Apply preset. The screen then says that the preset is applied to this draft and that you must publish to send it to devices. Nothing is sent until you publish.</p>"},{"location":"admin/fleet/policies.html#how-the-policies-of-a-computer-combine","title":"How the policies of a computer combine","text":"<p>For one computer and one AI service, the result comes from these layers, from the top:</p> <ol> <li>A policy for the person who is signed in on the computer, if one names them. See Policies for people.</li> <li>The policies of the groups the computer is directly in. For Access, Mode, Text mode, File mode and each upload control, the strictest value among the matching policies wins: Block beats Replace, which beats Record only, and Block beats Allow. Each of Text mode and File mode is worked out on its own.</li> <li>The organisation default.</li> <li>The product default: Allow, and Record only.</li> </ol> <p>Within a policy, a row for the service beats that policy's Default for all services row.</p> <p>Data types, document types, keywords, AI coding agents and advanced rules combine in their own ways, described above and in Policy rules. A group policy overrides the organisation default even when it is looser. Only groups the computer is in directly count. A computer in no group gets the organisation default.</p>"},{"location":"admin/fleet/policies.html#test-publish-and-roll-out","title":"Test, publish and roll out","text":""},{"location":"admin/fleet/policies.html#the-tester","title":"The tester","text":"<p>Select Tester to see what a computer gets and why. It runs in your browser and makes no call to the server.</p> <p></p> <p>Choose a Device group (\"None: organisation default only\" for a computer in no group), a Service, and Policies to test, for example \"The policies publishing would create\" before the first publish. The table Result shows the rows Access, If no one can be asked, Mode, Text mode, File mode, Images, Documents and Other files, with a \"Stricter wins\" badge where two policies met, and a Keywords block. It says \"Allowed: shared host, cannot be blocked\" or \"Allowed: block not enforced for this service yet\" instead of a false Block. The tester leaves out policies for people.</p>"},{"location":"admin/fleet/policies.html#publish","title":"Publish","text":"<p>A bar at the bottom shows \"N unpublished changes\". Publishing creates the next version. Computers get it at their next check. The server keeps one version of the set: there is no list of old versions and no restore. The admin log names the version and the policies in it.</p> <p>If you open the policy list with an old copy, publishing is refused with \"Refresh to load v, then make your change again.\" Only an <code>admin</code> or <code>super-admin</code> can publish."},{"location":"admin/fleet/policies.html#roll-out","title":"Roll out","text":"<p>The column Running and the rollout card count the computers by the version they report. A computer is counted only when it reports back the new version. Lifting a service block, or deleting a rule, reaches computers at their next check.</p>"},{"location":"admin/fleet/policies.html#what-a-computer-enforces","title":"What a computer enforces","text":"Part What a computer does Access The agent shows a block page for a blocked service Mode, Text mode, File mode Enforced when the computer reports a policy version Keywords Replaced in every mode Uploads Enforced where the computer can see the upload, and proven for four services Document types Applied by computers on an app that supports them. With Enforce document rules off they only record. A Warn is carried out as Block AI coding agents Report, notify and edit the person's own settings. Managed settings need the system service <p>Record only is not completely hands-off</p> <p>In Record only, the request is sent unchanged apart from machine secrets and the organisation's keywords, which are still replaced.</p> <p>Block and a search question in a web address</p> <p>A search question that an app sends in a web address, such as Google AI Mode or the Brave Search API, is replaced and sent. It is not refused, even in Block.</p> <p>Computers that do not report a policy version get an approximation: the mode and data types of the Default for all services row. They use one mode for text and files and do not apply keywords. The list says per group which services such computers can still refuse.</p>"},{"location":"admin/fleet/policies.html#policies-for-people","title":"Policies for people","text":"<p>A policy can name people and user groups in Applies to and Except. The list then reads, for example, \"Applies to the people in Interns (4 people), and 2 people named one by one\".</p> <ul> <li>The console cannot create or change people or user groups. A policy for people reaches the server only through the API (<code>/api/v2/admin/tenants/&lt;workspace&gt;/policies</code> with the people in <code>appliesTo</code>, and <code>/user-groups</code> for the groups). The console shows such a policy, with its name and how many computers can apply it.</li> <li>A person is the operating-system sign-in name that the computer reports.</li> <li>A policy for people can set Access, Mode, Text mode, File mode, upload controls, document types and keywords. It cannot set data types, advanced rules or AI coding agents.</li> <li>A person's policy applies on any enrolled computer where they sign in, once that computer has reported them. Only computers whose app applies policies for people do this. The Running column says \"X of Y devices apply policies for people\". For each control, the stricter of the person's value and the computer's value wins. If only one sets it, that one wins, even if it is looser.</li> <li>The server keeps a list of the sign-in names that computers report. See What the workspace records.</li> </ul> <p>Sign-in names</p> <p>Policies for people use the names the computers report, and the server stores them whatever you set for who audit records name.</p>"},{"location":"admin/fleet/settings.html","title":"Settings","text":"<p>The server sends two kinds of instructions to the agents.</p> <ul> <li>Settings are values in sections such as <code>firewall</code>, <code>dlp</code> and <code>secrets</code>. You change them on the Config tab of a workspace. This page covers them.</li> <li>Policies say, for each AI service, whether it is allowed, what happens to data, and which uploads are allowed. You change them on the Policies page. See Policies.</li> </ul> <p>Both reach a computer on its next check. An <code>admin</code> or <code>super-admin</code> can change them. For anyone else the buttons look active, but the server refuses.</p>"},{"location":"admin/fleet/settings.html#protection-modes","title":"Protection modes","text":"Word on screen What the agent does when it finds sensitive data Record only Sends the request unchanged and records what it found. Machine secrets and the organisation's keywords are still replaced Replace Swaps each detected value for a placeholder Block Does not send the request. A prompt or upload with a detection is stopped as a whole, unless its only findings are the organisation's keywords, which are replaced, or it is a search question that an app sends in a web address, which is replaced and sent <p>Block stops the whole request</p> <p>In Block, a prompt or file upload with any detection is refused as a whole, and nothing else in it is replaced. The one exception is the organisation's keywords, which are replaced. The employee sees \"Request blocked \u2014 PII detected\" or \"File upload blocked \u2014 PII detected\".</p> <p>A new workspace starts in Record only. In the settings the three modes are called <code>monitor</code>, <code>mask</code> and <code>protect</code>.</p>"},{"location":"admin/fleet/settings.html#the-config-tab","title":"The Config tab","text":"<p>Open Settings &gt; Tenants, pick the workspace in Tenant, and open the tab Config. A page has these tabs: Overview, Config, Policy, Config history, Devices, Groups, Tokens, Audit and Capture.</p> <p></p> <p></p> <p>The picture shows a test workspace. Names, addresses and IDs are examples.</p> <ul> <li>The scope at the top is Tenant default or Group: name. A group's settings apply only to computers directly in the group. See how groups are used.</li> <li>The list Sections is on the left. Each section is a card with its fields. A Changed badge marks a field you changed, and Clear removes it. Changes stay on the page until you publish them.</li> <li>Validate and Publish appear only after you change a field. Select Validate. When it says \"Looks good\", Publish (in the bar at the bottom, Publish config) becomes active. It sends the whole config with your changes laid over it. If someone else published meanwhile, you see \"Refresh to see the latest config, then re-apply your changes\".</li> <li>After publishing, the page shows how many computers acknowledged the new version, for example \"Published v7 \u00b7 all 12 devices acked\". It waits up to 60 seconds over the first 200 computers.</li> <li>If you leave the Config or Policy tab with changes that are not saved, a window asks Save your changes? with Cancel, Discard and Save. The Policies page does not ask.</li> </ul> <p>The server refuses a config bigger than 64 KB. It checks only the values you changed, so an old value that is wrong does not block an unrelated change.</p>"},{"location":"admin/fleet/settings.html#config-history","title":"Config history","text":"<p>The tab Config history shows every version of the workspace config, who made it and when.</p> <p></p> <p>Select a version to compare it with the one before. Download CSV and Download JSON export the list. Rollback to this version makes a new version that is identical to the old one, and you can add a comment that goes into the admin log. After a rollback the page opens the Config tab. History covers the workspace config only, not groups or policies.</p>"},{"location":"admin/fleet/settings.html#what-each-setting-does-on-this-product","title":"What each setting does on this product","text":"<p>The editor shows more sections than the agent uses. These are the ones this product reads.</p> Section What it sets <code>firewall</code> <code>mode</code> (the three modes above), <code>enabled</code>, <code>pausedUntil</code> (pauses protection until a time, then it comes back by itself), <code>neverRedact</code> and <code>customExclusions</code> (text never to replace), <code>bypassDomains</code>, <code>extraTargetDomains</code>, <code>pseudonymize</code>, and for pictures <code>ocrMode</code> and <code>ocrLanguage</code> <code>dlp</code> <code>modelId</code> (the detection model) and <code>enabledTypes</code> (the types of data to look for) <code>recognizers</code> Which countries' national identifiers computers look for. See below <code>customDlpPatterns</code> Your own patterns. See below <code>dlpThresholds</code> How sure a detector must be, per type <code>requestTimeout</code> How long to wait for an AI service <code>secrets</code> Secret scanning: <code>enabled</code> and the kinds to look for <code>documentInspection</code> How deep to look inside files and archives <code>traceContent</code> What the workspace keeps of conversations: <code>metadata</code>, <code>masked</code> or <code>full</code>. See What the workspace records <code>auditIdentity</code> Whether audit records name the person. See What the workspace records <code>capture</code> The counters and request details the agents send. See What the workspace records <code>runtimeLogs</code> Agent log lines for the device page: <code>enabled</code>, <code>minLevel</code>, <code>sampleRate</code> <code>resilience</code> What AI traffic does when protection is down: \"Block AI traffic until protection is back\" or \"Let AI traffic through, unprotected\" <code>steering</code>, <code>toolSteering</code> Which command-line AI tools are sent through protection <code>aiClientCatalog</code> Extra AI apps to recognise <code>updater</code> <code>autoUpdateEnabled</code>, <code>forceVersion</code> <code>policy</code> Advanced rules. See Policy rules <p>These sections are for another product and do nothing here: <code>userRiskProfiling</code>, <code>evalCapture</code> and <code>deviceKeyRotation</code>. Do not turn on <code>learningMode</code> or <code>evalCapture</code> expecting a feature: current agents record no prompt because of them. The setting <code>updater.silentInstall</code> also does nothing: every update still asks the person to confirm. Inside <code>firewall</code>, the fields <code>otelEndpoint</code>, <code>port</code>, <code>targetDomains</code> and <code>learningMode</code> are ignored.</p> <p><code>recognizers</code>. The field <code>countries</code> names the countries whose national identifiers (for example tax and ID numbers) computers look for. If the section is absent, computers look for every country. Only identifier checks are narrowed: names, e-mail addresses, phone numbers, card numbers and bank accounts are always checked. The help text says to \"Leave empty to look for every country\", but the server refuses an empty list. To look for every country, remove the section.</p> <p>Files that cannot be read are allowed by default</p> <p><code>documentInspection.uninspectableAction</code> is allow unless you set it, so a file that the agent cannot read is sent on. Set it to <code>block</code> yourself if you want such files to be refused.</p>"},{"location":"admin/fleet/settings.html#what-a-computer-does-before-it-reaches-the-server","title":"What a computer does before it reaches the server","text":"<p>After a restart, before its first check, a computer remembers only the protection mode the organisation set last. Everything else the organisation set starts again from the built-in values until the first check: the lists of text never to replace, the types of data, the policy rules, the thresholds and the secrets setting. A pause lapses too, so a paused computer protects again until it checks in. A computer with no server at all uses the built-in values: Record only and 20 types of data. A workspace starts with 17. Those two lists differ a little.</p>"},{"location":"admin/fleet/settings.html#policy-rules","title":"Policy rules","text":"<p>Policy rules are advanced rules for a workspace or a group. Open the tab Policy.</p> <p></p> <p>A rule has an id, a priority (lower runs first), an action (<code>pass</code>, <code>mask</code>, <code>block</code>, <code>reroute</code> or <code>log</code>), a reason shown to the person on a block, and conditions. The first rule that matches wins. Evaluate tests rules in the browser. Revert and Save rules appear after a change.</p> <p>When the workspace has published policies, the rules on the Whole workspace scope are the organisation default's advanced rules. Save rules then publishes a new version of the policies, and the page says \"Published policies v8 \u00b7 3 rules\". A policy of a group or a computer that sets its own rules gets those instead. A blocked service or upload is refused before any rule runs. If the page was loaded from an older version, saving is refused: \"Nothing was saved: this tenant has published policies (v8), and this page did not load its rules from them. Refresh, then make your change again.\"</p> <p>On the Config tab, the <code>policy</code> section shows a summary with the button Open policy builder, and Edit as JSON.</p> Part of a rule What a computer does <code>block</code>, <code>mask</code> Works in Replace and Block. In Record only the rule only records <code>pass</code> Changes nothing <code>log</code> Sends the request unchanged, even in Replace and Block. The name suggests logging, but the original text leaves the computer <code>reroute</code> Not carried out. The rule acts as if it had not matched, and the rules below it are not checked. The portal says so Conditions on domain, types of data and minimum size Work Conditions on departments and file types Never match. The portal marks them \"Not enforced on devices yet\" The switch Audit-capture on match Ignored. Nothing extra is recorded <p>A rule that is malformed makes the computer refuse the whole push and keep the last good rules. A group's rules saved here, while policies are published, can be overwritten by the published set, and the page shows a notice.</p>"},{"location":"admin/fleet/settings.html#text-that-is-never-replaced","title":"Text that is never replaced","text":"<p><code>neverRedact</code> lists text that stays in a request even when a detector matches it. Put one value on each line. <code>*.example.com</code> keeps a whole domain, and any other <code>*</code> is refused. <code>customExclusions</code> is a second list that works the same way. Only the organisation can set these lists. A person cannot add to them on their own computer.</p> <p><code>extraTargetDomains</code> adds AI services to inspect. <code>bypassDomains</code> turns inspection off for a service.</p>"},{"location":"admin/fleet/settings.html#custom-patterns","title":"Custom patterns","text":"<p><code>customDlpPatterns</code> lets you add your own patterns, for example an internal project code. Select + Add pattern. A pattern has an id, a name, a type (a prefix of your own that must not equal a built-in type), the pattern itself, flags and a severity from 0 to 100. Use Test against fixture to try it on sample text.</p> <p>The portal refuses a pattern that does not compile. It also flags back-references, look-ahead and look-behind, because the agent's pattern engine does not support them. A pattern can be 500 characters long. A bad pattern is skipped and logged. A new list replaces the old, and an empty list clears it.</p>"},{"location":"admin/fleet/what-is-recorded.html","title":"What the workspace records","text":"<p>Protection happens on the employee's computer. The agent checks each request there, before it leaves. The private values that it finds are not collected: the agent replaces them on the computer before anything is sent to the workspace.</p> <p>The workspace does receive a record, and you should tell your employees what it contains. This page lists it in plain words, for version 2.2.8.</p>"},{"location":"admin/fleet/what-is-recorded.html#what-reaches-the-workspace","title":"What reaches the workspace","text":"What Where you see it How long Notes Audit events. One for each request the agent scanned, including requests where it found nothing: the time, what it did (<code>pass</code>, <code>log</code>, <code>mask</code>, <code>block</code>), the host and path, the types of data found, the computer, and the size Audit, tab Events 90 days Each event carries a preview. See The preview Conversations. The messages the employee sent, with the detected values replaced Audit, tab Conversations 30 days Only when Trace content is <code>full</code>, which is the default. See How much of a conversation is kept A status report about every 30 seconds: host name, operating-system user name, machine ID, versions, protection state, counters Devices, the device page Updated as it arrives See Who the audit record names and The status report and the list of sign-in names Counters. How many requests were checked, how they were handled, how long scanning took, and how the two detectors agreed AI Activity, Tenants &gt; Capture, the device tab Capture No message text Agent log lines The device tab Logs Not stated On for a new workspace, at level <code>info</code>. A partial filter removes e-mail addresses, phone numbers, card numbers and file names. Host names and error messages go as written Developer tools. The tools found on the computer: their configuration file paths, the commands and arguments that start them, the names (never the values) of related environment variables, and risk flags Dashboards &gt; Dev Tools The paths start in the user's home folder, so they contain the operating-system account name. There is no switch to turn this off. The report can also carry a verdict on which MCP servers and skills your policy does not allow. See AI coding agents What administrators changed: who, when, which action Tenants &gt; Audit Not stated See Audit and evidence"},{"location":"admin/fleet/what-is-recorded.html#the-status-report-and-the-list-of-sign-in-names","title":"The status report and the list of sign-in names","text":"<p>Every enrolled computer sends a status report about every 30 seconds. It carries the host name, the operating-system user name, a machine ID, versions, the protection state and counters. The device page shows the latest report.</p> <p>The server also keeps a list of the sign-in names that computers report in the workspace, with when each name was first and last seen on each computer. It uses the list for policies for people. The server writes the list whatever you set for who audit records name, and nothing deletes it. The portal has no screen for it. It is available only through the API.</p>"},{"location":"admin/fleet/what-is-recorded.html#who-the-audit-record-names","title":"Who the audit record names","text":"<p>An audit event carries either the person or only the computer. The setting is <code>auditIdentity.employeeNoticeAcknowledged</code> on the Config tab.</p> <ul> <li>Off (the default for a new workspace). The event names the computer, as <code>device:&lt;device ID&gt;</code>, and no person. The portal chip on Overview reads Audit: device only.</li> <li>On. The event carries the operating-system user name of the person who was signed in. The chip reads Audit: person named.</li> </ul> <p>Turn it on only when your organisation has told its employees, and its works council where it has one, that audit records name them. The setting is your statement, not a switch the agent can change.</p> <p>This setting is read by agents from 2.2.8. Older agents named the person whenever the computer was enrolled, and rows they wrote stay as they were for up to 90 days.</p> <p>Even with the setting off, the workspace knows the user name</p> <p>Device only is not anonymous. Every enrolled computer sends its operating-system user name and its host name in its status report, and the device page shows them as OS user and Hostname. The people who can open the Devices page can see which user is on which computer. Say this in your privacy notice.</p> <p>Because of the setting, these screens look different when it is off:</p> <ul> <li>The User column in Audit is empty.</li> <li>Users, Risk and the Person grouping in Conversations show nothing for people. The Users page may explain the empty list by saying that older agents do not send the user e-mail yet. With this setting off, the setting is the reason.</li> <li>Alerts about people name the computer instead.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"admin/fleet/what-is-recorded.html#the-preview","title":"The preview","text":"<p>Each audit event carries a preview: the first 200 characters of the message, with the values the agent detected replaced by a tag such as <code>[EMAIL]</code>, and about 50 characters of context around each detection. It does not show the detected value.</p> <p>The preview is only as good as the detectors</p> <p>The agent replaces a value only where a detector found it. A name, an address or a number that no detector recognised stays readable in the preview, to everyone who can open Audit. The same holds for a find below a detector's cutoff (see Detection and thresholds).</p> <p>Setting Trace content to <code>metadata</code> removes the preview and the context from audit events.</p>"},{"location":"admin/fleet/what-is-recorded.html#how-much-of-a-conversation-is-kept","title":"How much of a conversation is kept","text":"<p>The setting Trace content, field <code>mode</code>, applies to the whole workspace. A group or a single computer can only make it narrower.</p> Level What the workspace keeps full (the default when nothing is set) The messages the employee sent, with detected values replaced, in Conversations masked No conversations. The 200-character preview stays in the audit events metadata No conversations and no preview: only the types and counts <p>In <code>masked</code> and <code>metadata</code>, the agent does not send conversations at all, and Conversations says that the workspace keeps no conversations. A change is not applied to what was stored before.</p> <p>The reply of the AI service is never sent. Conversations shows only the employee's side. Earlier replies that a later request carries as history are included in that request, so they are recorded with it.</p> <p>A conversation message is labelled with how it was recorded:</p> <ul> <li>\"As it left the device \u2014 detected values already replaced.\"</li> <li>\"A copy for the record, with detected values replaced. The AI service may have received some of these values.\"</li> <li>\"Not recorded. This message was not fully scanned, so its text was not kept in this record. The AI service still received it.\"</li> <li>\"Not recorded. This message could not be recorded with its detected values replaced\u2026\"</li> </ul>"},{"location":"admin/fleet/what-is-recorded.html#what-is-not-stored","title":"What is not stored","text":"<ul> <li>The detected values. The portal has no \"show the original\".</li> <li>The full prompt. It stays on the computer.</li> <li>The reply of the AI service.</li> <li>The content of images. For an image, Audit shows a redaction map only (\"Image not retained\").</li> <li>File names of attachments. A file is shown as <code>[document].&lt;extension&gt;</code>, with its type and size. A file name with an extension the product does not know shows only as <code>[document]</code>. Query strings are dropped from addresses.</li> <li>File hashes.</li> <li>The words of your keyword list. A record says that a keyword matched, never which word.</li> </ul> <p>Be careful with two things: file names are replaced in the path of an upload, but the path of a document on the device appears in the developer-tool list as described above, and agents older than 2.2.6 could keep raw text and file hashes. Those rows age out after 90 days.</p>"},{"location":"admin/fleet/what-is-recorded.html#detection-and-thresholds","title":"Detection and thresholds","text":"<p>The agent has two kinds of detector.</p> <ul> <li>The name detector (for person names, organisations, addresses): a find below its cutoff is dropped. It is not replaced and is not recorded anywhere. The cutoff depends on the model. For the default model it is 0 for person names and 0.5 for most others, not a general 80%. You can set it per type in <code>dlpThresholds</code>.</li> <li>Patterns (for example card numbers): a find below 0.4 is recorded, with its type and its tag in the preview, but it is not replaced or blocked in Replace and Block. The original value is sent on to the AI service.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"admin/fleet/what-is-recorded.html#what-is-not-covered-by-a-switch","title":"What is not covered by a switch","text":"<p>There is no setting that makes an agent send the text of a prompt to the server. The <code>learningMode</code> and <code>evalCapture</code> switches that the editor still shows record no prompt on current versions.</p> <p>Request counts and details such as host and path, without any text, can be sent for the Capture tab. That is off by default: it needs <code>capture.metadataEnabled</code>.</p>"},{"location":"admin/fleet/what-is-recorded.html#what-the-server-itself-fetches","title":"What the server itself fetches","text":"<p>By default the server downloads public vulnerability advisory data (the OSV lists for npm and Python packages) about a minute after it starts and then once a day. It is a download only. The setting ADVISORY_REFRESH=off on the server stops all contact and uses only the files already in its folder. If your servers must not reach the internet, set it.</p>"},{"location":"admin/fleet/what-is-recorded.html#limits-of-the-screens-that-use-this-data","title":"Limits of the screens that use this data","text":"<ul> <li>Users, Risk and Reports are worked out from the newest 500 audit events of the workspace, whatever window you choose, and the page gives no warning. On a busy workspace a 30-day or 90-day report covers much less than its title. Do not present them as complete.</li> <li>Risk is not a score out of 100. It adds 5 for each block, 2 for each mask and 0.5 for each log.</li> <li>Audit shows the last 90 days. There is no export on the Events tab.</li> <li>The agent log lines and the counters that the server keeps are meant to be deleted after 30 and 90 days, but nothing on the server does it. Treat their retention as not enforced. Ask your ScienceSoft representative.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"admin/fleet/workspaces.html","title":"Workspaces","text":"<p>A workspace holds one organisation's computers, settings, keys and records. Setup creates the first one, named <code>main</code> unless you chose another name. Most servers need only that one.</p> <p>In the portal, workspaces are under Settings &gt; Tenants. Pick one in the list Tenant at the top of the page. The page title is the name of the workspace. A workspace has nine tabs: Overview, Config, Policy, Config history, Devices, Groups, Tokens, Audit and Capture. The tab you are on is part of the address, so a reload or the browser's Back button keeps it.</p>"},{"location":"admin/fleet/workspaces.html#create-a-workspace","title":"Create a workspace","text":"<p>Only a <code>super-admin</code> can create a workspace. On Tenants, select + New.</p> <p></p> <p>The picture shows a test workspace. Names, addresses and IDs are examples.</p> Field What to enter Workspace name A short name in lower case with digits and hyphens, for example <code>finance</code>. It cannot be changed or deleted later. A name that exists already is refused. Region A label. Pick any value. The server does not use it to choose where data is kept, and nothing changes with it. Fleet endpoint The address the computers report to. The portal fills in your own server address. Keep it. Device limit (optional) Leave empty for no limit. See Device limit. <p>Select Provision. If it fails, the message starts with \"Provision failed\".</p> <p>A workspace cannot be deleted</p> <p>There is no way to delete a workspace in the portal. You can suspend it.</p>"},{"location":"admin/fleet/workspaces.html#what-a-new-workspace-starts-with","title":"What a new workspace starts with","text":"<p>A new workspace gets a starting set of settings, chosen once, when it is created. Your server uses the set called <code>production-monitoring</code>. Changing the set later does not change workspaces that exist.</p> Setting Starting value Protection mode Record only: the agent records what it finds and changes nothing Types of data 17: person names, e-mail addresses, phone numbers, social security numbers, card numbers, IP addresses, web addresses, passport numbers, driver licence numbers, ID card numbers, tax numbers, dates of birth, addresses, credentials, IBAN, routing numbers and bank account numbers Secrets On, for five kinds of secret Agent log lines On, at level <code>info</code> Who audit records name The device only, not the person. See What the workspace records Automatic updates On <p>Change these on the Config tab and on the Policies page. Move to Replace when you are ready for the agents to change what leaves the computer.</p>"},{"location":"admin/fleet/workspaces.html#the-overview-tab","title":"The Overview tab","text":"<p>Overview shows the status of the workspace, counters for devices and tokens, and a chip that says who audit records name. Select the chip to open the setting.</p> <p></p> <p>The picture shows a test workspace. Names, addresses and IDs are examples.</p> <p>The chip reads Audit: person named or Audit: device only. It tells you only what the audit records carry. Every enrolled computer still reports its operating-system user name and host name to the server, and the device page shows them. See What the workspace records.</p>"},{"location":"admin/fleet/workspaces.html#device-limit","title":"Device limit","text":"<p>A device limit is meant for a pilot. Type a number to stop the workspace accepting more computers than that.</p> <ul> <li>A workspace with a limit becomes a trial workspace with no end date. A Trial panel then appears on Overview.</li> <li>A computer counts toward the limit if it is not revoked and was seen in the last 30 days. A computer that is uninstalled still counts until it has been silent for 30 days, or you revoke it.</li> <li>When the limit is full, the next computer is refused. It keeps protecting its user on its own, as a standalone app.</li> <li>To lift the limit, select Convert to a paid workspace in the Trial panel. This ends the limit and the trial period. Computers keep working. An <code>admin</code> of the workspace can do this.</li> </ul> <p>There is no control in the portal to set an end date or to change the limit later. Ask your ScienceSoft representative.</p>"},{"location":"admin/fleet/workspaces.html#suspend-a-workspace","title":"Suspend a workspace","text":"<p>On Overview, Suspend tenant and Unsuspend tenant stop and start new joins. An <code>admin</code> of the workspace can do both. There is no confirmation question.</p> <p>Suspending does not stop computers that have already joined</p> <p>A suspended workspace refuses only new computers. Computers that have joined keep reporting, receiving settings and being protected. No block-all configuration is sent to them. To stop one computer, revoke it and revoke the keys that could bring it back.</p>"},{"location":"admin/fleet/workspaces.html#who-can-sign-in","title":"Who can sign in","text":"<p>Overview has a panel Who can sign in. It lists the people who can open this workspace and checks each one.</p> Badge Meaning Ready The person can sign in and sees this workspace Sees whole fleet The person is in the group <code>fleet-operators</code> and sees every workspace. Give this only to people you trust with all of them Needs attention Something is missing: no account, no workspace attribute, no role, or the profile has no first or last name. The row says what <p>Add a login needs a <code>super-admin</code> and a server that is allowed to create accounts. Fill in e-mail, first name, last name and Access (<code>admin</code>, <code>analyst</code> or <code>auditor</code>), then select Create login. The server makes a one-time password and shows it once. The person must change it at the first sign-in. If the server cannot create accounts, the panel says so and shows the steps to follow in the sign-in service. Then select Check to confirm. See Sign-in and roles for the same steps in the sign-in service.</p> <p>Other actions on a row are Disable, Re-enable and Reset password. A disabled person is signed out at once, but pages that are already open can keep working for up to 15 minutes.</p>"},{"location":"admin/fleet/workspaces.html#licence-and-seats","title":"Licence and seats","text":"<p>Settings &gt; License shows the licence of the whole server, not of one workspace.</p> <p></p> <p>The picture shows a test server. Names, the licence ID and the fingerprint are examples.</p> Shown Meaning Active, In grace, Expired, No license The state of the licence. In grace lasts 7 days after the end date Seats Computers counted against the licence, with the percentage used. Healthy is below 90%, Approaching cap from 90% to 100%, Over cap above 100% Expiry date and fingerprint When it ends, and the first characters of its fingerprint <p>Seats are computers that are not revoked and were seen in the last 30 days, counted across all workspaces of the server.</p> <ul> <li>When the licence has run out or the seats are full, new computers cannot join. The refused computer works on its own and says so. Computers that have joined keep working.</li> <li>At exactly 100% new computers are already refused, even though the screen still says \"Approaching cap\".</li> <li>Over the seat count, every computer of the server shows the notice \"License device limit reached\". The notice says the computer keeps protecting its user and is still reported to your workspace, and that your IT team can free a device or extend the licence. It shows no numbers.</li> <li>A new licence is read when the server starts. There is no way to upload one in the portal. To renew, run <code>aifw setup</code> again on the server and give the path of the new <code>.license</code> file (see Install the server), then check the License page.</li> <li>A licence dated after the server's clock lets the server start, with a warning, but refuses new computers until the clock is right (use NTP) and the control plane is restarted. A missing, changed or wrong-key licence still stops the server.</li> </ul> <p>When the licence has a problem, the Devices screen shows a banner with the reason.</p>"},{"location":"admin/server/index.html","title":"Set up your self-hosted server","text":"<p>The server holds your policy, the list of computers, the audit records and the admin portal. You install and run it on one Linux server that you own. This part of the guide takes you from an empty Ubuntu server to a working installation, and then shows how to keep it healthy.</p>"},{"location":"admin/server/index.html#what-this-part-covers","title":"What this part covers","text":"Page Read it to learn Server requirements What you need before you start: the server, the network, and what you receive from us. Install the server Step by step: from an empty Ubuntu server to a working ScienceSoft AI Endpoint Firewall. The installer script Where to get <code>install.sh</code>, and how to use it. aifw commands Every <code>aifw</code> command with example output: setup, status, doctor, backup, upgrade. Reverse proxy How to put your own load balancer or proxy in front of the server. Sign-in and roles The first sign-in, who can do what, and the sign-in service console. Single sign-on Letting people sign in with your company directory, for example Microsoft Entra ID. Operate and upgrade Checking health, upgrades and rolling back. Backup and restore What to back up, how, and how to bring the server back. Troubleshooting What to do when the installation or sign-in does not work."},{"location":"admin/server/index.html#the-installation-at-a-glance","title":"The installation at a glance","text":"<ol> <li>Get the server ready and the network open (see Server requirements).</li> <li>Run the installer script <code>install.sh</code>. It installs Docker and the <code>aifw</code> command.</li> <li>Run <code>aifw preflight</code>, then <code>aifw setup</code>. Setup asks a few questions and does the rest.</li> <li>Save the sign-in details that setup shows, then sign in to the admin portal.</li> </ol> <p>It takes about 20 minutes on a prepared server.</p> <p>When the server runs, continue with Roll out the agent.</p>"},{"location":"admin/server/aifw-reference.html","title":"<code>aifw</code> command reference","text":"<p><code>aifw</code> installs and operates the whole server. It is the only supported way to install the server. The sequence is:</p> <pre><code>sudo bash install.sh     # installs the aifw command (see \"The installer script\")\nsudo aifw setup          # installs and configures the server\n</code></pre> <p>Find your command in the summary table, then read its section. Every command is run with <code>sudo</code>.</p>"},{"location":"admin/server/aifw-reference.html#before-you-start","title":"Before you start","text":"<ul> <li>Access: SSH to the server, and an account that can use <code>sudo</code>.</li> <li>The <code>aifw</code> command is installed: <code>install.sh</code> has finished with \"Next step: sudo aifw setup\". See The installer script.</li> <li>The registry is reachable: <code>versions</code>, <code>setup</code> and <code>upgrade</code> need to reach the software registry. The other commands work offline.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"admin/server/aifw-reference.html#summary","title":"Summary","text":"Group Command What it does Changes the server? Before you install or upgrade <code>aifw versions</code> Lists the server versions available to install No Setting up and repairing <code>aifw setup</code> Sets up the server, guided Yes <code>aifw setup --check</code> Shows what <code>setup</code> would do No <code>aifw setup --answers &lt;file&gt;</code> Sets up with no questions, reading answers from a file Yes Moving to a newer version <code>aifw upgrade [--version &lt;v&gt;]</code> Moves the server to a newer version. Backs up first, keeps data Yes Checking a running server <code>aifw status</code> Is everything healthy? No <code>aifw security-audit</code> Checks the security posture No <code>aifw doctor</code> A full check-up with numbered findings and fixes No Backing up <code>aifw backup</code> Backs up the database and settings now Yes (adds files only) The tool itself <code>aifw --version</code> Prints the version of <code>aifw</code> No <code>aifw --help</code> Prints the help (also what you get with no arguments) No <code>aifw preflight</code> The machine checks on their own. <code>setup</code> runs them for you No <p>The marks in the output are the same everywhere: <code>\u2713</code> passed, <code>!</code> a recommendation, <code>\u2717</code> a requirement that is not met.</p>"},{"location":"admin/server/aifw-reference.html#aifw-versions","title":"<code>aifw versions</code>","text":"<p>Lists the versions that the software registry holds, newest first. Changes nothing.</p> <p>A version that is not in the list cannot be installed or upgraded to. If the version you need is missing, it has not been published yet: wait for it, or contact support. Older versions stay in the list and stay installable.</p> <pre><code># aifw versions\nAvailable server versions (newest first):\n2.2.6\n2.2.5\n2.0.75\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"admin/server/aifw-reference.html#aifw-setup","title":"<code>aifw setup</code>","text":"<p>Run this after <code>install.sh</code> has finished. It performs the installation, and it is also the repair command.</p> <p>The order:</p> <ol> <li>The questions. It asks for the server type, the public address, how HTTPS works, the version, whether the emergency token field appears on the sign-in page, the license file, your email, the name of the first workspace, and an optional limit on SSH addresses. It then prints the answers back and asks for a yes. Nothing on the server has changed yet.</li> <li>The machine checks. If a requirement is not met, setup stops and prints what to fix. Nothing has changed yet.</li> <li>The phases, one at a time, printing each step as it runs.</li> <li>The security posture. The checks that did not pass, each with a fix. The installation has already succeeded. These are open items, not failures.</li> </ol> <p>Running it again is safe. Each step looks at the server before it changes anything, so a second run changes only what has drifted. The questions offer your previous answers.</p> <p><code>setup</code> is the only command that configures the sign-in service. When a check says the administrator sign-in failed, run <code>sudo aifw setup</code>. An upgrade does not do this.</p> <p>What a repeat run can and cannot change. A repeat run is safe when your answers are the same. Setup refuses to change the public address, the version or the administrator e-mail of a server that is already set up, because the sign-in service, the database and the enrolled computers already hold those values. It stops and names the setting that differs. To move to a newer version, use <code>aifw upgrade</code>. You can change how HTTPS is handled by running setup again with the new choice. To renew the licence, run setup again and give the path of the new license file; press Enter to keep the installed one.</p> <p>There is no command that changes the public address of a configured server. If you gave a wrong address and have not used the server yet, restore the snapshot that you took before the installation, and run setup again with the right address.</p> <p>Options:</p> <ul> <li><code>--check</code> prints what would change and changes nothing. Use it before a repeat run on a live server.</li> <li><code>--answers &lt;file&gt;</code> runs without questions, reading the answers from a file. See Install the server.</li> </ul> <p>Also: setup turns on the automatic security updates of the system.</p> <p>Example of a first installation on a server that gets its own certificates (the questions come before this and are not shown):</p> <pre><code># aifw setup\nSetting up this server:\nPhase 1 of 8 \u2014 Check the server\nServer check:\n\u2713 Administrator rights\n\u2713 Machine type \u2014 this machine is x86_64\n\u2713 Operating system \u2014 Ubuntu 24.04.5 LTS\n\u2713 Processors \u2014 2 available\n\u2713 Memory (minimum 4 GB) \u2014 7.8 GB installed\n\u2713 Memory (recommended 8 GB) \u2014 7.8 GB installed\n\u2713 Free disk space (minimum 20 GB) \u2014 82 GB free\n\u2713 Service manager (systemd)\n\u2713 Docker installed and running\n\u2713 Docker Compose available\n\u2713 Software registry reachable\n\u2713 Web ports free (80, 443, 8090)\nThis server is ready.\nPhase 2 of 8 \u2014 Prepare the server\n\u2026 Create the installation folders\n\u2713 Create the installation folders \u2014 done\n\u2026 Turn on the firewall (SSH stays allowed)\n\u2713 Turn on the firewall (SSH stays allowed) \u2014 done\n\u2026 Turn on automatic security updates\n\u2713 Turn on automatic security updates \u2014 done\nPhase 4 of 8 \u2014 Configuration and secrets\n\u2026 Confirm the chosen software version exists\n\u2713 Confirm the chosen software version exists \u2014 done\n\u2026 Write the server configuration and secrets\n\u2713 Write the server configuration and secrets \u2014 done\nPhase 5 of 8 \u2014 Download and start\n\u2026 Download the server software images\n\u2713 Download the server software images \u2014 done\n\u2026 Start the server and wait until it is healthy\n\u2713 Start the server and wait until it is healthy \u2014 done\nPhase 6 of 8 \u2014 Secure web access\n\u2026 Start secure web access\n\u2713 Start secure web access \u2014 done\nPhase 7 of 8 \u2014 Sign-in service\n\u2026 Create your admin account\n\u2713 Create your admin account \u2014 done\n\u2026 Secure the built-in sign-in admin password\n\u2713 Secure the built-in sign-in admin password \u2014 done\nPhase 8 of 8 \u2014 Finish and verify\n\u2026 Create your first workspace\n\u2713 Create your first workspace \u2014 done\n\u2026 Create the device enrollment key\n\u2713 Create the device enrollment key \u2014 done\n\u2026 Confirm the server accepts the license\n\u2713 Confirm the server accepts the license \u2014 done\n\u2026 Write your credentials sheet\n\u2713 Write your credentials sheet \u2014 done\n\u2026 Turn on daily backups\n\u2713 Turn on daily backups \u2014 done\n</code></pre> <p>(The example is shortened: a real run prints every step of every phase.)</p>"},{"location":"admin/server/aifw-reference.html#aifw-upgrade","title":"<code>aifw upgrade</code>","text":"<p>Moves the server to a newer version. It takes a backup first, and your data stays.</p> <p>Run it without <code>--version</code> and, in a terminal, a menu lets you pick a version. Without a terminal (a script or a timer) it only names the newest stable version and changes nothing. Run it with <code>--version 2.2.5</code> to take a named one.</p> <p>Four limits:</p> <ol> <li>It cannot reach a version that is not published. Run <code>aifw versions</code> first. If the target is the version already running, it stops with <code>This server is already on &lt;version&gt; \u2014 nothing to do</code>. Nothing was changed.</li> <li>Going back to an older version is guarded. Database changes only move forward, so older software would run against data that a newer version has already changed. In a terminal the command warns you and asks for a yes. Without a terminal, in a script, it refuses. If you must go back, restore the snapshot or the backup that you took before the upgrade.</li> <li>It does not set up sign-in. The server keeps the sign-in setup it had. Run <code>sudo aifw setup</code> for that.</li> <li>It does not move third-party software. The database, the sign-in service, the web front end and the monitoring stay where they are. They move when the product pins newer versions of them.</li> </ol> <p>The run ends with the security posture, the same list that <code>security-audit</code> prints.</p> <p>Upgrade the server before the agents</p> <p>Deploy the server first, or at the same time as the first agent of a new version. An older server drops the parts of a newer agent's data that it does not recognise. It can also report findings that are not findings. Nothing checks the order for you.</p> <p>Two lines are worth recognising:</p> <pre><code>The installer updated itself \u2014 continuing the upgrade with the new version\nThis server is already on &lt;version&gt; \u2014 nothing to do\n</code></pre> <p>The first means the tool replaced its own program and ran once more. That is normal. The second is a success: nothing newer is published.</p>"},{"location":"admin/server/aifw-reference.html#the-upgrade-step-by-step","title":"The upgrade, step by step","text":"<ol> <li>Take a snapshot of the server, or run <code>sudo aifw backup</code>.</li> <li>Run <code>sudo aifw versions</code> to see the newest published versions, and choose one.</li> <li>Run <code>sudo aifw upgrade --version &lt;version&gt;</code>. Without <code>--version</code> the command shows a menu of versions in a terminal. The command does these things in order: it replaces its own program if the new version has a newer one, and runs again; it takes a backup, and stops if the backup fails; it updates the configuration files; it pins the new version; it pulls the new images, restarts the services and waits until they are healthy; it runs the health checks.</li> <li>Run <code>sudo aifw status</code> and check that every line passes.</li> <li>After an upgrade that adds sign-in settings, run <code>sudo aifw setup</code> once. <code>aifw upgrade</code> does not apply them.</li> </ol> <p>If an upgrade stops half way, it says so. Your data is untouched, and going back is the same command with the previous version. See the limits above.</p>"},{"location":"admin/server/aifw-reference.html#checking-a-running-server","title":"Checking a running server","text":"<p>Three commands, three questions:</p> <ul> <li><code>aifw status</code>: is the server working?</li> <li><code>aifw security-audit</code>: is it safe?</li> <li><code>aifw doctor</code>: both, plus the machine checks, with a fix beside each finding.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"admin/server/aifw-reference.html#aifw-status","title":"<code>aifw status</code>","text":"<p>Reports whether the server is working. Changes nothing.</p> <p>It runs the health checks that the stack performs on itself, then prints the services, the free disk space, the age of the last backup, whether the sign-in address matches the configuration, and how long the web certificate is still valid.</p> <p>One health check would create a workspace. <code>status</code> skips it and says so.</p> <pre><code># aifw status\nServer status:\nServer check:\n\u2713 Server configuration present\n\u2713 All services running \u2014 7 services up\n\u2713 Health checks \u2014 PASSED: 24 FAILED: 0\n\u2713 Free disk space \u2014 81 GB free\n\u2713 Recent backup (under 26 hours old) \u2014 backup-2026-09-30T00-27-04.sql.gz, 7.3 h old\n\u2713 Sign-in service address matches the configuration\n\u2713 Web certificate validity \u2014 expires in 633 day(s)\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"admin/server/aifw-reference.html#aifw-security-audit","title":"<code>aifw security-audit</code>","text":"<p>Reports the security posture. Changes nothing.</p> <p>It checks the firewall and SSH settings, file permissions, whether <code>credentials.txt</code> is still on the server, license enforcement, demo accounts, the sign-in service settings (lockout, password rules and authenticator enrolment for the administrator), the stored registry sign-in and pending system updates.</p> <p>The last line says <code>This server is not ready yet</code> in two different cases: a posture that is not hardened, or a check that could not run at all. The <code>\u2717</code> rows say which. <code>could not check</code> means nothing is known either way. It is not a pass.</p> <p>Abridged example. The long \"What to do\" texts are shortened:</p> <pre><code># aifw security-audit\nSecurity posture:\nServer check:\n\u2713 Server configuration present\n\u2713 Firewall enabled, incoming denied by default\n! SSH password sign-in disabled (keys only)\nWhat to do: Consider key-only SSH \u2026\n! SSH reachable only from addresses you trust \u2014 port 22 accepts connections from any address\nWhat to do: Restrict SSH to the addresses your administrators use \u2026\n\u2713 Configuration and credentials are administrator-only\n! Credentials sheet moved to a password manager \u2014 credentials.txt still on the server\nWhat to do: Move its contents to your password manager, then delete credentials.txt.\n\u2713 License enforced with the built-in verification key \u2014 license active\n\u2713 Registry sign-in \u2014 this must be a READ-ONLY access token, never a publishing one\n\u2717 No demo accounts in the sign-in service \u2014 could not check\nWhat to do: This check could not run, so nothing is known either way \u2026\n! System packages current \u2014 10 update(s) pending\nWhat to do: Automatic security updates are on; a reboot may be needed \u2026\nThis server is not ready yet: 1 requirement(s) unmet, 4 recommendation(s).\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"admin/server/aifw-reference.html#aifw-doctor","title":"<code>aifw doctor</code>","text":"<p>Runs everything that <code>status</code> and <code>security-audit</code> run, plus the machine checks. Prints the result as numbered findings, each with its fix. Changes nothing.</p> <p>Run it when something is wrong and you want a list of actions, or when you do not know which of the three to use.</p>"},{"location":"admin/server/aifw-reference.html#aifw-backup","title":"<code>aifw backup</code>","text":"<p>Dumps the database and the settings. The database of the sign-in service is included. Dumps older than 30 days are deleted.</p> <p>Set <code>AIFW_BACKUP_REMOTE</code> and the dump is also copied off the machine. <code>setup</code> installs a timer that takes a backup every day. <code>upgrade</code> takes one before anything moves. Run the command by hand before a risky change.</p> <p>In a terminal, <code>aifw backup</code> opens a menu with Backup and Restore. Without a terminal (a timer or a script) it makes a backup, as described here. It adds files and deletes its own old dumps. Nothing else is written.</p> <pre><code># aifw backup\nBacking up the database (this can take a minute)...\nNote: backups live only on this server. An off-server copy protects against losing the machine.\nBackup done: /opt/ai-firewall/backups/backup-2026-10-06T08-45-59.sql.gz (0.1 MB)\n</code></pre> <p>The note appears when <code>AIFW_BACKUP_REMOTE</code> is not set. A settings file is written beside the dump with the same time stamp. See Backup and restore for what else to save and how to restore.</p>"},{"location":"admin/server/aifw-reference.html#the-tool-itself","title":"The tool itself","text":""},{"location":"admin/server/aifw-reference.html#aifw-version","title":"<code>aifw --version</code>","text":"<p>Prints the version of the <code>aifw</code> program on this machine. That is not always the version the server runs: the two drift apart when the program is left behind. An old program then reports services as missing, because it looks for containers that a newer stack has retired.</p> <pre><code># aifw --version\naifw 2.2.6\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"admin/server/aifw-reference.html#aifw-help","title":"<code>aifw --help</code>","text":"<p>Lists every command with a one-line description. <code>aifw</code> with no arguments prints the same.</p>"},{"location":"admin/server/aifw-reference.html#aifw-preflight","title":"<code>aifw preflight</code>","text":"<p>Runs the machine checks on their own, without starting an installation. Changes nothing. <code>setup</code> runs the same checks as its first step and stops if a requirement is not met, so there is rarely a reason to type it. On a running server, <code>doctor</code> includes it.</p> <p>It checks: administrator rights, machine type, operating system, number of processors, memory (4 GB minimum, 8 GB recommended), free disk space (20 GB minimum), <code>systemd</code>, Docker installed and running, Docker Compose, whether the registry can be reached, and ports 80, 443 and 8090.</p> <pre><code># aifw preflight\nServer check:\n\u2713 Administrator rights\n\u2713 Machine type \u2014 this machine is x86_64\n\u2713 Operating system \u2014 Ubuntu 24.04.5 LTS\n\u2713 Processors \u2014 2 available\n\u2713 Memory (minimum 4 GB) \u2014 7.8 GB installed\n\u2713 Memory (recommended 8 GB) \u2014 7.8 GB installed\n\u2713 Free disk space (minimum 20 GB) \u2014 82 GB free\n\u2713 Service manager (systemd)\n\u2713 Docker installed and running\n\u2713 Docker Compose available\n\u2713 Software registry reachable\n\u2713 Web ports free (80, 443, 8090)\n</code></pre> <p>On a server that is already running, the ports check recognises the product's own services and names them. A ports warning means another program holds the port.</p>"},{"location":"admin/server/aifw-reference.html#if-something-goes-wrong","title":"If something goes wrong","text":"Symptom Likely cause What to do <code>setup</code> stops on the server check, before asking anything The machine is short of memory or disk, or cannot reach the registry Each <code>\u2717</code> row names the fix. Nothing was changed. Fix it, then run <code>sudo aifw setup</code> again The chosen version does not exist in the registry That version was never published Run <code>aifw versions</code>. It lists everything that can be installed This server is already on a version \u2014 nothing to do <code>upgrade</code> resolved to the version already running Nothing is wrong. The newer version is not published yet The version is OLDER than the version this server runs A downgrade, usually a typing mistake Check the version. Do not force it A check warns that port 80, 443 or 8090 is in use A program other than the product holds the port Find out which program before you continue <code>security-audit</code> says the server is not ready, but it looks healthy The sign-in posture is not hardened, or could not be checked Read the <code>\u2717</code> rows. For <code>could not check</code>, run <code>sudo aifw setup</code> so the sign-in steps are applied <code>setup</code> or <code>upgrade</code> ends with security items it never showed before The run prints the posture now. Older versions did not Nothing broke. The items were always there A service is reported as not running, but the stack looks fine The installed <code>aifw</code> expects a container that the current stack no longer has Compare <code>aifw --version</code> with the version of the stack. An old program reports containers that a newer stack removed on purpose"},{"location":"admin/server/aifw-reference.html#related-pages","title":"Related pages","text":"<ul> <li>The installer script <code>install.sh</code></li> <li>Install the server</li> <li>Operate and upgrade</li> <li>Backup and restore</li> </ul>"},{"location":"admin/server/backup-restore.html","title":"Backup and restore","text":"<p>This page explains what to back up, how to make a backup, and how to bring the server back from one.</p> <p>Never run <code>docker compose down -v</code> on a server</p> <p>It deletes the data volumes, including the database. Use <code>docker compose stop</code> or <code>down</code> without <code>-v</code>. Nothing on this page removes a volume.</p> <p>Restore is a manual procedure</p> <p>On a terminal, <code>aifw backup</code> opens a menu with Backup and Restore. The manual steps below show what a restore does and cover a move to a new server. They have been proven only in part. Rehearse the whole procedure on a test server before you rely on it.</p>"},{"location":"admin/server/backup-restore.html#what-holds-your-data","title":"What holds your data","text":"What Where What it holds Priority Saved by <code>aifw backup</code> Database <code>ai_firewall</code> volume <code>pg-data</code> Workspaces, devices, enrolment keys, policies, admin users, audit records and their chain, conversations, statistics Critical Yes Database <code>keycloak</code> the same volume People, groups, one-time codes, clients and the signing keys of the sign-in service Critical Yes Database roles the same volume The database accounts and their password hashes Critical Yes The settings file <code>.env</code> <code>docker/.env</code> in the install folder Every credential and shared secret of the server, the license, the image versions, the public address Critical A copy is kept on the server only Captured data volume <code>capture-data</code> Per-workspace capture data and approved destinations High No Generated briefs volume <code>briefs-data</code> Generated discovery files Medium No Policy presets, secrets folder <code>presets/</code>, <code>secrets/</code> in the install folder Policy presets, optional secret files High No Your own TLS certificate <code>docker/caddy/certs/</code> The certificate and key, if you chose your own certificate Medium. A Let's Encrypt certificate can be issued again No Installation files the install folder <code>provision-manifest.json</code>, <code>credentials.txt</code> if you kept it, <code>proxy-snippets/</code> Medium No Registry sign-in <code>/root/.docker/config.json</code> The access that lets <code>aifw upgrade</code> download images Medium. It can be issued again No Metrics volumes <code>prometheus-data</code>, <code>grafana-data</code> Metrics for 30 days and dashboard settings Low No <p>What you do not need to back up: the containers (they are rebuilt from the images), the device certificates and private keys (they exist only on each computer; the server keeps public keys only, and these are in the database), and encryption keys (the server has none).</p>"},{"location":"admin/server/backup-restore.html#why-env-must-travel-with-the-database","title":"Why <code>.env</code> must travel with the database","text":"<p>A database dump alone cannot be restored into a working server. The dump restores the database account password hashes, and the passwords in <code>.env</code> must match them. The keys of the sign-in service and the shared secrets in <code>.env</code> must match the restored database too. Keep the <code>.env</code> copy as carefully as a password vault. It holds every credential of the server.</p>"},{"location":"admin/server/backup-restore.html#make-a-backup","title":"Make a backup","text":"<p>All commands run on the server, with <code>sudo</code>.</p>"},{"location":"admin/server/backup-restore.html#what-aifw-backup-does","title":"What <code>aifw backup</code> does","text":"<pre><code>sudo aifw backup\n</code></pre> <ol> <li>Dumps both databases and all roles into <code>/opt/ai-firewall/backups/backup-&lt;time&gt;.sql.gz</code>. A failed or empty dump is deleted, and the command fails.</li> <li>Copies <code>docker/.env</code> to <code>/opt/ai-firewall/backups/backup-&lt;time&gt;.env</code>, readable by the administrator only.</li> <li>Deletes its own backups older than 30 days.</li> <li>If <code>AIFW_BACKUP_REMOTE</code> is set, copies only the <code>.sql.gz</code> file to that place with <code>scp</code>. A failed copy shows a warning.</li> </ol> <p>The <code>.env</code> copy stays on the server. Move it to your backup storage yourself.</p> <p><code>aifw upgrade</code> makes the same backup before it changes anything, and stops if the backup fails.</p>"},{"location":"admin/server/backup-restore.html#set-it-up-once","title":"Set it up once","text":"<ol> <li> <p>Choose a place outside the server. Add this line to <code>/opt/ai-firewall/docker/.env</code>, and make sure the administrator's SSH key is allowed on that machine:</p> <pre><code>AIFW_BACKUP_REMOTE=&lt;user&gt;@&lt;backup-host&gt;:/path/to/backups/\n</code></pre> </li> <li> <p>Turn on the daily schedule. Setup already does this. To install it again:</p> <pre><code>sudo aifw backup --install-timer\nsystemctl list-timers aifw-backup.timer\n</code></pre> <p>It runs once a day, with a random delay of up to an hour, and catches up after a restart.</p> </li> <li> <p>Run a first backup now, and look at the result:</p> <pre><code>sudo aifw backup\nls -lh /opt/ai-firewall/backups/\n</code></pre> </li> </ol>"},{"location":"admin/server/backup-restore.html#save-what-aifw-backup-does-not","title":"Save what <code>aifw backup</code> does not","text":"<p>Copy the volumes and files from the table above, and move the <code>.env</code> copy and the archives off the server.</p> <pre><code>STACK=/opt/ai-firewall/docker\nBK=/var/backups/ai-firewall/$(date +%F_%H%M)\nsudo mkdir -p \"$BK\" &amp;&amp; sudo chmod 700 \"$BK\"\n\n# the data volumes\nfor v in capture-data briefs-data; do\n  sudo docker run --rm -v ai-firewall_${v}:/data:ro -v \"$BK\":/backup debian:stable-slim \\\n    tar czf /backup/${v}.tgz --numeric-owner -C /data .\ndone\n\n# configuration, the compose files, and the installation files\nsudo tar czf \"$BK/files.tgz\" -C \"$STACK\" presets secrets caddy\nsudo cp \"$STACK\"/docker-compose*.yml \"$BK\"/\nsudo tar czf \"$BK/install-files.tgz\" -C /opt/ai-firewall provision-manifest.json proxy-snippets\n</code></pre> <p>Then copy <code>$BK</code> and the <code>.env</code> copy to storage outside the server.</p>"},{"location":"admin/server/backup-restore.html#check-that-a-backup-is-good","title":"Check that a backup is good","text":"Check Expected The <code>.sql.gz</code> file Not empty, and <code>gunzip -t</code> finds no error. <code>zgrep -c 'CREATE DATABASE'</code> shows at least 2 The <code>.env</code> copy Present, readable by the administrator only The archives <code>tar tzf</code> lists the files without errors The copy outside the server Exists and is complete How fresh it is <code>sudo aifw status</code> and <code>sudo aifw doctor</code> warn when the newest backup is more than 26 hours old, or empty A restore drill A recent dump loads into a throw-away PostgreSQL 16 container with no errors except \"already exists\""},{"location":"admin/server/backup-restore.html#restore","title":"Restore","text":""},{"location":"admin/server/backup-restore.html#before-you-start","title":"Before you start","text":"<ul> <li>Use the same or a newer version. The database is upgraded on start. A version older than the dump is not supported. The version is in the <code>.env</code> copy.</li> <li>Keep the same public address. The address is stored in the database, in the sign-in service and in <code>.env</code>, and the agents have it in their settings. Changing it is not covered here.</li> <li>The order matters. Load the databases before the sign-in service and the server start against them. Otherwise the sign-in service creates a new empty realm, and the server migrates an empty database. Setup and upgrade start everything at once, so load the database by hand.</li> <li>You need: the database dump, the <code>.env</code> copy, the volume and file archives, and the registry access details from your welcome email.</li> </ul> <p>Variables used below:</p> <pre><code>STACK=/opt/ai-firewall/docker\nBK=/path/to/the/unpacked/backup\nCOMPOSE=\"docker compose --project-directory $STACK --env-file $STACK/.env -f $STACK/docker-compose.prod.yml\"\n# with bundled HTTPS, add:  -f $STACK/docker-compose.tls.yml\nPG_USER=ai_firewall        # or the PG_USER value from the .env copy\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"admin/server/backup-restore.html#scenario-a-a-new-server","title":"Scenario A: a new server","text":"<ol> <li>Prepare the server. Ubuntu 24.04, the same network rules as the old one. Install the same version of the setup tool with <code>install.sh</code>.</li> <li> <p>Put the <code>.env</code> back before you run setup. Setup never rewrites an existing <code>.env</code>. It only adds missing keys.</p> <pre><code>sudo mkdir -p /opt/ai-firewall/docker\nsudo install -m 600 \"$BK\"/backup-*.env /opt/ai-firewall/docker/.env\nsudo tar xzf \"$BK/files.tgz\" -C \"$STACK\"\nsudo cp \"$BK\"/docker-compose*.yml \"$STACK\"/\nsudo tar xzf \"$BK/install-files.tgz\" -C /opt/ai-firewall\n</code></pre> <p>The compose files (<code>docker-compose.prod.yml</code>, and <code>docker-compose.tls.yml</code> for bundled HTTPS) are not part of <code>aifw backup</code>. On a new server they exist only after setup has run, so step 4 needs the copies that you saved.</p> </li> <li> <p>Sign in to the registry with the access details from your welcome email, so the images can be downloaded. <code>install.sh</code> does this.</p> </li> <li> <p>Start only the database, and wait until it is ready. On an empty volume it starts, restarts once and sets itself up, so wait twice:</p> <pre><code>$COMPOSE up -d postgres\nuntil docker exec ai-firewall-pg pg_isready -U $PG_USER; do sleep 2; done\nsleep 5\nuntil docker exec ai-firewall-pg pg_isready -U $PG_USER; do sleep 2; done\n</code></pre> </li> <li> <p>Load the dump (see Load the database dump).</p> </li> <li> <p>Restore the data volumes. Create them first, then unpack:</p> <pre><code>$COMPOSE up --no-start\nfor v in capture-data briefs-data; do\n  docker run --rm -v ai-firewall_${v}:/data -v \"$BK\":/backup debian:stable-slim \\\n    sh -c \"tar xzf /backup/${v}.tgz --numeric-owner -C /data &amp;&amp; chown -R 10005:10005 /data\"\ndone\n</code></pre> </li> <li> <p>Start everything, then run setup again to put back the host settings (the backup timer and the checks). It finds the existing <code>.env</code> and keeps it:</p> <pre><code>$COMPOSE up -d\nsudo aifw setup\n</code></pre> </li> <li> <p>Point traffic at the new server. Change the DNS record, or the target of your load balancer or proxy.</p> </li> <li>Check (see Check after a restore). Switch off the old server only when the checks pass.</li> </ol> <p>Images are not loaded from a file. They are downloaded from the software registry, with the registry sign-in that <code>install.sh</code> stored, when you run <code>aifw setup</code> or <code>docker compose up</code>.</p> <p>When you run <code>sudo aifw setup</code> in step 7, give the same public address, version and administrator e-mail that the restored <code>.env</code> holds. Setup keeps the restored <code>.env</code> and refuses a different address, version or e-mail. Setup writes <code>credentials.txt</code> again. Delete it after you save what you need.</p>"},{"location":"admin/server/backup-restore.html#scenario-b-the-same-server-damaged-data","title":"Scenario B: the same server, damaged data","text":"<ol> <li> <p>Take a safety dump of the current state first. It is the only way back if the restore goes wrong:</p> <pre><code>sudo aifw backup\n</code></pre> </li> <li> <p>Stop everything except the database. Do not use <code>down -v</code>:</p> <pre><code>$COMPOSE stop control-plane keycloak admin-portal grafana prometheus\n</code></pre> </li> <li> <p>Put back the matching <code>.env</code> if it changed since the backup (for example after credentials were changed):</p> <pre><code>sudo install -m 600 \"$BK\"/backup-*.env \"$STACK/.env\"\n$COMPOSE up -d postgres\n</code></pre> </li> <li> <p>Load the dump (next section).</p> </li> <li>Restore files and volumes only if they are damaged, as in Scenario A, step 6.</li> <li>Start the server with the same or a newer version: <code>$COMPOSE up -d</code>. Then check.</li> </ol>"},{"location":"admin/server/backup-restore.html#load-the-database-dump","title":"Load the database dump","text":"<p>The dump already holds the commands that create the roles and both databases. Remove the existing databases first, then load the dump while connected to the maintenance database:</p> <pre><code>docker exec -i ai-firewall-pg psql -U $PG_USER -d postgres &lt;&lt;'SQL'\nSELECT pg_terminate_backend(pid) FROM pg_stat_activity\nWHERE datname IN ('ai_firewall','keycloak') AND pid &lt;&gt; pg_backend_pid();\nDROP DATABASE IF EXISTS keycloak;\nDROP DATABASE IF EXISTS ai_firewall;\nSQL\n\ngunzip -c \"$BK\"/backup-*.sql.gz | docker exec -i ai-firewall-pg psql -U $PG_USER -d postgres 2&gt;&amp;1 | tee /tmp/restore.log\ngrep -i error /tmp/restore.log\n</code></pre> <p>Expected error: <code>role \"ai_firewall\" already exists</code>. Later lines still set the right password hash. Any other error means the restore is incomplete. Stop, find the cause, and go back to the safety dump if needed.</p> <p>Quick check before you start the rest:</p> <pre><code>docker exec ai-firewall-pg psql -U $PG_USER -d ai_firewall -c \"SELECT count(*) FROM tenants;\"\ndocker exec ai-firewall-pg psql -U $PG_USER -d keycloak -c \"SELECT name FROM realm;\"\n</code></pre> <p>The first must give the number of workspaces you expect. The second must list <code>master</code> and <code>ai-firewall</code>.</p>"},{"location":"admin/server/backup-restore.html#check-after-a-restore","title":"Check after a restore","text":"# Check Command Expected 1 Containers are healthy <code>docker ps --format '{{.Names}} {{.Status}}'</code> The database, sign-in service, portal and server are <code>healthy</code> 2 The server is alive <code>curl -fsS http://localhost:8090/health</code> <code>{\"status\":\"ok\"}</code> 3 The database is reachable <code>curl -fsS http://localhost:8090/ready</code> <code>{\"status\":\"ready\"}</code> 4 The database was upgraded <code>docker logs ai-firewall-control-plane 2&gt;&amp;1 \\| grep -i migrat</code> Pending changes applied, no failure 5 The built-in checks <code>sudo aifw status &amp;&amp; sudo aifw doctor</code> All pass, including the sign-in address and the backup age 6 The sign-in address <code>curl -fsS https://&lt;your-address&gt;/keycloak/realms/ai-firewall/.well-known/openid-configuration</code> The <code>issuer</code> starts with your public address 7 The console is closed <code>curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}' https://&lt;your-address&gt;/keycloak/admin/</code> <code>404</code> 8 The portal Sign in at <code>https://&lt;your-address&gt;/admin/</code> Your workspaces, devices and policies are there 9 The audit chain The chain check in the portal It verifies. A gap from the restore shows as a re-anchor, not as tampering 10 Agents The device list in the portal Devices report again within minutes"},{"location":"admin/server/backup-restore.html#what-agents-experience-after-a-restore","title":"What agents experience after a restore","text":"<ul> <li>Computers enrolled before the backup reconnect with their existing keys. Nothing to do.</li> <li>Computers enrolled after the backup are refused at first. The agent then drops its key and joins again by itself with its enrolment key. This works only if the enrolment keys did not change, which is another reason to restore the original <code>.env</code>.</li> <li>Audit events between the backup and the failure are lost on the server, unless agents still hold them and send them again. The gap is recorded as a re-anchor.</li> <li>Changes made in the portal after the backup (policies, groups, administrators, sign-in accounts and one-time codes) are lost and must be done again.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"admin/server/identity-providers.html","title":"Single sign-on with your company directory","text":"<p>An identity provider (IdP) lets people sign in to the admin portal with the accounts they already have, for example their Microsoft Entra ID account.</p> <p>Preview</p> <p>Single sign-on is provided by Keycloak's own identity brokering. ScienceSoft AI Endpoint Firewall does not set it up for you and has no Microsoft-specific settings. You set it up in the Keycloak console. It is offered as a preview: the steps below follow Keycloak and Microsoft documentation, and ScienceSoft has not tested them with every directory setup. Test with a test account before you tell people.</p> <p>Creating an identity provider needs the built-in Keycloak administrator account (<code>kcadmin</code>). The personal console account that setup creates cannot do it. For the same reason, the Identity providers option is not shown to a <code>super-admin</code> account by default.</p>"},{"location":"admin/server/identity-providers.html#why-use-one","title":"Why use one","text":"<ul> <li>One account and one password. People do not get a separate portal password, and you do not have to deliver passwords.</li> <li>Central account life. When your organisation disables an employee in the directory, that person can no longer sign in to the portal.</li> <li>Your sign-in rules. Multi-factor sign-in, conditional access and password rules of the IdP apply to the portal.</li> </ul> <p>The IdP only confirms who the person is. Keycloak still decides what they can do: the role, the workspace group and all-workspace access are set in the <code>ai-firewall</code> realm, in the same way as for local accounts. See Sign-in and roles.</p>"},{"location":"admin/server/identity-providers.html#how-single-sign-on-works","title":"How single sign-on works","text":"<p>A person opens <code>https://&lt;your-address&gt;/admin</code>. They are sent to the Keycloak sign-in page, choose the IdP, sign in there and return to the portal. Keycloak sits between the portal and the IdP.</p>"},{"location":"admin/server/identity-providers.html#steps-for-any-provider","title":"Steps for any provider","text":"<ol> <li>Add the IdP to the <code>ai-firewall</code> realm only. Never to <code>master</code>.</li> <li>Choose the alias first, for example <code>entra-id</code>. The alias is part of the redirect address and cannot be changed after you create the provider.</li> <li> <p>Register Keycloak as an application in the IdP. The redirect address is:</p> <pre><code>https://&lt;your-address&gt;/keycloak/realms/ai-firewall/broker/&lt;alias&gt;/endpoint\n</code></pre> <p>Do not copy the redirect address that the Keycloak console shows. The console opens through an SSH tunnel and shows an address that starts with <code>http://127.0.0.1</code>. The IdP and the users' browsers cannot reach it. Take the value from the console and replace the part before <code>/keycloak</code> with your public address.</p> </li> <li> <p>Create the provider in Keycloak with the client ID and the client secret from the IdP.</p> </li> <li>Plan how people get access. A person who signs in through the IdP for the first time is created in the <code>ai-firewall</code> realm without a role, so the portal does not work for them yet. The realm can also add new people to its default workspace group (<code>default-tenant</code>). Check the group of each new person, because it may not be the workspace that you want. Either give them the role and the group by hand after the first sign-in, or add mappers that do it automatically. Never map anyone to <code>fleet-operators</code> automatically.</li> <li>Test with a test account before you tell people: open the portal, choose the IdP, and check that the person sees the right workspace.</li> <li>Optional: skip the Keycloak page. Set the IdP as the default provider of the Identity Provider Redirector step in the <code>browser</code> authentication flow of the realm. Keep at least one local administrator account for when the IdP is not available.</li> </ol>"},{"location":"admin/server/identity-providers.html#microsoft-entra-id","title":"Microsoft Entra ID","text":"<p>You can connect Entra ID in two ways. Both use an app registration in Entra ID.</p> Built-in Microsoft provider Generic OpenID Connect provider Effort Low: client ID, client secret and tenant ID Higher: discovery address, client authentication, scopes, signature checks Map Entra groups or app roles to Keycloak Not supported Supported, with mappers Limit to your organisation Only if you fill in the tenant ID. An empty field lets any Microsoft account in Built in Choose it when People only need to sign in, and you give roles and groups in Keycloak You want Entra groups or app roles to give access"},{"location":"admin/server/identity-providers.html#option-1-built-in-microsoft-provider","title":"Option 1. Built-in Microsoft provider","text":"<p>In the Microsoft Entra admin center:</p> <ol> <li>Open App registrations, then New registration.</li> <li>Enter a name, for example <code>AI Firewall admin portal</code>. For Supported account types, choose Accounts in this organizational directory only.</li> <li>For Redirect URI, choose the Web platform and enter the redirect address from above. Select Register.</li> <li>On Overview, copy the Application (client) ID and the Directory (tenant) ID.</li> <li>Open Certificates &amp; secrets, create a new client secret and copy its Value. Note the expiry date: sign-in stops when the secret expires.</li> <li>In API permissions, check that the delegated Microsoft Graph permission <code>User.Read</code> is there.</li> </ol> <p>In the Keycloak console (realm <code>ai-firewall</code>):</p> <ol> <li>Open Identity providers and choose Microsoft.</li> <li>Fill in Alias, Display name (the text of the button on the sign-in page), Client ID, Client Secret and Tenant ID. Do not leave Tenant ID empty.</li> <li>Select Add.</li> </ol>"},{"location":"admin/server/identity-providers.html#option-2-generic-openid-connect-provider","title":"Option 2. Generic OpenID Connect provider","text":"<p>In the Microsoft Entra admin center:</p> <ol> <li>Register the application as in steps 1 to 5 above, with the same redirect address.</li> <li>Open Token configuration and add the optional claim <code>email</code> to the ID token.</li> <li>To pass Entra groups, select Add groups claim. To pass app roles, define them in App roles and assign people to them in Enterprise applications.</li> </ol> <p>In the Keycloak console (realm <code>ai-firewall</code>):</p> <ol> <li>Open Identity providers and choose OpenID Connect v1.0.</li> <li>Fill in Alias and Display name.</li> <li>For Discovery endpoint, enter <code>https://login.microsoftonline.com/&lt;tenant-id&gt;/v2.0/.well-known/openid-configuration</code>.</li> <li>Set Client authentication to Client secret sent as post. Fill in Client ID and Client Secret.</li> <li>Select Add. Then set Scopes to <code>openid profile email</code>, make sure Validate Signatures and Use JWKS URL are on, and select Save.</li> <li>If Entra passes groups or app roles, open Mappers and add Claim to Role or Advanced Claim to Group mappers that turn them into the role and the workspace group.</li> </ol>"},{"location":"admin/server/identity-providers.html#after-you-connect","title":"After you connect","text":"<ul> <li>Without mappers, give each new person the role and the workspace group by hand.</li> <li>Never map Entra groups or roles to <code>fleet-operators</code> automatically.</li> <li>Before the client secret expires, create a new one in Entra ID and update Client Secret in the provider.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"admin/server/identity-providers.html#more-information","title":"More information","text":"<ul> <li>Keycloak: Server Administration Guide, the sections on identity providers and mappers.</li> <li>Microsoft: Register an application and OpenID Connect on the Microsoft identity platform.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"admin/server/install-script.html","title":"The installer script: <code>install.sh</code>","text":"<p><code>install.sh</code> is the first step of installing the ScienceSoft AI Endpoint Firewall server. It prepares an empty Ubuntu server and installs one command, <code>aifw</code>. You then run <code>aifw setup</code>, which installs and configures everything else: the server, the admin portal, the sign-in service, the database, HTTPS and monitoring.</p> <pre><code>sudo bash install.sh\nsudo aifw setup\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"admin/server/install-script.html#download","title":"Download","text":"<p>Get the current script from the ScienceSoft repository: install.sh. The same file is in your welcome email.</p> <p>You need the registry access name and token from your welcome email to run it. The software cannot be downloaded without them. The script does not contain them.</p> <p>The script does not contain the registry access name or token. It contains the address of the software registry, which you can override with <code>AIFW_REGISTRY</code>.</p>"},{"location":"admin/server/install-script.html#before-you-start","title":"Before you start","text":"<ul> <li>An Ubuntu server. Ubuntu 24.04 LTS is the tested system. On another system the script prints a note and continues. Intel/AMD (x86_64) and ARM (aarch64) processors are supported.</li> <li>Administrator rights (you run it with <code>sudo</code>).</li> <li>Internet access from the server: to the Ubuntu package archive, to the software registry and, for Let's Encrypt, to the certificate authority.</li> <li>The registry access name and token, and the license file, from your welcome email.</li> <li>The server size and ports from Server requirements.</li> </ul> <p>Take a snapshot or backup of the server first.</p>"},{"location":"admin/server/install-script.html#how-to-use-it","title":"How to use it","text":"<ol> <li> <p>Copy <code>install.sh</code> to the server. If you copied it from a Windows computer, convert the line endings first, or it can fail without a message:</p> <pre><code>sed -i 's/\\r$//' install.sh\n</code></pre> </li> <li> <p>Run the script as administrator:</p> <pre><code>sudo bash install.sh\n</code></pre> </li> <li> <p>When asked, enter the registry access name and the access token. The token you type is hidden. They are checked at once.</p> </li> <li> <p>Wait for the script to finish. It ends with:</p> <pre><code>Done. The aifw command is installed:\naifw &lt;version&gt;\n\nNext step:  sudo aifw setup\n</code></pre> </li> <li> <p>Check the server, then set it up. See Install the server and the aifw command reference:</p> <pre><code>sudo aifw preflight\nsudo aifw setup\n</code></pre> </li> </ol>"},{"location":"admin/server/install-script.html#what-the-script-does","title":"What the script does","text":"<ol> <li>Checks that you run it as administrator.</li> <li>Notes the operating system. It continues on a system other than Ubuntu 24.04.</li> <li>Checks the processor type. Anything other than x86_64 or aarch64 stops the script before anything is changed.</li> <li>Installs Docker Engine and the Docker Compose component if they are missing. Docker comes from the Ubuntu package archive.</li> <li>Signs in to the software registry with your access name and token, and checks them.</li> <li>Downloads the installer image for this processor type and takes the <code>aifw</code> command out of it.</li> <li>Puts <code>aifw</code> at <code>/usr/local/bin/aifw</code> and prints its version.</li> </ol> <p>It does not set up the server. That is <code>aifw setup</code>.</p> <p>The registry sign-in is stored for the administrator account (<code>root</code>) in Docker's settings, so that <code>aifw upgrade</code> can download newer versions later. The access token should be a read-only token.</p>"},{"location":"admin/server/install-script.html#settings","title":"Settings","text":"<p>For an unattended installation, give the script its settings as environment variables. All are optional, except the two access values, which the script asks for when they are not set.</p> Variable Meaning <code>AIFW_REGISTRY_USER</code> The registry access name <code>AIFW_REGISTRY_TOKEN</code> The registry access token <code>AIFW_VERSION</code> The version of the installer to download. By default: the version this server already runs, or the newest published version on a new server <code>AIFW_REGISTRY</code> The software registry address (set by default) <code>AIFW_REGISTRY_PATH</code> The project in the registry (set by default) <p>Example, with no questions asked:</p> <pre><code>sudo AIFW_REGISTRY_USER='&lt;name&gt;' AIFW_REGISTRY_TOKEN='&lt;token&gt;' bash install.sh\n</code></pre> <p>A command line is not private</p> <p>Other users of the server can see a command line for a moment, and it can stay in the shell history. Prefer the questions on the screen, unless the installation is automated and the secret comes from a secret store.</p> <p>To install a specific version:</p> <pre><code>sudo AIFW_VERSION=&lt;version&gt; bash install.sh\n</code></pre> <p>Running the script again is safe. It repairs a damaged <code>aifw</code> command. On a server that already works it keeps the version that server runs. To change the version, use <code>aifw upgrade</code>.</p>"},{"location":"admin/server/install-script.html#if-something-goes-wrong","title":"If something goes wrong","text":"<p>The script stops with a line that starts with \"Problem:\".</p> The message says What to do This installer needs administrator rights Run it with <code>sudo</code>: <code>sudo bash install.sh</code> The software is published for Intel/AMD (x86_64) and ARM (aarch64) servers The server has another processor type. Use a supported server Another program is installing software right now Often the automatic security updater, just after boot. Wait a minute and run the script again This server could not reach the Ubuntu package archive Check the internet access and proxy settings of the server Both the access name and the token are needed Both are in your welcome email. Ask your ScienceSoft contact if you cannot find them The registry did not accept the access token Check for typing mistakes. If it still fails, ask for a new token Could not download the installer image Check the internet access of the server to the software registry This version was not published for this machine There is no build for this processor type. Ask your ScienceSoft contact, or use an Intel/AMD server Could not write the <code>aifw</code> command The disk is full or read-only. Check it with <code>df -h /usr/local/bin</code> Docker is installed but not running Start it with <code>systemctl start docker</code> <p>After you fix the cause, run the script again. For problems with setup, see Troubleshooting.</p>"},{"location":"admin/server/install-script.html#security-notes","title":"Security notes","text":"<ul> <li>Read the script before you run it as administrator. It is a short, plain shell script.</li> <li>Download it only from the ScienceSoft repository linked above or from your welcome email.</li> <li>Keep the access token secret. Do not put it into tickets or chats.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"admin/server/install.html","title":"Install the server","text":"<p>This page takes you from an empty Ubuntu server to a working ScienceSoft AI Endpoint Firewall server. Read Server requirements first.</p>"},{"location":"admin/server/install.html#step-1-prepare-the-server","title":"Step 1. Prepare the server","text":"<p>Connect over SSH and become the administrator:</p> <pre><code>ssh &lt;user&gt;@&lt;server-address&gt;\nsudo -i\ncd &lt;folder that holds install.sh&gt;\nchmod +x ./install.sh\n</code></pre> <p>If you copied install.sh from a Windows computer</p> <p>A script saved with Windows line endings can fail silently on Linux. Convert it before you run it:</p> <pre><code>sed -i 's/\\r$//' install.sh\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"admin/server/install.html#step-2-install-docker-and-the-aifw-command","title":"Step 2. Install Docker and the <code>aifw</code> command","text":"<pre><code>bash install.sh\n</code></pre> <p>The script:</p> <ol> <li>installs Docker Engine and the Docker Compose plugin, if they are missing;</li> <li>asks for the registry access name and token from your welcome email (what you type is hidden) and checks them at once;</li> <li>downloads the setup tool and installs the <code>aifw</code> command.</li> </ol> <p>Check that it worked:</p> <pre><code>aifw --version\n</code></pre> <p>To install a specific version of the <code>aifw</code> command, set <code>AIFW_VERSION</code> when you run the script, for example <code>AIFW_VERSION=&lt;version&gt; bash install.sh</code>. By default the script takes the version that this server already runs, or the newest published version on a new server. To upgrade a server that already works, do not run the script again: use <code>aifw upgrade</code>. All the settings of the script and its error messages are on The installer script. Every <code>aifw</code> command is described on aifw commands.</p>"},{"location":"admin/server/install.html#step-3-check-the-server-optional-recommended","title":"Step 3. Check the server (optional, recommended)","text":"<pre><code>aifw preflight\n</code></pre> <p>It checks the processor, memory and disk, that the needed ports are free, that <code>systemd</code> is present and that the server can reach the software registry. It changes nothing. A problem shows up here instead of half way through setup. Run it before a non-interactive installation, when nobody watches the questions.</p>"},{"location":"admin/server/install.html#step-4-run-setup","title":"Step 4. Run setup","text":"<p>Setup creates and starts everything. You can run it in two ways.</p> <p>Interactive. Setup asks the questions one by one and shows a summary to confirm before it changes anything:</p> <pre><code>aifw setup\n</code></pre> <p>Non-interactive. Setup reads the answers from a file, so an installation is repeatable:</p> <pre><code>aifw setup --answers /opt/ai-firewall/answers.json\n</code></pre> <p>Unknown keys in the file are rejected with an error, so a typing mistake fails loudly. Passwords and tokens are never part of this file. They are always generated for your server.</p> <p>To see the whole plan without changing anything:</p> <pre><code>aifw setup --check\n</code></pre> <p>Whichever way you run it, setup goes through eight phases: server check, server preparation, your answers, configuration and generated secrets, download and start, secure web access, sign-in service set-up, and the final sign-in details and backup set-up. It reports what it created and what was already in place.</p> <p>Running setup again is safe</p> <p>Each step looks at the server before it changes anything, so a second run changes only what has drifted. Your previous answers are offered again. Use <code>aifw setup --check</code> first on a live server: it shows what would change and changes nothing.</p> <p>Before you change a basic answer</p> <p>Setup does not change the public address, the version or the administrator e-mail of a server that is already set up. It stops and names the setting. If one of them is wrong and the server is not in use yet, restore the snapshot taken before the installation, then run setup again. To change the version, use <code>aifw upgrade</code>. You can change how HTTPS is handled by running setup again. To renew the licence, run setup again and give the path of the new <code>.license</code> file. Press Enter to keep the installed one. The licence is read when the server starts, so check the License page afterwards. Take a snapshot of the server before you change anything.</p>"},{"location":"admin/server/install.html#the-answers","title":"The answers","text":"Answer Prompt What it is <code>flavor</code> Server type <code>standard</code> for a normal installation. <code>publicUrl</code> Public address The full <code>https://</code> address of the server. No path, no trailing slash. <code>version</code> Version The exact version to install, for example <code>2.2.7</code>. Not <code>latest</code>. <code>authMode</code> Emergency token field on the sign-in page <code>compat</code>: the sign-in page has the single sign-on button and an emergency token field (recommended). <code>keycloak</code>: single sign-on only. <code>licenseBlobPath</code> License file path The full path of the <code>.license</code> file on the server. On a repeat run, give the path of a new file to replace the installed licence. <code>monitoringDataDir</code> Folder for metrics and logs (optional) An absolute folder, for example on a separate disk, where metrics and logs are kept. <code>adminEmail</code> Admin email The first administrator. A temporary password is made and shown once at the end. <code>workspaceId</code> First workspace name <code>main</code>, unless you need another name. <code>adminSshSourceIp</code> Restrict SSH to one address Optional. Lets only this address connect over SSH. <code>hardenSsh</code> (answers file only) Optional. <code>true</code> turns off SSH sign-in by password. Default <code>false</code>. <code>rootDir</code> (answers file only) Where the installation lives. Default <code>/opt/ai-firewall</code>."},{"location":"admin/server/install.html#how-https-is-handled","title":"How HTTPS is handled","text":"<p>One more answer decides how the server gets its HTTPS certificate. It is called HTTPS handling in the questions and <code>tlsMode</code> in the answers file.</p>"},{"location":"admin/server/install.html#option-1-automatic-certificates-lets-encrypt","title":"Option 1. Automatic certificates (Let's Encrypt)","text":"<p>Choose this when the server has a public DNS name that is reachable from the internet, and you want the certificate to be issued and renewed automatically.</p> Key Value <code>tlsMode</code> <code>caddy-letsencrypt</code> <code>letsencryptEmail</code> The contact address for certificate notices <p>Port 80 must be open, as well as 443. The DNS name must already point at this server, because the certificate authority checks it during setup.</p> <pre><code>{\n  \"flavor\": \"standard\",\n  \"publicUrl\": \"https://ai-firewall.example.com\",\n  \"version\": \"&lt;version&gt;\",\n  \"authMode\": \"compat\",\n  \"licenseBlobPath\": \"/opt/ai-firewall/company.license\",\n  \"adminEmail\": \"admin@example.com\",\n  \"workspaceId\": \"main\",\n  \"tlsMode\": \"caddy-letsencrypt\",\n  \"letsencryptEmail\": \"admin@example.com\",\n  \"hardenSsh\": false\n}\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"admin/server/install.html#option-2-your-own-certificate","title":"Option 2. Your own certificate","text":"<p>Choose this when you have your own certificate, for example from a company certificate authority, or when the server has no public DNS name.</p> Key Value <code>tlsMode</code> <code>caddy-custom</code> <code>tlsCertPath</code> The full path of the certificate (<code>.pem</code>) on the server <code>tlsKeyPath</code> The full path of its private key <p>Put the certificate and key on the server before you start, at those paths, readable by the user who runs setup. Port 80 is not needed.</p> <pre><code>{\n  \"flavor\": \"standard\",\n  \"publicUrl\": \"https://ai-firewall.example.com\",\n  \"version\": \"&lt;version&gt;\",\n  \"authMode\": \"compat\",\n  \"licenseBlobPath\": \"/opt/ai-firewall/company.license\",\n  \"adminEmail\": \"admin@example.com\",\n  \"workspaceId\": \"main\",\n  \"tlsMode\": \"caddy-custom\",\n  \"tlsCertPath\": \"/opt/ai-firewall/certs/server.pem\",\n  \"tlsKeyPath\": \"/opt/ai-firewall/certs/server.key\",\n  \"hardenSsh\": false\n}\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"admin/server/install.html#option-3-your-own-load-balancer-or-proxy","title":"Option 3. Your own load balancer or proxy","text":"<p>Choose this when a load balancer or reverse proxy in front of the server already ends HTTPS, and only plain HTTP reaches the server. This is usual in a company network or behind a managed load balancer in a cloud.</p> Key Value <code>tlsMode</code> <code>external</code> <code>lbIp</code> Optional. The address of the load balancer, to allow it through the firewall <p>Set up and start the load balancer before you run setup. Setup also writes ready-made settings for nginx and IIS that you can use. See Reverse proxy.</p> <pre><code>{\n  \"flavor\": \"standard\",\n  \"publicUrl\": \"https://ai-firewall.example.com\",\n  \"version\": \"&lt;version&gt;\",\n  \"authMode\": \"compat\",\n  \"licenseBlobPath\": \"/opt/ai-firewall/company.license\",\n  \"adminEmail\": \"admin@example.com\",\n  \"workspaceId\": \"main\",\n  \"tlsMode\": \"external\",\n  \"lbIp\": \"&lt;load-balancer-ip&gt;\",\n  \"hardenSsh\": false\n}\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"admin/server/install.html#step-5-check-that-it-works","title":"Step 5. Check that it works","text":"<pre><code>docker ps\n</code></pre> <p>All containers must show <code>Up</code>, and <code>(healthy)</code> once their start-up time has passed:</p> Container What it does <code>ai-firewall-caddy</code> Handles HTTPS on ports 80 and 443 (not present when you use your own load balancer) <code>ai-firewall-control-plane</code> The server itself: devices, administration and audit. Listens on port 8090 inside the server <code>ai-firewall-admin-portal</code> The admin portal <code>ai-firewall-keycloak</code> The sign-in service <code>ai-firewall-pg</code> The database <code>ai-firewall-prometheus</code>, <code>ai-firewall-alertmanager</code>, <code>ai-firewall-loki</code>, <code>ai-firewall-alloy</code>, <code>ai-firewall-grafana</code> Metrics, alerts, logs and dashboards <p><code>ai-firewall-init-keycloak</code> runs once when the server starts, creates the sign-in database and stops. It does not appear in <code>docker ps</code>. That is normal.</p> <p>From the server, ask the service if it is healthy:</p> <pre><code>curl -fsS http://127.0.0.1:8090/health\ncurl -fsS http://127.0.0.1:8090/ready\n</code></pre> <p>Or run:</p> <pre><code>aifw status\n</code></pre> <p>Then, from outside:</p> <ul> <li>health: <code>https://&lt;your-address&gt;/health</code> must answer <code>{\"status\":\"ok\"}</code>;</li> <li>the sign-in page: <code>https://&lt;your-address&gt;/admin</code>.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"admin/server/install.html#step-6-save-the-sign-in-details","title":"Step 6. Save the sign-in details","text":"<p>Setup writes the sign-in details to <code>credentials.txt</code> in the install folder, for example <code>/opt/ai-firewall/credentials.txt</code>. They are also shown on the last screen. The file holds:</p> <ul> <li>the address of the admin portal, the administrator's email and a temporary password;</li> <li>the device enrolment key, which the agent installers use to join computers to your workspace;</li> <li>the emergency sign-in token;</li> <li>the way to reach Grafana (dashboards): it is not on the public address. Open an SSH tunnel to port 3000 on the server, browse to <code>http://127.0.0.1:3000</code> and sign in with a super-admin account. The password in the file is for emergencies;</li> <li>the sign-in service console account and the command to reach the console (see Sign-in and roles).</li> </ul> <p>Move every value into your password manager now, then delete the file:</p> <pre><code>rm /opt/ai-firewall/credentials.txt\n</code></pre> <p>Other secrets that the installation needs (the database password, internal tokens and the license) are generated into a file that only the administrator can read, in the install folder. Keep it on the server. Do not copy it, and never paste it into a ticket or a chat.</p>"},{"location":"admin/server/install.html#step-7-check-the-firewall-of-the-server","title":"Step 7. Check the firewall of the server","text":"<pre><code>ufw status verbose\n</code></pre> <p>Only the ports for your HTTPS option (see Server requirements) should be open. Everything else is blocked.</p>"},{"location":"admin/server/install.html#next","title":"Next","text":"<ul> <li>Sign-in and roles: sign in and add administrators.</li> <li>Operate and upgrade: turn on backups.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"admin/server/operate.html","title":"Operate and upgrade","text":"<p>All commands run on the server, as the administrator (use <code>sudo</code>).</p>"},{"location":"admin/server/operate.html#everyday-commands","title":"Everyday commands","text":"Command What it does <code>aifw status</code> Shows whether everything is healthy. Safe to run at any time. <code>aifw doctor</code> Looks for problems and explains what it finds. <code>aifw security-audit</code> Checks the security settings of the server. <code>aifw versions</code> Lists the versions you can upgrade to. <code>aifw backup</code> Makes a backup now. See Backup and restore. <code>aifw --version</code> Shows the version of the <code>aifw</code> command. <p><code>aifw security-audit</code> checks the safety of the server: the firewall, the SSH settings, file permissions, the license, the sign-in service settings and pending system updates. It changes nothing. <code>aifw doctor</code> runs the machine checks, the health checks and the security checks, and prints one numbered list of findings with a fix beside each. It changes nothing too. Both end with a summary line, and return a non-zero exit code if a requirement is not met.</p>"},{"location":"admin/server/operate.html#backups","title":"Backups","text":"<p>The database holds everything: workspaces, devices, audit history and usage statistics. Back it up and keep the backups away from the server.</p> <pre><code>sudo aifw backup\n</code></pre> <ul> <li>It writes a dated copy of both databases and a copy of the settings file (<code>.env</code>) into the <code>backups</code> folder under the install folder. It keeps the last 30 days.</li> <li>Setup also installs a daily timer that runs the backup.</li> <li>If you set <code>AIFW_BACKUP_REMOTE</code>, it copies the database dump only to that place. The copy of the settings file stays on the server. Move it to your backup storage yourself. A database dump alone cannot be restored without it.</li> <li>Some data is not saved by <code>aifw backup</code>: captured data, policy presets and your own TLS certificate.</li> </ul> <p>The full list, the steps for a complete backup and the restore procedure are on Backup and restore. Restore is in the menu of <code>aifw backup</code> on a terminal. Read the procedure, and rehearse it on a test server, before you need it.</p>"},{"location":"admin/server/operate.html#upgrade","title":"Upgrade","text":"<pre><code>sudo aifw versions               # what can I upgrade to?\nsudo aifw upgrade --version &lt;version&gt;\nsudo aifw status\n</code></pre> <ul> <li>Take a snapshot or a backup first. <code>aifw upgrade</code> also makes a backup before it changes anything, and stops if that backup fails.</li> <li>The database is upgraded automatically. Updates are additive, so a server upgrade does not reject or lose data from computers that already use ScienceSoft AI Endpoint Firewall.</li> <li>After the upgrade, check <code>https://&lt;your-address&gt;/health</code>.</li> <li><code>aifw upgrade</code> does not apply new sign-in settings. After an upgrade that adds them, run <code>sudo aifw setup</code> once. See aifw commands.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"admin/server/operate.html#roll-back","title":"Roll back","text":"<p>If the new version does not work, go back to the previous one:</p> <pre><code>sudo aifw upgrade --version &lt;previous-version&gt;\n</code></pre> <p>There is no separate rollback command. In a terminal the command warns that older software will run against data that the newer version already changed, and asks you to confirm. In a script it refuses. If you must go back, restore the snapshot or the backup that you took before the upgrade.</p>"},{"location":"admin/server/operate.html#re-running-setup","title":"Re-running setup","text":"<p>Running <code>aifw setup</code> again is safe. It checks every part of the installation and repairs what has drifted, without touching your data, and offers your previous answers. Use <code>aifw setup --check</code> first to see what would change. Setup does not change the public address, the version or the administrator e-mail of a server that is already set up. It stops and names the setting. To move to a newer version, use <code>aifw upgrade</code>. See Install the server.</p>"},{"location":"admin/server/operate.html#monitoring","title":"Monitoring","text":"<p>The installation includes Grafana (dashboards), Prometheus (metrics, kept for 30 days by default), Alertmanager (alerts), and Loki and Alloy (logs). None of them is on the public address. <code>sudo aifw monitoring retention</code> changes how long metrics and logs are kept. Prometheus is reachable only inside the server's own network, so you cannot open it from outside.</p> <ul> <li>The dashboard \"ai-firewall \u2014 Production Overview\" shows whether the server answers, how long <code>/health</code> takes, the database connections and size, and how many computers are in a degraded protection state.</li> <li>Alert rules ship with the server, for example a disk, memory or database problem, a stopped service, many computers that stopped reporting, and a computer whose protection has been degraded for a long time. Alerts are sent by e-mail to the address in <code>AIFW_ALERT_EMAIL_TO</code> (by default the administrator address given at setup), through the mail settings of the server. The alert for many computers that stopped reporting is sent only in working hours.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"admin/server/operate.html#open-grafana","title":"Open Grafana","text":"<p>From version 2.2.8, Grafana is reached through an SSH tunnel, in the same way as the Keycloak console. It is published on the server's own loopback address only, never on the network.</p> <ol> <li> <p>On your own computer, open the tunnel and keep it running:</p> <pre><code>ssh -N -L 3000:127.0.0.1:3000 &lt;user&gt;@&lt;server&gt;\n</code></pre> </li> <li> <p>Open <code>http://127.0.0.1:3000</code> in your browser. Use exactly that address, with <code>127.0.0.1</code>, not <code>localhost</code>.</p> </li> <li>Sign in with your personal account through Keycloak. Only <code>super-admin</code> accounts are accepted. Every other role is refused, because the dashboard shows the computers of every workspace.</li> <li>In an emergency, you can sign in with the local account <code>admin</code>. Its password is in the settings file as <code>GRAFANA_ADMIN_PASSWORD</code>, and in <code>credentials.txt</code> if you kept it.</li> </ol> <p><code>aifw upgrade</code> sets this up on a server that was installed earlier. If its sign-in step fails, the upgrade still finishes. It then prints a warning with the cause and the <code>aifw setup</code> command that completes it. In versions before 2.2.8, Grafana has no port on the server, so this tunnel does not work.</p>"},{"location":"admin/server/prerequisites.html","title":"Server requirements","text":"<p>Have everything on this page ready before you start.</p>"},{"location":"admin/server/prerequisites.html#what-you-receive-from-sciencesoft","title":"What you receive from ScienceSoft","text":"<p>You get a welcome email. It contains:</p> <ul> <li>the registry access details: a name and a token. The installer needs them to download the software;</li> <li>the license file for your organisation (a file that ends in <code>.license</code>);</li> <li>the installer script <code>install.sh</code>.</li> </ul> <p>The software is not available for anonymous download. You need the registry access details to install it.</p>"},{"location":"admin/server/prerequisites.html#the-server","title":"The server","text":"Minimum Recommended Operating system Ubuntu 24.04 LTS, Intel/AMD (x86_64) or ARM (aarch64) Ubuntu 24.04 LTS CPU 2 vCPU 4 vCPU Memory 4 GB for the product itself, plus what the operating system needs 8 GB in total Free disk 20 GB 100 GB (the database, logs and metrics share it), and more as audit history grows. You can keep metrics and logs on a separate disk with <code>aifw monitoring data-dir</code> Software <code>systemd</code> <ul> <li>The installer is tested on Ubuntu 24.04. On a newer release it continues, but shows a warning.</li> <li>The installer adds Docker Engine and the Docker Compose plugin if they are missing.</li> <li>The server needs a static IP address or a DHCP reservation.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"admin/server/prerequisites.html#the-network","title":"The network","text":"<p>The server needs internet access during the installation. It downloads software from the vendor's private container registry and, depending on the version, from public container registries. Once a day the running server also downloads public vulnerability advisory data (OSV) for the Developer Tool Inventory. Set <code>ADVISORY_REFRESH=off</code> on the server to stop it, and the server then uses only the files already in its data folder.</p>"},{"location":"admin/server/prerequisites.html#ports","title":"Ports","text":"<p>The ports you need depend on how HTTPS is handled (see Install the server):</p> HTTPS handling Open inbound Notes Automatic certificates (Let's Encrypt) 22, 80, 443 Port 80 is used to issue the certificate. The server's DNS name must already point at this server. Your own certificate 22, 443 Setup also opens port 80. This mode does not need it. Close it yourself if you want. Your own load balancer or proxy 22, and 8090 only from the proxy's address You configure the proxy yourself. See Reverse proxy. Give the proxy's address to setup. If you leave it empty, port 8090 is open to every address. <p>The ports must be free before setup. Setup turns on the server's firewall (<code>ufw</code>) with these rules.</p> <p>Setup always allows SSH first, on the port that <code>sshd</code> uses. If you give setup one address for SSH, only that address can connect. The port of the sign-in console (8080) is never opened to the network. It is reachable only on the server itself, through an SSH tunnel.</p>"},{"location":"admin/server/prerequisites.html#a-name-for-the-server","title":"A name for the server","text":"<p>Choose the address users and devices will use, for example <code>https://ai-firewall.example.com</code>. It must be a full <code>https://</code> address, with no path and no trailing slash. A DNS record for it must exist.</p>"},{"location":"admin/server/prerequisites.html#before-you-start","title":"Before you start","text":"<ol> <li>Take a snapshot or backup of the server. If you need to change an installation parameter later, restore the snapshot and run setup again from a clean state.</li> <li>Have the license file and the registry access details at hand.</li> <li>If you will use your own certificate, have the certificate and its key on the server, readable by the user who runs the installation.</li> <li>If you will use your own load balancer, set it up first. See Reverse proxy.</li> <li>Decide the email address of the first administrator.</li> </ol>"},{"location":"admin/server/prerequisites.html#tools-on-your-own-computer","title":"Tools on your own computer","text":"<p>You need an SSH client to connect to the server, and a password manager to keep the sign-in details that setup shows once.</p>"},{"location":"admin/server/reverse-proxy.html","title":"Reverse proxy","text":"<p>Use this page when your own load balancer or reverse proxy ends HTTPS in front of the server (HTTPS option 3 in Install the server). Set the proxy up before you run setup, and before the first device connects.</p> <p>You can use nginx, IIS, HAProxy, or a load balancer from your cloud provider.</p>"},{"location":"admin/server/reverse-proxy.html#what-the-proxy-must-do","title":"What the proxy must do","text":"Requirement Value Public address HTTPS on port 443 for your server's name, with your certificate. HTTPS ends at the proxy Where it sends traffic Everything (<code>/</code>) to <code>http://&lt;server-ip&gt;:8090</code> Server firewall Port 8090 open only to the proxy's address Headers Keep the original <code>Host</code>. Send <code>X-Forwarded-Proto: https</code> and <code>X-Forwarded-Host: &lt;your-name&gt;</code> WebSocket Pass the <code>Upgrade</code> and <code>Connection</code> headers through Timeouts An idle (read and send) timeout of 600 seconds or more Buffering Streamed responses (<code>text/event-stream</code>) must reach the client as they are written, not when the response ends Request size Allow request bodies of at least 8 MB. Computers upload audit data in bodies of up to 8 MB. nginx refuses bodies over 1 MB by default <p>You do not need to do anything for the sign-in service console. The server refuses it on the public address. Administrators reach it through an SSH tunnel (see Sign-in and roles).</p>"},{"location":"admin/server/reverse-proxy.html#why-these-values","title":"Why these values","text":"<p>The server keeps long connections open:</p> <ul> <li>Every managed computer keeps a stream open, so that a policy change reaches it at once. The server sends a keep-alive signal every 25 seconds.</li> <li>The admin portal keeps streams open for audit records, with a keep-alive every 30 seconds.</li> <li>One stream (the violations stream, for the top administrator role only) has no keep-alive yet.</li> </ul> <p>Because of the keep-alive signals, a timeout of 60 seconds is enough for most streams. Setup generates 600 seconds, which also covers the stream without a keep-alive. A proxy that buffers responses holds events back, so computers get policy changes late. The server tells the proxy not to buffer its streams (<code>X-Accel-Buffering: no</code>).</p>"},{"location":"admin/server/reverse-proxy.html#nginx","title":"nginx","text":"<p>Setup writes this file for you when you choose your own proxy: <code>&lt;install folder&gt;/proxy-snippets/nginx-server-block.conf</code>. nginx waits only 60 seconds by default, so the two timeout lines are required. The file that setup writes has no <code>client_max_body_size</code> line. Add it, as shown.</p> <pre><code>server {\n    listen 443 ssl;\n    server_name ai-firewall.example.com;\n    ssl_certificate     /path/to/fullchain.pem;\n    ssl_certificate_key /path/to/privkey.pem;\n    client_max_body_size 8m;\n\n    location / {\n        proxy_pass http://&lt;server-ip&gt;:8090;\n        proxy_set_header Host $host;\n        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto https;\n        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Host ai-firewall.example.com;\n        proxy_http_version 1.1;\n        proxy_set_header Upgrade $http_upgrade;\n        proxy_set_header Connection \"upgrade\";\n        proxy_read_timeout 600s;\n        proxy_send_timeout 600s;\n    }\n}\n</code></pre> <p>nginx obeys the \"do not buffer\" header from the server, so you do not need another setting.</p>"},{"location":"admin/server/reverse-proxy.html#iis-with-url-rewrite-and-arr","title":"IIS with URL Rewrite and ARR","text":"<p>Setup writes <code>&lt;install folder&gt;/proxy-snippets/iis-web.config</code>.</p> <ol> <li>Install the URL Rewrite and Application Request Routing (ARR) modules.</li> <li> <p>Turn on the ARR proxy and set its timeout to 600 seconds or more. In IIS Manager, open the server node, then Application Request Routing Cache, then Server Proxy Settings. Or run:</p> <pre><code>%windir%\\system32\\inetsrv\\appcmd.exe set config -section:system.webServer/proxy /enabled:\"True\" /timeout:\"00:10:00\" /commit:apphost\n</code></pre> <p>The server tells proxies not to buffer its event streams, but IIS ARR does not read that header. If computers receive policy changes late, set Response buffer threshold in the same place to 0, then check again. ScienceSoft has not verified this setting on a customer installation.</p> </li> <li> <p>In the site's URL Rewrite settings, add <code>HTTP_X_FORWARDED_PROTO</code> and <code>HTTP_X_FORWARDED_HOST</code> to the Allowed Server Variables. Without them the rule below fails.</p> </li> <li> <p>Bind the site to your name on HTTPS port 443 with your certificate, and add this rule:</p> <pre><code>&lt;configuration&gt;\n  &lt;system.webServer&gt;\n    &lt;rewrite&gt;\n      &lt;rules&gt;\n        &lt;rule name=\"proxy-to-ai-firewall\" stopProcessing=\"true\"&gt;\n          &lt;match url=\"(.*)\" /&gt;\n          &lt;action type=\"Rewrite\" url=\"http://&lt;server-ip&gt;:8090/{R:1}\" /&gt;\n          &lt;serverVariables&gt;\n            &lt;set name=\"HTTP_X_FORWARDED_PROTO\" value=\"https\" /&gt;\n            &lt;set name=\"HTTP_X_FORWARDED_HOST\" value=\"ai-firewall.example.com\" /&gt;\n          &lt;/serverVariables&gt;\n        &lt;/rule&gt;\n      &lt;/rules&gt;\n    &lt;/rewrite&gt;\n  &lt;/system.webServer&gt;\n&lt;/configuration&gt;\n</code></pre> </li> </ol>"},{"location":"admin/server/reverse-proxy.html#haproxy","title":"HAProxy","text":"<p>Setup does not write a file for HAProxy. This is the equivalent of the nginx block. HAProxy does not buffer responses and passes WebSocket upgrades by itself. After the upgrade, <code>timeout tunnel</code> applies instead of <code>timeout server</code>.</p> <pre><code>frontend aifw_https\n    mode http\n    bind :443 ssl crt /etc/haproxy/certs/ai-firewall.pem\n    timeout client 600s\n    http-request set-header X-Forwarded-Proto https\n    http-request set-header X-Forwarded-Host ai-firewall.example.com\n    default_backend aifw_control_plane\n\nbackend aifw_control_plane\n    mode http\n    timeout server 600s\n    timeout tunnel 600s\n    option httpchk GET /health\n    server cp1 &lt;server-ip&gt;:8090 check\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"admin/server/reverse-proxy.html#check-after-you-configure-the-proxy","title":"Check after you configure the proxy","text":"<pre><code>curl -fsS https://ai-firewall.example.com/health\n</code></pre> <p>The answer must be <code>{\"status\":\"ok\"}</code>. Use your own name instead of <code>ai-firewall.example.com</code>, and your own server address instead of <code>&lt;server-ip&gt;</code>, everywhere on this page.</p>"},{"location":"admin/server/sign-in.html","title":"Sign-in and roles","text":"<p>This page explains who can sign in to ScienceSoft AI Endpoint Firewall, what each role can do, and how to add people.</p>"},{"location":"admin/server/sign-in.html#how-people-and-devices-are-identified","title":"How people and devices are identified","text":"<p>ScienceSoft AI Endpoint Firewall uses Keycloak as its sign-in service. Keycloak runs as one of the containers of the server. It handles sign-in to the admin portal, roles and the sign-in log. It has two realms (separate sets of accounts):</p> Realm What it is for <code>ai-firewall</code> Sign-in to the admin portal. The role of a person decides what they can do. Their workspace group decides which workspace they see. <code>master</code> Sign-in to the Keycloak console, for the administrators who manage people and roles. What How it is identified People who use the admin portal Keycloak, realm <code>ai-firewall</code> Keycloak administrators Keycloak, realm <code>master</code>. One personal account for each administrator Computers (agents) Not through Keycloak. At first start the agent exchanges the device enrolment key for its own device key. Static tokens (the emergency sign-in token and the administration tokens) Not through Keycloak. They always have the highest rights and are not recorded in the sign-in log. Keep them for emergencies only."},{"location":"admin/server/sign-in.html#first-sign-in","title":"First sign-in","text":"<ol> <li> <p>Open <code>https://&lt;your-address&gt;/admin</code>.</p> <p></p> <p>The button View live demo opens a sample portal with invented data. It does not connect to your server.</p> </li> <li> <p>Select the single sign-on button. Sign in with the administrator email that you gave to setup and the temporary password from <code>credentials.txt</code>.</p> </li> <li>You are asked to choose a new password, and to register an authenticator app for one-time codes.</li> </ol> <p>If you set <code>authMode</code> to <code>compat</code> (the recommended value), the sign-in page also has an emergency token field. Use it only in an emergency, and keep the token in your password manager. With <code>keycloak</code>, the page has the single sign-on button only.</p>"},{"location":"admin/server/sign-in.html#roles","title":"Roles","text":"<p>Every person who uses the admin portal has one base role. The server checks the role on every request, not only in the screens.</p> Role What it allows Who gets it <code>admin</code> Everything in their own workspace IT or security administrators who set up and run protection <code>analyst</code> Reading and investigating in their own workspace, without changes Analysts who review detections but must not change settings <code>auditor</code> Audit only: reading and export Auditors and compliance staff <code>super-admin</code> Added on top of a base role. All workspaces, creating workspaces, releases, platform audit and the sign-in log People who run the whole installation. Given only through the <code>fleet-operators</code> group <p>On a server that belongs to one organisation, the people who run the whole installation need <code>super-admin</code>. Only they can create workspaces, see all workspaces, and use the platform audit log, the releases view and the other all-workspace views. Setup gives <code>super-admin</code> to the first administrator. Give it to others only by adding them to the <code>fleet-operators</code> group, and only to people you trust with every workspace. Everyone else needs one base role and one workspace group. The emergency token always has this level.</p> <p>A role is not enough. A person also needs a workspace group. Without it the portal shows no workspace.</p>"},{"location":"admin/server/sign-in.html#the-default-role","title":"The default role","text":"<p>Give new portal users the <code>auditor</code> role. It has the fewest rights, so a person who gets it by mistake cannot change anything. Give <code>analyst</code> or <code>admin</code> only when the work needs it.</p> <ul> <li>Do not add the product roles to the default roles of the <code>ai-firewall</code> realm. If you do, every new person, including people who sign in through a company directory, gets access automatically.</li> <li>Never give <code>super-admin</code> by default, and never make <code>fleet-operators</code> a default group.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"admin/server/sign-in.html#two-accounts-for-an-administrator","title":"Two accounts for an administrator","text":"<p>The Keycloak console and the admin portal use different realms, so an administrator who needs both has two accounts with the same username and separate passwords. Changing the password of one does not change the other. An account from the <code>master</code> realm cannot sign in to the admin portal. A person who needs the portal needs an account in the <code>ai-firewall</code> realm.</p> <p>Use the built-in emergency administrator account only in an emergency, for example to create the first additional administrator. For regular work, create a personal administrator account for each person. Do not share one account.</p>"},{"location":"admin/server/sign-in.html#open-the-keycloak-console","title":"Open the Keycloak console","text":"<p>The console is never on the public address. Those addresses always answer \"not found\". You open it from your own computer through an SSH tunnel.</p> <ol> <li> <p>In <code>credentials.txt</code>, find the console account and the tunnel command. The command looks like this:</p> <pre><code>ssh -N -L 18080:127.0.0.1:8080 &lt;user&gt;@&lt;server&gt;\n</code></pre> <p>Keep it running.</p> </li> <li> <p>Open <code>http://127.0.0.1:18080/keycloak/admin/</code> in your browser. Use exactly that address, with <code>127.0.0.1</code>, not <code>localhost</code>.</p> </li> <li>Sign in with your <code>master</code> realm account. A one-time code from an authenticator app is required. The first time, you register the app. Every later sign-in asks for the code.</li> <li>Close the tunnel when you finish.</li> </ol> <p>The tunnel command is the same on every server installed with <code>aifw</code>: port 8080 is on the server, and 18080 is on your own computer. If the console does not load after an upgrade from an older version, run <code>sudo aifw setup</code> once.</p>"},{"location":"admin/server/sign-in.html#add-a-person-to-the-admin-portal","title":"Add a person to the admin portal","text":"<p>In the Keycloak console:</p> <ol> <li> <p>Switch the realm (top left) from <code>master</code> to <code>ai-firewall</code>. A person created in <code>master</code> cannot sign in to the portal.</p> <p></p> </li> <li> <p>Open Users, then Add user. Fill in the username, email and name. Turn on Email verified only if you have confirmed the address. Select Create.</p> <p> </p> </li> <li> <p>On the Credentials tab, select Set password. Enter a strong password and keep Temporary on, so the person must change it at the first sign-in.</p> <p></p> </li> <li> <p>On the Role mapping tab, select Assign role, filter by realm roles, and choose one base role (<code>admin</code>, <code>analyst</code> or <code>auditor</code>).</p> <p> </p> </li> <li> <p>On the Groups tab, select Join group and choose the workspace group.</p> <p></p> <p>The realm adds every new person to its default workspace group, <code>default-tenant</code>, as the screenshot shows. Check that this is the group of your workspace. If it is not, leave it and join the right group. Never join <code>fleet-operators</code> for a person who should see one workspace only (see below).</p> </li> </ol> <p>Deliver the password in a safe way:</p> <ul> <li>Never send it in plain text by email, chat or in a ticket.</li> <li>Send the username and the password through different channels, or use a password-sharing tool with a one-time link.</li> <li>Ask the person to change the temporary password and to confirm when it is done.</li> </ul> <p><code>super-admin</code> is not given directly. Add the person to the <code>fleet-operators</code> group. To check, open Role mapping and turn off Hide inherited roles. A person with all-workspace access must show the base role and the inherited <code>super-admin</code> role. A person with one workspace must not show <code>super-admin</code>.</p>"},{"location":"admin/server/sign-in.html#the-same-from-the-command-line","title":"The same from the command line","text":"<p>On the server, <code>kcadm.sh</code> runs inside the Keycloak container, so no tunnel is needed:</p> <pre><code>KC=\"sudo docker exec -it ai-firewall-keycloak /opt/keycloak/bin/kcadm.sh\"\n$KC config credentials --server http://localhost:8080/keycloak --realm master --user &lt;your-admin-username&gt;\n$KC create users -r ai-firewall -s username=&lt;email&gt; -s email=&lt;email&gt; -s enabled=true\n$KC set-password -r ai-firewall --username &lt;email&gt; --new-password '&lt;temporary-password&gt;' --temporary\n$KC add-roles -r ai-firewall --uusername &lt;email&gt; --rolename admin\n</code></pre> <p>Then add the person to the workspace group in the console.</p>"},{"location":"admin/server/sign-in.html#add-a-keycloak-administrator","title":"Add a Keycloak administrator","text":"<ol> <li>In the console, make sure the realm is <code>master</code>.</li> <li>Open Users, then Add user, and set a temporary password as above.</li> <li> <p>Give the access that fits:</p> Scope Who needs it What to give The <code>ai-firewall</code> realm only An administrator who manages portal people, roles and groups Join the group <code>aifw-realm-operators</code> All realms, including <code>master</code> An administrator who replaces the emergency account for regular work The realm role <code>admin</code> of the <code>master</code> realm </li> </ol> <p>For everyday work with portal people, the first scope is enough. Never create administrator accounts for the people who only use the portal.</p>"},{"location":"admin/server/sign-in.html#connect-your-companys-directory","title":"Connect your company's directory","text":"<p>You can let people sign in with their company account, for example Microsoft Entra ID. See Single sign-on with your company directory.</p>"},{"location":"admin/server/sign-in.html#required-realm-settings","title":"Required realm settings","text":"<p>Setup imports the <code>ai-firewall</code> realm when Keycloak starts for the first time. The realm has no people in it. These settings are required for the product to work. Do not change them.</p> Area Setting Required Roles <code>admin</code>, <code>analyst</code>, <code>auditor</code>, <code>super-admin</code> One base role for each person. <code>super-admin</code> is extra. A fifth role, <code>trial</code>, is used only on trial servers Groups <code>fleet-operators</code> Gives <code>super-admin</code>. Never a default group Groups Workspace groups Each has a <code>tenant</code> attribute. Every person must be in one Client <code>admin-portal-spa</code> A public client with PKCE <code>S256</code>. Direct access grants off. No client secret Client <code>admin-api</code> Bearer-only. No client secret Client <code>admin-cli</code> Do not change Login User registration Off <p>A running server does not read the realm file again. The realm also sets a minimum password length of 12, a lockout after 5 failed sign-ins and a one-time-code policy for the administrator. <code>aifw setup</code> applies the same rules to servers that were set up earlier. Do not weaken them.</p>"},{"location":"admin/server/troubleshooting.html","title":"Troubleshooting","text":""},{"location":"admin/server/troubleshooting.html#start-here","title":"Start here","text":"<pre><code>sudo aifw status\nsudo aifw doctor\ndocker ps\n</code></pre> <p><code>aifw status</code> says if the installation is healthy. <code>docker ps</code> shows each container. A container that is restarting or <code>unhealthy</code> is where to look first:</p> <pre><code>docker logs &lt;container-name&gt; --tail 200\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"admin/server/troubleshooting.html#installsh-does-nothing-or-fails-with-a-strange-error","title":"<code>install.sh</code> does nothing, or fails with a strange error","text":"<p>The script may have Windows line endings, for example if you copied it from a Windows computer. Convert it and run it again:</p> <pre><code>sed -i 's/\\r$//' install.sh\nbash install.sh\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"admin/server/troubleshooting.html#the-installer-does-not-accept-the-registry-access-details","title":"The installer does not accept the registry access details","text":"<ul> <li>Check that you copied the name and the token completely, without spaces.</li> <li>The server needs internet access to the vendor's container registry.</li> <li>If it still fails, ask your ScienceSoft representative to check that the access is active.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"admin/server/troubleshooting.html#aifw-preflight-fails","title":"<code>aifw preflight</code> fails","text":"<p>It says which requirement is not met. Typical causes:</p> Message about What to do Memory, CPU or disk Add resources. See Server requirements. A port is in use Stop what uses it, or choose another HTTPS option. The registry cannot be reached Check the proxy and firewall rules for outgoing traffic. <code>systemd</code> The server must use <code>systemd</code>."},{"location":"admin/server/troubleshooting.html#setup-stops-at-start-secure-web-access","title":"Setup stops at \"Start secure web access\"","text":"<p>You see this message: \"Start secure web access \u2014 failed\". It is usually one of three things:</p> <ol> <li>The cloud firewall (for example a network security group) does not let ports 80 and 443 in.</li> <li>The DNS name does not point at this server yet.</li> <li>The certificate is still being issued. Wait a few minutes.</li> </ol> <p>If you use your own load balancer, it must be fully ready before you run setup. For details, run:</p> <pre><code>docker logs ai-firewall-caddy\n</code></pre> <p>Fix the cause. If nothing about the answers has to change, run <code>sudo aifw setup</code> again.</p>"},{"location":"admin/server/troubleshooting.html#the-server-answers-but-the-sign-in-page-redirects-to-the-wrong-address-or-shows-an-error","title":"The server answers, but the sign-in page redirects to the wrong address or shows an error","text":"<p>The single sign-on service may have been set up for a different public address than the one your server uses.</p> <p>There is no command that changes the public address of a server that is already set up. Setup refuses a different address.</p> <ul> <li>If you have not used the server yet, restore the snapshot that you took before the installation, and run <code>aifw setup</code> again with the right address.</li> <li>If the server is in use (computers are enrolled and people have signed in), do not change the address by hand. Send your ScienceSoft representative the output of <code>sudo aifw status</code> and <code>sudo aifw doctor</code>, and the public address you gave to setup.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"admin/server/troubleshooting.html#computers-get-policy-changes-late","title":"Computers get policy changes late","text":"<p>A proxy that buffers responses, or an idle timeout that is too short, delays the streams. See Reverse proxy.</p>"},{"location":"admin/server/troubleshooting.html#i-lost-credentialstxt","title":"I lost <code>credentials.txt</code>","text":"<p>The file is meant to be deleted after you save its contents. Running <code>aifw setup</code> again writes a new <code>credentials.txt</code> from the current settings. Delete it again after you copy what you need.</p> <ul> <li> <p>The enrolment key and the emergency token are kept in the settings file <code>/opt/ai-firewall/docker/.env</code>, which only the administrator can read. To read the enrolment key:</p> <pre><code>sudo grep '^AIFW_ENROL_TOKEN=' /opt/ai-firewall/docker/.env\n</code></pre> </li> <li> <p>A forgotten portal password. Another administrator can set a new temporary password in the Keycloak console, or with <code>kcadm.sh</code>. See Sign-in and roles.</p> </li> <li>The temporary password from the sheet works only for the first sign-in. After the administrator has changed it, the sheet shows an old value.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"admin/server/troubleshooting.html#getting-help","title":"Getting help","text":"<p>Send your ScienceSoft representative:</p> <ul> <li>the output of <code>sudo aifw status</code>;</li> <li>the output of <code>sudo aifw doctor</code>;</li> <li>the last 200 lines of the log of the container that fails.</li> </ul> <p>Do not send the settings file, <code>credentials.txt</code>, or any token.</p>"}]}